/* Built by tools/build-split.py from data.js — do not edit. e2b, SDLs 19, 20, 21, 22, 23, 24 */
(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e2b.1b4dfb916f.js"]={"19":[{"id":"sdl19_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the generation of the corticopapillary osmotic gradient, deconstructing how active countercurrent multiplication and the \"single effect\" (apical NKCC2 solute transport across the water-impermeable thick ascending limb) establish medullary hypertonicity","batch":1,"stem":"What is the \"single effect\" in countercurrent multiplication?","choices":{"A":"Water reabsorption by the water-permeable descending limb concentrates the tubular fluid there","B":"NaCl reabsorption by the water-impermeable ascending limb dilutes its own tubular fluid","C":"Urea reabsorption by the inner medullary collecting duct concentrates the interstitium there","D":"Active Na+ secretion by the thin ascending limb dilutes the surrounding interstitial fluid","E":"Passive NaCl secretion by the descending limb concentrates the tubular fluid there"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The single effect refers specifically to NaCl reabsorption via the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter in the thick ascending limb; because this segment is impermeable to water, the reabsorbed NaCl leaves without water, diluting the ascending limb fluid while raising the osmolarity of the surrounding interstitium and the adjacent descending limb.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the single effect is a repeatable step, not the finished gradient — pair it with the flow of tubular fluid to understand how a small osmotic difference gets multiplied into the full corticopapillary gradient.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 1 — the single effect and fluid flow that multiply a small osmotic difference into the full corticopapillary gradient","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the generation of the corticopapillary osmotic gradient, deconstructing how active countercurrent multiplication and the \"single effect\" (apical NKCC2 solute transport across the water-impermeable thick ascending limb) establish medullary hypertonicity","batch":1,"stem":"Why does water flow out of the descending limb during the single effect, even though the descending limb has no active transport?","choices":{"A":"The descending limb actively pumps water via an ATP-dependent water pump","B":"The descending limb is impermeable to water, so water is trapped and concentrated by evaporation","C":"The descending limb actively cotransports water with the NaCl reabsorbed from the ascending limb","D":"The descending limb responds to a direct hormonal signal from the ascending limb's NaCl transport","E":"The descending limb is water-permeable and equilibrates with the nearby interstitial gradient"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The thin descending limb is passively permeable to water; as the ascending limb's single effect raises interstitial osmolarity, water simply diffuses out of the descending limb down this osmotic gradient until the descending limb fluid equilibrates with the surrounding interstitium, with no active transport or hormonal signal required.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: contrast this passive descending limb water permeability with the ascending limb's water impermeability, since mixing these two up is a common source of countercurrent multiplication errors.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 1 — the single effect and fluid flow that multiply a small osmotic difference into the full corticopapillary gradient","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the generation of the corticopapillary osmotic gradient, deconstructing how active countercurrent multiplication and the \"single effect\" (apical NKCC2 solute transport across the water-impermeable thick ascending limb) establish medullary hypertonicity","batch":1,"stem":"What continuously renews the corticopapillary gradient beyond a single round of the \"single effect\"?","choices":{"A":"Ongoing secretion of ADH, which increases interstitial NaCl concentration","B":"Ongoing reabsorption of urea, which dominates the process at each step","C":"Ongoing flow of new fluid, pushing concentrated fluid toward the loop's bend","D":"Ongoing filtration at the glomerulus, which raises medullary osmolarity","E":"Ongoing vasa recta blood flow, which actively deposits NaCl each cycle"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Countercurrent multiplication is a two-step repeating process: the single effect deposits NaCl into the interstitium, and then the ongoing flow of new tubular fluid through the loop pushes the newly concentrated descending limb fluid toward the bend, resetting the ascending limb for another round of the single effect; repeating these two steps is what multiplies a small osmotic difference into the full gradient.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this is why countercurrent multiplication requires continuous glomerular filtration and tubular flow, not just the transporter activity of the thick ascending limb alone.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 1 — the single effect and fluid flow that multiply a small osmotic difference into the full corticopapillary gradient","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the molecular process of medullary urea recycling, evaluating how ADH-stimulated facilitated urea transporters (UT1) in the inner medullary collecting ducts deposit urea into the interstitium to generate roughly half of the corticopapillary gradient","batch":1,"stem":"Why does urea become concentrated in the tubular fluid of the cortical and outer medullary collecting ducts?","choices":{"A":"ADH raises urea permeability there but not water permeability, so urea leaves while water remains","B":"ADH lowers water permeability there, concentrating both water and urea together","C":"ADH has no effect on this segment, and urea concentration simply reflects filtration rate","D":"ADH raises water permeability there but not urea permeability, so water leaves while urea remains","E":"ADH stimulates a dedicated urea pump that actively concentrates urea in this segment"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In the cortical and outer medullary collecting ducts, ADH increases water permeability without increasing urea permeability, so water is reabsorbed while urea is left behind; this differential permeability effect concentrates urea in the tubular fluid, setting up the gradient that the inner medullary collecting duct will later use.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this differential water-versus-urea permeability is the setup step; the payoff comes once fluid reaches the inner medullary collecting duct, where urea is reabsorbed.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 2 — how ADH-driven UT1 transporters recycle urea into the medulla to reinforce the gradient built by NaCl","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the molecular process of medullary urea recycling, evaluating how ADH-stimulated facilitated urea transporters (UT1) in the inner medullary collecting ducts deposit urea into the interstitium to generate roughly half of the corticopapillary gradient","batch":1,"stem":"During water deprivation with high ADH levels, the amount of urea arriving at the hairpin turn of the loop exceeds the filtered load by about 10%. UT1 transporters in the inner medullary collecting ducts then return about 70% of the filtered load to the interstitium. Roughly what share of the filtered urea appears in the final urine?","choices":{"A":"About 40%","B":"About 110%","C":"About 70%","D":"About 50%","E":"About 10%"},"correct":"A","explanation":"With ADH present, UT1 in the inner medullary collecting duct reabsorbs about 70% of the filtered urea, and because about 110% of the filtered load reaches the bend of the loop (net addition to the lumen in the descending limb) while the thick ascending limb, distal tubule, and cortical and outer medullary collecting ducts are impermeable to urea, about 40% is left to be excreted. 110% is the amount at the bend, before UT1 reabsorption. 70% is the amount reabsorbed by UT1, not the amount excreted. 50% is what remains at the end of the proximal tubule, before urea enters the descending limb. 10% would require far more inner medullary reabsorption than UT1 provides.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Urea bookkeeping with ADH: 50% left after the proximal tubule → 110% at the bend (thin descending limb secretion) → UT1 reabsorbs about 70% → about 40% excreted; the reabsorbed urea adds to the corticopapillary gradient. COMLEX: Low ADH removes UT1 upregulation, so urea recycling and maximal concentrating ability both fall.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 2 — passive urea handling along the nephron sets up this recycling","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the molecular process of medullary urea recycling, evaluating how ADH-stimulated facilitated urea transporters (UT1) in the inner medullary collecting ducts deposit urea into the interstitium to generate roughly half of the corticopapillary gradient","batch":1,"stem":"Why is urea recycling described as ADH-dependent?","choices":{"A":"Urea transport requires ADH to activate a separate hormonal cascade","B":"ADH is required to synthesize urea within the inner medullary collecting duct cells","C":"The differential permeability effects that concentrate and move urea require ADH","D":"Urea recycling occurs independent of ADH but is measured using ADH as a marker","E":"ADH is required to filter urea at the glomerulus before recycling can begin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Every step of urea recycling, from concentrating urea in the cortical and outer medullary collecting ducts to transporting it via UT1 in the inner medullary collecting duct, depends on ADH-driven changes in membrane permeability; without ADH, these differential permeability effects do not occur and urea is excreted rather than recycled.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this is the second mechanism by which ADH augments the corticopapillary gradient, alongside its enhancement of the single effect in the thick ascending limb.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 2 — how ADH-driven UT1 transporters recycle urea into the medulla to reinforce the gradient built by NaCl","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate the biophysics of countercurrent exchange in the vasa recta, analyzing how slow capillary blood flow and highly permeable, passive hairpin loops prevent medullary solute washout to preserve the corticopapillary osmotic gradient","batch":1,"stem":"Why is countercurrent exchange in the vasa recta described as passive, unlike countercurrent multiplication?","choices":{"A":"It relies on ADH-stimulated active transport identical to the collecting duct mechanism","B":"It relies on diffusion of solutes and water down their existing gradients","C":"It relies on Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransport identical to the thick ascending limb mechanism","D":"It relies on active urea transport through the UT1 transporter in the vasa recta wall","E":"It relies on active NaCl secretion by specialized cells lining the vasa recta"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The vasa recta behave like ordinary capillaries: they are freely and passively permeable to small solutes and water, with no active transporter of their own, so exchange between vasa recta blood and the interstitium occurs entirely by diffusion down existing concentration gradients, in contrast to the active NaCl transport that drives countercurrent multiplication in the loop of Henle.","boardPrep":"Step 1: countercurrent multiplication builds the gradient actively; countercurrent exchange in the vasa recta only helps preserve it passively — a frequently tested distinction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 3 — why slow, permeable vasa recta blood flow preserves the gradient instead of washing it out","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate the biophysics of countercurrent exchange in the vasa recta, analyzing how slow capillary blood flow and highly permeable, passive hairpin loops prevent medullary solute washout to preserve the corticopapillary osmotic gradient","batch":1,"stem":"Why does slow blood flow through the vasa recta help preserve the corticopapillary gradient?","choices":{"A":"It gives solutes more time to diffuse back out before the blood exits","B":"It lowers the osmolarity of the blood entering the descending limb","C":"It prevents any solute from entering the descending limb of the vasa recta","D":"It increases the hydrostatic pressure gradient driving glomerular filtration","E":"It stimulates additional ADH release from the posterior pituitary gland"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Slow blood flow through the hairpin-shaped vasa recta gives solutes and water more time to equilibrate at every level of the medulla; on the way up the ascending limb, this extra time allows most of the solute picked up on the way down to diffuse back out into the interstitium before the blood returns to the systemic circulation, limiting net solute loss. Vasa recta flow does not set glomerular filtration pressure, which is determined at the glomerular capillaries upstream, and it does not stimulate ADH, whose release is driven by plasma osmolarity sensed in the hypothalamus.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: vasodilators that markedly increase medullary blood flow shorten this equilibration time and can wash out the gradient, the clinical counterpart to this normal physiology.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 3 — why slow, permeable vasa recta blood flow preserves the gradient instead of washing it out","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate the biophysics of countercurrent exchange in the vasa recta, analyzing how slow capillary blood flow and highly permeable, passive hairpin loops prevent medullary solute washout to preserve the corticopapillary osmotic gradient","batch":1,"stem":"What happens to blood osmolarity as it travels from the descending to the ascending limb of the vasa recta?","choices":{"A":"It rises to match the papilla and stays elevated after exiting the medulla","B":"It falls throughout the entire hairpin loop, from entry to exit","C":"It stays constant throughout, since the vasa recta are impermeable to solutes","D":"It rises to match the papilla, then falls back near its entering value","E":"It rises briefly, then falls and exits below its original entering value"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Blood entering the descending limb of the vasa recta equilibrates with the progressively higher interstitial osmolarity, reaching about 1200 mOsm/L at the papilla; as it then travels up the ascending limb through progressively lower osmolarity, solutes diffuse back out and water diffuses back in, so the exiting blood osmolarity falls close to, though slightly above, its original entering value.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the small residual rise in exiting osmolarity (about 325 mOsm/L versus 300 mOsm/L entering) represents the modest net solute loss that countercurrent multiplication and urea recycling continuously replace.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 3 — why slow, permeable vasa recta blood flow preserves the gradient instead of washing it out","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the generation of the corticopapillary osmotic gradient, deconstructing how active countercurrent multiplication and the \"single effect\" (apical NKCC2 solute transport across the water-impermeable thick ascending limb) establish medullary hypertonicity","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man is deprived of water for 24 hours. His plasma ADH rises markedly. Which change in his corticopapillary osmotic gradient would be expected as a direct result of this ADH rise?","choices":{"A":"An augmented gradient, since ADH increases Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter activity","B":"A diminished gradient, since ADH suppresses Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter activity to conserve energy","C":"No change in the gradient, since ADH primarily raises water permeability","D":"An augmented gradient, attributed to a rise in glomerular filtration rate","E":"A diminished gradient, since dehydration reduces medullary blood flow below a critical threshold"},"correct":"A","explanation":"ADH augments the corticopapillary gradient through two mechanisms: it enhances the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter and therefore the single effect in the thick ascending limb, and it separately promotes urea recycling; in water deprivation, rising ADH strengthens both mechanisms, producing a larger gradient than when ADH is low.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: dehydration and SIADH both raise ADH and augment the gradient, while water drinking and central diabetes insipidus both lower ADH and diminish it.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — ADH enhances the single effect by increasing NKCC2 activity in the thick ascending limb","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the generation of the corticopapillary osmotic gradient, deconstructing how active countercurrent multiplication and the \"single effect\" (apical NKCC2 solute transport across the water-impermeable thick ascending limb) establish medullary hypertonicity","batch":2,"stem":"A physiology instructor models countercurrent multiplication in a loop of Henle that starts at 300 mOsm/L throughout, with the surrounding interstitium also at 300 mOsm/L. In the first single effect, NaCl transport out of the thick ascending limb raises the adjacent interstitial osmolarity to 400 mOsm/L. Before any new fluid flows in, what are the osmolarities of the ascending and descending limb fluids?","choices":{"A":"Ascending limb 400; descending limb 200 mOsm/L","B":"Ascending limb 300; descending limb 300 mOsm/L","C":"Ascending limb 200; descending limb 300 mOsm/L","D":"Ascending limb 200; descending limb 400 mOsm/L","E":"Ascending limb 400; descending limb 400 mOsm/L"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In the single effect, NaCl leaves the water-impermeable thick ascending limb without water, diluting its fluid to 200 mOsm/L while the interstitium rises to 400 mOsm/L; because the descending limb is water-permeable, water leaves it until its fluid also reaches 400 mOsm/L. Reversing the two values would require the ascending limb to gain solute and the descending limb to lose it, the opposite of the transport and permeability properties. Leaving both limbs at 300 mOsm/L ignores both the dilution of the ascending limb and the equilibration of the descending limb. Diluting the ascending limb while leaving the descending limb at 300 mOsm/L ignores its water permeability. Raising the ascending limb to 400 mOsm/L would require it to reabsorb water, which it cannot do.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Each cycle has two steps: the single effect (ascending limb diluted, interstitium and descending limb concentrated) and flow of new 300 mOsm/L fluid that pushes concentrated fluid toward the bend; repeating them builds the gradient to about 1200 mOsm/L at the human papilla. COMLEX: ADH enhances the single effect by increasing NKCC2 activity, and loop diuretics abolish it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 1 — the thin limb permeabilities that allow the descending limb to equilibrate","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the generation of the corticopapillary osmotic gradient, deconstructing how active countercurrent multiplication and the \"single effect\" (apical NKCC2 solute transport across the water-impermeable thick ascending limb) establish medullary hypertonicity","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old man undergoes a water deprivation test for evaluation of nocturia. Blood pressure is 122/78 mm Hg. Serum sodium is 142 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L). After overnight deprivation, his urine osmolality reaches 1,180 mOsm/kg (reference range: greater than 800 mOsm/kg after deprivation), close to the roughly 1,200 mOsm/kg present at the bend of the human loop of Henle. His physician explains that desert rodents, whose loops of Henle are much longer, produce a much higher medullary osmolarity than humans. Which explanation best accounts for this difference?","choices":{"A":"A longer loop contains a proportionally larger thick ascending limb Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter density","B":"A longer loop allows more repeating cycles of the single effect to multiply the gradient","C":"A longer loop increases glomerular filtration rate, directly raising interstitial osmolarity","D":"A longer loop increases vasa recta blood flow, delivering more solute to the papilla directly","E":"A longer loop removes the need for urea recycling in these species"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Countercurrent multiplication works by repeating the single effect and fluid-flow steps many times along the length of the loop of Henle; a longer loop simply allows more repetitions of this two-step cycle before fluid reaches the bend, multiplying a small osmotic difference into a much larger gradient.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this loop-length-to-gradient relationship is the physiologic basis for why species built for extreme water conservation have disproportionately long loops of Henle.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — each repeat of the single effect and fluid flow multiplies the gradient, so loop length matters","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the molecular process of medullary urea recycling, evaluating how ADH-stimulated facilitated urea transporters (UT1) in the inner medullary collecting ducts deposit urea into the interstitium to generate roughly half of the corticopapillary gradient","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man on a chronically low-protein diet is noted to have a reduced maximal urine concentrating ability despite normal ADH secretion. Which explanation best accounts for this finding?","choices":{"A":"Reduced protein intake inhibits the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter in the thick ascending limb","B":"Reduced protein intake decreases circulating ADH secretion despite normal osmotic stimulation","C":"Reduced protein intake increases vasa recta blood flow, washing out the medullary gradient","D":"Reduced protein intake lowers urea production, limiting the substrate for medullary urea recycling","E":"Reduced protein intake impairs water permeability in the inner medullary collecting duct"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Because urea recycling contributes roughly half of the corticopapillary gradient and depends on urea generated from protein metabolism, a chronically low-protein diet reduces the amount of urea available to be recycled through UT1, limiting the urea-dependent portion of the gradient even when ADH secretion and signaling remain entirely normal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: reduced urine concentrating ability on a low-protein diet is a classic example of a substrate-limited rather than a hormone- or transporter-limited process.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — urea recycling needs urea as substrate, alongside ADH-driven UT1 activity","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the molecular process of medullary urea recycling, evaluating how ADH-stimulated facilitated urea transporters (UT1) in the inner medullary collecting ducts deposit urea into the interstitium to generate roughly half of the corticopapillary gradient","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with SIADH has persistently elevated ADH and produces small volumes of highly concentrated urine despite hyponatremia. Which mechanism specifically involving urea contributes to his urine concentrating ability?","choices":{"A":"Persistently high ADH increases urea filtration at the glomerulus, raising tubular urea delivery","B":"Persistently high ADH keeps UT1 active in the inner medullary collecting duct, sustaining urea transport","C":"Persistently high ADH suppresses UT1 activity, redirecting urea toward the systemic circulation","D":"Persistently high ADH increases hepatic urea synthesis, raising the total urea available for excretion","E":"Persistently high ADH increases proximal urea reabsorption independent of the medullary gradient"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In SIADH, chronically elevated ADH continuously activates UT1 in the inner medullary collecting duct, allowing urea to keep diffusing into the interstitium and reinforcing the corticopapillary gradient built by countercurrent multiplication; this sustained urea recycling is part of why SIADH produces persistently concentrated urine rather than an occasional or self-limited effect.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: SIADH is essentially the pathologic exaggeration of the same ADH-driven urea recycling mechanism seen physiologically during water deprivation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — ADH keeps UT1 active in the inner medullary collecting duct, sustaining urea recycling","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the molecular process of medullary urea recycling, evaluating how ADH-stimulated facilitated urea transporters (UT1) in the inner medullary collecting ducts deposit urea into the interstitium to generate roughly half of the corticopapillary gradient","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old man in a hypercatabolic state after a severe burn has markedly increased urea production. Assuming ADH secretion is appropriately elevated, which effect on his corticopapillary gradient would be expected from this increased urea production alone?","choices":{"A":"A smaller contribution from urea recycling, since excess urea saturates the UT1 transporter capacity","B":"No change in urea recycling, since ADH is presumed to be the single determinant of the process","C":"A larger contribution from urea recycling, since more substrate is available for UT1-mediated transport","D":"A larger contribution from countercurrent multiplication, since urea directly stimulates Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransport","E":"A smaller contribution from countercurrent multiplication, since excess urea competes with NaCl for reabsorption"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Because urea recycling is substrate-dependent, an increase in urea production, as occurs in the hypercatabolic state following a severe burn, provides more urea available to be concentrated in the collecting duct and transported via UT1, increasing the urea-driven contribution to the corticopapillary gradient as long as ADH remains available to permit the differential permeability effects.","boardPrep":"Step 1: urea recycling depends on both urea supply and ADH-driven UT1 activity, so more urea with adequate ADH means a larger urea contribution to the gradient.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — urea recycling via UT1 contributes about half of the corticopapillary gradient","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate the biophysics of countercurrent exchange in the vasa recta, analyzing how slow capillary blood flow and highly permeable, passive hairpin loops prevent medullary solute washout to preserve the corticopapillary osmotic gradient","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man takes a vasodilator that markedly increases renal medullary blood flow. Which effect on his corticopapillary osmotic gradient would be expected from this increased vasa recta flow alone?","choices":{"A":"An augmented gradient, since faster flow delivers more NaCl directly to the thick ascending limb","B":"No change in the gradient, since vasa recta flow rate does not influence solute exchange efficiency","C":"A diminished gradient, caused by direct inhibition of the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter by increased flow","D":"An augmented gradient, since faster flow increases urea delivery to the inner medullary collecting duct","E":"A diminished gradient, since faster flow carries away more solute than exchange can return"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Countercurrent exchange in the vasa recta depends on blood moving slowly enough for solutes to diffuse back out of the ascending limb before the blood exits the medulla; when medullary blood flow increases substantially, as with certain vasodilators, there is less time for this equilibration, so more solute is carried away than can be returned, producing medullary washout and a diminished gradient.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: certain vasodilators can markedly increase renal medullary blood flow and wash out the gradient, which lowers maximal urine concentrating ability.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 3 — slow vasa recta flow gives solutes time to exchange, and faster flow shortens that time","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate the biophysics of countercurrent exchange in the vasa recta, analyzing how slow capillary blood flow and highly permeable, passive hairpin loops prevent medullary solute washout to preserve the corticopapillary osmotic gradient","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman undergoes a water deprivation test for evaluation of polyuria. Blood pressure is 114/72 mm Hg. Serum sodium is 141 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L). After overnight deprivation, her urine osmolality reaches 1,100 mOsm/kg (reference range: greater than 800 mOsm/kg after deprivation), and the diagnosis of diabetes insipidus is excluded. Her intact concentrating ability depends partly on the hairpin course of the vasa recta through the medulla. If her vasa recta instead ran as straight vessels through the medulla, what would be the expected consequence for her corticopapillary gradient?","choices":{"A":"The gradient would be unaffected, since vessel geometry does not influence passive solute exchange","B":"The gradient would be augmented, since straight vessels would reduce turbulence and improve solute retention","C":"The gradient would be washed out, since straight vessels cannot return solute picked up on the way in","D":"The gradient would be augmented, since straight vessels deliver blood to the papilla more efficiently","E":"The gradient would be reversed, with the cortex more hypertonic than the papilla"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The hairpin shape of the vasa recta is essential to countercurrent exchange because it lets the ascending limb recapture solute that the descending limb picked up from the interstitium on the way down; a straight vessel would carry that solute directly out of the medulla with no return path, rapidly washing out the corticopapillary gradient despite the vasa recta's passive permeability being unchanged.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this thought experiment isolates why the hairpin geometry itself, not just passive permeability, is what makes the vasa recta effective at preserving rather than dissipating the gradient.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 3 — hairpin loops let the ascending limb recapture solute from the descending limb","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate the biophysics of countercurrent exchange in the vasa recta, analyzing how slow capillary blood flow and highly permeable, passive hairpin loops prevent medullary solute washout to preserve the corticopapillary osmotic gradient","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman donates her left kidney to her brother. Before surgery, her blood pressure was 116/74 mm Hg and her serum creatinine was 0.8 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). During her donor work-up, a medical student reviewing renal microvascular anatomy notes that juxtamedullary nephrons, unlike her superficial cortical nephrons, are associated with peritubular capillaries organized as vasa recta rather than ordinary capillaries. Which functional consequence follows most directly from this anatomic difference?","choices":{"A":"Their vasa recta specifically enable countercurrent exchange that preserves a deep medullary gradient","B":"Superficial cortical nephrons achieve the same countercurrent exchange using ordinary capillaries","C":"Juxtamedullary nephrons lose the ability to concentrate urine because of that hairpin capillary arrangement","D":"Superficial cortical nephrons generate a deeper medullary gradient despite lacking vasa recta","E":"Juxtamedullary nephrons rely on active NaCl pumping by the vasa recta to preserve the gradient"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Juxtamedullary nephrons have long loops of Henle that descend deep into the medulla, and their specialized peritubular capillaries, the vasa recta, follow this same hairpin course to passively preserve the deep gradient those long loops help build; superficial cortical nephrons, whose short loops barely enter the outer medulla, have no comparable need for this hairpin capillary arrangement.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: pairing long-loop juxtamedullary nephrons with vasa recta and short-loop cortical nephrons with ordinary peritubular capillaries is a frequently tested anatomic correlation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 3 — vasa recta follow the long juxtamedullary loops to preserve the deep medullary gradient","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex2t_sdl19_q1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the generation of the corticopapillary osmotic gradient, deconstructing how active countercurrent multiplication and the \"single effect\" (apical NKCC2 solute transport across the water-impermeable thick ascending limb) establish medullary hypertonicity","batch":3,"stem":"A 44-year-old man with pulmonary edema is treated with a drug that blocks Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransport. While he is still receiving the drug, he undergoes water deprivation, and his maximal urine osmolality is 300 mOsmol/kg H2O (reference range: 50–1200 mOsmol/kg H2O) despite a high plasma vasopressin level. Loss of which process best explains this finding?","choices":{"A":"Active salt transport out of the ascending limb","B":"Passive water exit from the thin descending limb","C":"Urea delivery into the inner medullary interstitium","D":"Countercurrent exchange within the vasa recta","E":"Vasopressin binding to principal cell V2 receptors"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The single effect of countercurrent multiplication is active NaCl transport out of the water-impermeable thick ascending limb, which creates a horizontal gradient of about 200 mOsm/kg H2O that the countercurrent flow multiplies into the corticopapillary gradient. Blocking NKCC2 abolishes the single effect, the medullary gradient dissipates, and even maximal ADH can concentrate urine only to near-isosmotic levels. Passive water exit from the descending limb depends on that gradient but is not blocked by the drug. Urea recycling adds to the inner medullary gradient, but NaCl transport is the step the drug inhibits. Countercurrent exchange in the vasa recta preserves the gradient rather than generating it. A V2 receptor defect would leave the urine dilute rather than near isosmotic, and the drug acts on NKCC2, not on the receptor. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 19, countercurrent multiplication); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 6 (countercurrent multiplication and the corticopapillary gradient); Guyton and Hall Ch. 32 (loop diuretics impair urine concentration).","boardPrep":"Step 1: loop diuretics wash out the medullary gradient, so they impair both concentrating and diluting ability. COMLEX: because they also blunt urine concentration, loop diuretics are in general protective against hyponatremia, unlike thiazides.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 3 — loop diuretics bind the Cl- site of NKCC2 to stop the single effect","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex2t_sdl19_q2","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the molecular process of medullary urea recycling, evaluating how ADH-stimulated facilitated urea transporters (UT1) in the inner medullary collecting ducts deposit urea into the interstitium to generate roughly half of the corticopapillary gradient","batch":3,"stem":"A 38-year-old man with severe protein-calorie malnutrition reports nocturia. After 14 hours of water deprivation, his urine osmolality peaks at 500 mOsmol/kg H2O (reference range: 50–1200 mOsmol/kg H2O), and his serum sodium is 145 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L); plasma vasopressin is elevated, and desmopressin produces no further rise. Which deficiency best explains his limited concentrating ability?","choices":{"A":"Less sodium chloride pumped by the thick ascending limb","B":"Fewer aquaporin-2 channels inserted by principal cells","C":"Less urea deposited in the inner medullary interstitium","D":"Faster medullary blood flow washing out interstitial solute","E":"Reduced glomerular filtration of water and solute"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Urea contributes about half of the inner medullary osmolality. ADH stimulates UT-A1 and UT-A3 transporters in the inner medullary collecting duct, so urea diffuses into the interstitium and is recycled. With a very low protein intake, little urea is produced, the inner medullary gradient is lower, and maximal urine osmolality falls even though ADH is high. Thick ascending limb NaCl transport depends on Na+-K+-ATPase activity and is not limited by protein intake. Aquaporin-2 insertion is driven by the high ADH of water deprivation. Malnutrition does not speed medullary blood flow. Reduced filtration would affect volume more than the maximal osmolality of the urine produced. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 19, medullary urea recycling); Guyton and Hall Ch. 29 (urea transporters and impaired concentrating ability with low protein intake); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 6 (urea recycling).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ADH increases water permeability through aquaporin-2 and urea permeability through UT-A1 in the inner medullary collecting duct. COMLEX: malnourished and low-protein patients may concentrate urine poorly without true diabetes insipidus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 4 — ADH also makes the collecting duct water permeable so urine can equilibrate with the gradient","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex2t_sdl19_q3","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate the biophysics of countercurrent exchange in the vasa recta, analyzing how slow capillary blood flow and highly permeable, passive hairpin loops prevent medullary solute washout to preserve the corticopapillary osmotic gradient","batch":3,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with cirrhosis receives a vasodilator that preferentially dilates juxtamedullary efferent arterioles. Her urine osmolality falls from 900 to 450 mOsmol/kg H2O (reference range: 50–1200 mOsmol/kg H2O) while her plasma vasopressin level remains high. Which mechanism best explains the fall?","choices":{"A":"Fewer aquaporin-2 channels in the collecting duct","B":"Washout of interstitial solute by rapid vasa recta flow","C":"Inhibition of NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb","D":"Greater urea permeability of cortical collecting ducts","E":"Reduced filtrate delivery to the loop of Henle"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The vasa recta act as countercurrent exchangers: blood entering the medulla gains solute and loses water, and blood leaving it does the reverse, so solute stays trapped in the interstitium. This works because medullary blood flow is low. A large rise in flow carries NaCl and urea out faster than the loop and collecting duct replace them (medullary washout), the gradient falls, and urine osmolality drops despite ADH. Aquaporin-2 insertion is maintained by the high vasopressin. The vasodilator does not block NKCC2. The cortical collecting duct is relatively urea impermeable, and greater urea permeability there would not explain washout. Reduced delivery to the loop would not result from a vasodilator that increases flow. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 19, countercurrent exchange in the vasa recta); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 6 (vasa recta and countercurrent exchange); Guyton and Hall Ch. 29 (sluggish medullary blood flow and solute washout).","boardPrep":"Step 1: countercurrent multiplication creates the gradient, and countercurrent exchange in the vasa recta maintains it; high medullary flow causes washout. COMLEX: osmotic diuresis and loop diuretics also dissipate the medullary gradient.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 1 — countercurrent multiplication builds the gradient that the vasa recta preserve","isHighYield":true}],"20":[{"id":"sdl20_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Contrast the pathophysiology, etiology, and clinical presentation of SIADH and Diabetes Insipidus (central and nephrogenic), evaluating the diagnostic use of water deprivation testing and exogenous ADH (dDAVP) administration","batch":1,"stem":"What is the core physiological defect in SIADH?","choices":{"A":"ADH is released mainly in response to volume depletion, not in response to osmolarity","B":"ADH receptors are upregulated, amplifying a normal osmotic stimulus excessively","C":"ADH is absent, so principal cells fail to insert aquaporin channels","D":"ADH is present but principal cells are structurally resistant to its V2 receptor","E":"ADH is released autonomously, independent of plasma osmolarity or blood volume"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In SIADH, ADH is secreted autonomously, without regard to the usual osmotic or volume-based triggers; this unregulated secretion drives continuous water reabsorption regardless of the patient's actual osmotic or volume status, producing dilutional hyponatremia. Absent ADH describes central diabetes insipidus, and principal cell resistance to ADH describes nephrogenic diabetes insipidus; both cause dilute urine, the opposite of SIADH.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the word \"inappropriate\" in SIADH refers specifically to ADH secretion occurring without its normal physiologic trigger, not to any structural or receptor-level abnormality.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 1 — distinguishing SIADH from central and nephrogenic DI by ADH level and renal responsiveness","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Contrast the pathophysiology, etiology, and clinical presentation of SIADH and Diabetes Insipidus (central and nephrogenic), evaluating the diagnostic use of water deprivation testing and exogenous ADH (dDAVP) administration","batch":1,"stem":"Why does central diabetes insipidus produce dilute urine despite a strong osmotic stimulus?","choices":{"A":"The posterior pituitary secretes ADH normally, but principal cells fail to respond to it","B":"The hypothalamus overproduces ADH, paradoxically suppressing its own receptor sensitivity","C":"The collecting ducts secrete excess water directly, independent of any hormonal signal","D":"The hypothalamus or posterior pituitary cannot supply ADH, so ducts stay impermeable","E":"The kidneys actively excrete ADH before it can reach the collecting duct principal cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Central diabetes insipidus reflects a failure of the hypothalamus or posterior pituitary to synthesize, store, or release ADH; without circulating ADH, principal cells cannot insert aquaporin-2 channels, leaving the late distal tubule and collecting duct impermeable to water and producing large volumes of dilute urine despite rising plasma osmolarity.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this is the ADH-deficiency half of diabetes insipidus, distinguished from nephrogenic DI's ADH-resistance defect by the response to exogenous dDAVP.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 1 — distinguishing SIADH from central and nephrogenic DI by ADH level and renal responsiveness","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Contrast the pathophysiology, etiology, and clinical presentation of SIADH and Diabetes Insipidus (central and nephrogenic), evaluating the diagnostic use of water deprivation testing and exogenous ADH (dDAVP) administration","batch":1,"stem":"How does nephrogenic diabetes insipidus differ from central diabetes insipidus in terms of circulating ADH?","choices":{"A":"Circulating ADH is low or absent, identical to the defect in central diabetes insipidus","B":"Circulating ADH is normal, but osmoreceptors fail to detect rising plasma osmolarity","C":"Circulating ADH is normal or elevated, but principal cells fail to respond to it","D":"Circulating ADH is elevated, and principal cells respond normally but excessively","E":"Circulating ADH is absent because the kidneys destroy it before it reaches the nephron"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Unlike central diabetes insipidus, nephrogenic diabetes insipidus involves normal or even elevated circulating ADH; the defect instead lies in the renal principal cell, whether from a V2 receptor mutation, aquaporin-2 mutation, or acquired causes such as lithium toxicity, so the kidney remains unresponsive despite abundant hormone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a rising ADH level in nephrogenic DI reflects appropriate compensatory pituitary secretion in response to persistently high plasma osmolarity, not a primary secretory abnormality.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 1 — distinguishing SIADH from central and nephrogenic DI by ADH level and renal responsiveness","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the pharmacological management of dysnatremias, deconstructing the therapeutic mechanism of ADH antagonists (demeclocycline, vaptans), the physiological paradox of utilizing thiazide diuretics in nephrogenic DI, and the biophysical limits of sodium correction required to prevent Osmotic Demyelination Syndrome (ODS)","batch":1,"stem":"By what mechanism does demeclocycline treat SIADH?","choices":{"A":"It inhibits V2 signaling, inducing a controlled, reversible nephrogenic DI state","B":"It blocks ADH synthesis directly within the posterior pituitary gland itself","C":"It stimulates renal Na+ excretion directly, independent of any ADH-related pathway","D":"It increases aquaporin-2 degradation, without directly affecting V2 receptor signaling","E":"It competes with ADH for binding sites on the hypothalamic osmoreceptors directly"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Demeclocycline is a tetracycline derivative that interferes with V2 receptor-coupled Gs signaling and downstream cAMP generation in principal cells, deliberately inducing a state resembling nephrogenic diabetes insipidus; this blunts the excessive water reabsorption of SIADH and promotes solute-free water excretion, raising serum sodium. It does not act in the pituitary or on hypothalamic osmoreceptors; its target is ADH action on the renal principal cells.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: demeclocycline works at the receptor-signaling level in the kidney, not by reducing ADH secretion at the pituitary, which is why it remains effective even when the ectopic ADH source cannot be removed.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 2 — the drugs used to correct dysnatremias and the strict limits that prevent ODS","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the pharmacological management of dysnatremias, deconstructing the therapeutic mechanism of ADH antagonists (demeclocycline, vaptans), the physiological paradox of utilizing thiazide diuretics in nephrogenic DI, and the biophysical limits of sodium correction required to prevent Osmotic Demyelination Syndrome (ODS)","batch":1,"stem":"By what mechanism do vaptans raise serum sodium in SIADH?","choices":{"A":"They stimulate renal Na+ reabsorption, independent of any water excretion effect","B":"They competitively block ADH at the V2 receptor, preventing aquaporin-2 insertion","C":"They inhibit aquaporin-2 synthesis at the transcriptional level over days","D":"They increase ADH secretion, paradoxically promoting solute-free water excretion","E":"They inhibit the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter in the thick ascending limb directly"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Tolvaptan (a selective V2 receptor antagonist) and conivaptan (listed in the course notes as V2-selective) competitively block ADH binding on the basolateral membrane of principal cells, preventing aquaporin-2 channel translocation to the luminal membrane; this produces pure aquaresis, solute-free water excretion without a significant accompanying electrolyte loss, raising serum sodium. Inhibiting Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransport is how loop diuretics work, not vaptans.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"aquaresis\" (water loss without solute loss) is the key term that distinguishes vaptans from a standard diuretic, which excretes both water and solute together.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 2 — the drugs used to correct dysnatremias and the strict limits that prevent ODS","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the pharmacological management of dysnatremias, deconstructing the therapeutic mechanism of ADH antagonists (demeclocycline, vaptans), the physiological paradox of utilizing thiazide diuretics in nephrogenic DI, and the biophysical limits of sodium correction required to prevent Osmotic Demyelination Syndrome (ODS)","batch":1,"stem":"What is the strict upper limit for correcting severe hyponatremia within 24 hours to avoid osmotic demyelination syndrome?","choices":{"A":"About 15 to 20 mEq/L, with no meaningful hourly limit","B":"About 20 to 25 mEq/L, adjusted mainly for the patient's total body weight","C":"About 2 to 4 mEq/L, set without regard to the patient's risk factors","D":"About 8 to 10 mEq/L, monitored closely throughout the correction","E":"About 12 to 14 mEq/L, adjusted mainly for the patient's serum creatinine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Serum sodium correction in severe hyponatremia must not exceed about 8 to 10 mEq/L in any 24-hour period, because brain cells that have adapted to chronic hyponatremia by extruding organic osmolytes cannot re-accumulate them fast enough to keep pace with a more rapid rise in extracellular osmolarity; frequent monitoring during correction helps avoid exceeding this limit.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: in high-risk patients (malnutrition, alcoholism, advanced liver disease), the target correction limit is tightened further, to less than 6 to 8 mEq/L in 24 hours.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 2 — the drugs used to correct dysnatremias and the strict limits that prevent ODS","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Deconstruct the biophysics and mathematics of \"the normal saline trap\" in SIADH, explaining why infusing isotonic 0.9% saline into a patient with fixed concentrated urine results in net solute-free water retention and worsening hyponatremia","batch":1,"stem":"In SIADH, why does infusing isotonic 0.9% saline paradoxically worsen hyponatremia?","choices":{"A":"The saline directly stimulates additional ADH release from the posterior pituitary gland itself","B":"The saline is markedly hypotonic relative to plasma, diluting serum sodium immediately on contact","C":"The kidneys excrete the saline's entire solute load in a much larger urine volume than was infused","D":"The saline raises ECF volume substantially, triggering compensatory aldosterone-driven Na+ retention","E":"The fixed high urine osmolarity forces the infused solute into a smaller volume than infused"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Because ADH is fixed and high in SIADH, the kidney can only excrete urine at its fixed, elevated osmolarity; the roughly 308 mOsm of solute in a liter of normal saline is therefore excreted in a urine volume smaller than one liter, leaving the difference behind as retained solute-free water that dilutes serum sodium further.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the direction of the effect flips entirely with 3% hypertonic saline, whose osmolarity exceeds the fixed urine osmolarity, producing a net free water deficit instead.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 3 — why isotonic saline can paradoxically worsen hyponatremia in SIADH","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Deconstruct the biophysics and mathematics of \"the normal saline trap\" in SIADH, explaining why infusing isotonic 0.9% saline into a patient with fixed concentrated urine results in net solute-free water retention and worsening hyponatremia","batch":1,"stem":"What general biophysical rule predicts whether an IV fluid will worsen or improve hyponatremia in SIADH?","choices":{"A":"Fluid worsens hyponatremia whenever its osmolarity is lower than plasma osmolarity by itself","B":"Fluid worsens hyponatremia whenever its osmolarity is lower than the fixed urine osmolarity","C":"Fluid worsens hyponatremia whenever its total volume exceeds the patient's daily urine output","D":"Fluid improves hyponatremia whenever its osmolarity is lower than the fixed urine osmolarity","E":"Fluid improves hyponatremia whenever its total volume is less than one liter infused"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because the SIADH kidney excretes urine at a fixed osmolarity regardless of intake, any infused fluid with an osmolarity below that fixed urine osmolarity requires more urine volume to excrete its solute than was infused, leaving free water behind; a fluid with a higher osmolarity than the fixed urine osmolarity does the opposite, producing a net water deficit.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this rule is why plain water, D5W, and even 0.9% saline can all worsen hyponatremia in SIADH, while only hypertonic saline reliably improves it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 3 — why isotonic saline can paradoxically worsen hyponatremia in SIADH","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Deconstruct the biophysics and mathematics of \"the normal saline trap\" in SIADH, explaining why infusing isotonic 0.9% saline into a patient with fixed concentrated urine results in net solute-free water retention and worsening hyponatremia","batch":1,"stem":"Which intervention would be expected to raise serum sodium in a patient with SIADH and a fixed urine osmolarity of 600 mOsm/L?","choices":{"A":"3% hypertonic saline, since its osmolarity exceeds the fixed urine osmolarity","B":"0.9% normal saline, since its osmolarity exceeds the fixed urine osmolarity","C":"5% dextrose in water, since its osmolarity exceeds the fixed urine osmolarity","D":"Lactated Ringer's solution, since its osmolarity exceeds the fixed urine osmolarity","E":"0.45% half-normal saline, since its osmolarity exceeds the fixed urine osmolarity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Only a fluid whose osmolarity exceeds the patient's fixed urine osmolarity of 600 mOsm/L will raise serum sodium; 3% hypertonic saline has an osmolarity around 1026 mOsm/L, comfortably above this threshold, while normal saline (308 mOsm/L), isotonic lactated Ringer's solution, D5W (effectively near 0), and half-normal saline (154 mOsm/L) all fall below it and would worsen hyponatremia instead.","boardPrep":"Step 1: memorize the rule, not just the drug name — any hypotonic-relative-to-urine fluid worsens SIADH hyponatremia, and hypertonic saline is simply the fluid that reliably clears that bar.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 3 — why isotonic saline can paradoxically worsen hyponatremia in SIADH","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Evaluate the reciprocal autonomic and somatic motor pathways (sympathetic L1-L3, parasympathetic S2-S4, somatic S2-S4) regulating lower urinary tract dynamics, utilizing these pathways to differentiate neurogenic bladder types (atonic/flaccid vs. spastic/hyperreflexic) and the clinical etiologies of urge, stress, and overflow incontinence","batch":1,"stem":"What is the net action of sympathetic innervation on the bladder during the storage phase?","choices":{"A":"It contracts the detrusor via beta-3 receptors and relaxes the internal sphincter via alpha-1 receptors","B":"It relaxes the detrusor via beta-3 receptors and tightens the internal sphincter via alpha-1 receptors","C":"It relaxes the detrusor via muscarinic receptors and constricts the sphincter via nicotinic receptors","D":"It contracts the detrusor via alpha-1 receptors and relaxes the sphincter via beta-3 receptors","E":"It has no direct effect on the detrusor and mainly modulates external sphincter tone"},"correct":"B","explanation":"During storage, sympathetic fibers of the hypogastric nerve (sympathetic outflow from T11-L2) release norepinephrine, which relaxes detrusor smooth muscle via beta-3 receptors (increasing bladder compliance) and simultaneously constricts the internal urethral sphincter via alpha-1 receptors, together promoting continence while the bladder fills.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: pair beta-3 (detrusor relaxation) with alpha-1 (sphincter constriction) as the two sympathetic storage-phase effects, both driven by the same sympathetic T11-L2 outflow through the hypogastric nerve.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 4 — the sympathetic/parasympathetic/somatic pathways that store and void urine","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Evaluate the reciprocal autonomic and somatic motor pathways (sympathetic L1-L3, parasympathetic S2-S4, somatic S2-S4) regulating lower urinary tract dynamics, utilizing these pathways to differentiate neurogenic bladder types (atonic/flaccid vs. spastic/hyperreflexic) and the clinical etiologies of urge, stress, and overflow incontinence","batch":1,"stem":"What is the net action of parasympathetic innervation on the bladder during voiding?","choices":{"A":"It contracts the detrusor via M3 receptors and relaxes the internal sphincter via nitric oxide","B":"It relaxes the detrusor via M3 receptors and contracts the internal sphincter via nitric oxide","C":"It contracts the detrusor via beta-3 receptors and relaxes the sphincter via alpha-1 receptors","D":"It relaxes the detrusor via alpha-1 receptors and contracts the sphincter via M3 receptors","E":"It has no effect on the internal sphincter and mainly acts on the external sphincter"},"correct":"A","explanation":"During voiding, parasympathetic fibers from spinal segments S2 to S4 release acetylcholine, which drives powerful detrusor contraction via M3 muscarinic receptors while separately triggering nitric-oxide-mediated relaxation of the internal urethral sphincter, together coordinating effective bladder emptying.","boardPrep":"Step 1: M3 (detrusor contraction) and nitric oxide (sphincter relaxation) are both parasympathetic S2-S4 effects, the functional opposite of the sympathetic storage-phase pairing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 4 — the sympathetic/parasympathetic/somatic pathways that store and void urine","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Evaluate the reciprocal autonomic and somatic motor pathways (sympathetic L1-L3, parasympathetic S2-S4, somatic S2-S4) regulating lower urinary tract dynamics, utilizing these pathways to differentiate neurogenic bladder types (atonic/flaccid vs. spastic/hyperreflexic) and the clinical etiologies of urge, stress, and overflow incontinence","batch":1,"stem":"Which nerve provides voluntary somatic control of the external urethral sphincter?","choices":{"A":"The pelvic splanchnic nerve, via muscarinic receptors on smooth sphincter muscle","B":"The hypogastric nerve, via alpha-1 receptors acting on smooth muscle","C":"The pudendal nerve, via nicotinic receptors on striated sphincter muscle","D":"The pudendal nerve, via muscarinic receptors on smooth sphincter muscle","E":"The obturator nerve, via nicotinic receptors on striated sphincter muscle"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The pudendal nerve carries somatic motor fibers from the sacral spinal cord (S2-S4) to the striated external urethral sphincter, releasing acetylcholine onto nicotinic receptors to provide tonic, voluntary contraction that maintains continence and can be voluntarily relaxed during voiding.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the external sphincter is the striated, voluntarily controlled urethral sphincter; the internal sphincter is smooth muscle under autonomic control.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 4 — the sympathetic/parasympathetic/somatic pathways that store and void urine","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Contrast the pathophysiology, etiology, and clinical presentation of SIADH and Diabetes Insipidus (central and nephrogenic), evaluating the diagnostic use of water deprivation testing and exogenous ADH (dDAVP) administration","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman with polyuria and excessive fluid intake undergoes a water deprivation test. Urine osmolarity rises to 900 mOsm/kg as plasma osmolarity increases. Which diagnosis is best supported by this result?","choices":{"A":"Central diabetes insipidus, since urine remained dilute despite rising plasma osmolarity","B":"Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, since urine remained persistently dilute despite rising plasma osmolarity","C":"SIADH, since urine osmolarity rose despite a falling plasma osmolarity","D":"Primary polydipsia, since intact ADH release concentrated the urine while plasma osmolarity climbed","E":"Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, since dDAVP administration produced no further concentration"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A urine osmolarity that concentrates appropriately (here, 900 mOsm/kg) during water deprivation shows that both ADH secretion and renal responsiveness are intact, which points to primary polydipsia as the cause of her polyuria rather than either form of diabetes insipidus, which would not concentrate urine to this degree under the same osmotic stimulus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the water deprivation test's first phase distinguishes primary polydipsia from both types of DI; the second phase, exogenous dDAVP, is what then distinguishes central from nephrogenic DI.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — a urine that concentrates during water deprivation points away from diabetes insipidus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Contrast the pathophysiology, etiology, and clinical presentation of SIADH and Diabetes Insipidus (central and nephrogenic), evaluating the diagnostic use of water deprivation testing and exogenous ADH (dDAVP) administration","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with polyuria has a urine osmolarity of 180 mOsm/kg despite water deprivation and a plasma osmolarity of 300 mOsm/kg. After receiving dDAVP, his urine osmolarity rises to 650 mOsm/kg. Which diagnosis is best supported by this response?","choices":{"A":"Central diabetes insipidus, since exogenous ADH restored a robust concentrating response","B":"Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, since exogenous ADH restored a robust concentrating response","C":"Primary polydipsia, since exogenous ADH restored a robust concentrating response","D":"Central diabetes insipidus, since exogenous ADH produced no meaningful change","E":"Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, since exogenous ADH produced no meaningful change"},"correct":"A","explanation":"His persistently dilute urine despite water deprivation first rules out primary polydipsia; the dramatic rise in urine osmolarity after exogenous dDAVP then shows that his principal cells are fully capable of responding to ADH once it is supplied, confirming that his own posterior pituitary was the source of the deficiency, the defining feature of central diabetes insipidus.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: a nephrogenic DI patient would show little to no response (typically less than 10-15% change) to the same dDAVP challenge, since the defect is intrinsic to the kidney.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — a robust dDAVP response separates central from nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Contrast the pathophysiology, etiology, and clinical presentation of SIADH and Diabetes Insipidus (central and nephrogenic), evaluating the diagnostic use of water deprivation testing and exogenous ADH (dDAVP) administration","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman on long-term lithium for bipolar disorder develops polyuria and polydipsia with dilute urine despite an elevated plasma ADH level. Which mechanism best explains her findings?","choices":{"A":"Lithium suppresses posterior pituitary ADH secretion, producing acquired central diabetes insipidus","B":"Lithium impairs principal cell responsiveness to ADH, producing acquired nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","C":"Lithium directly stimulates thirst, producing primary polydipsia independent of ADH signaling","D":"Lithium destroys the corticopapillary osmotic gradient through direct medullary toxicity","E":"Lithium excessively increases aquaporin-2 synthesis and inappropriate water reabsorption"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Lithium is a well-recognized cause of acquired nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, impairing principal cell responsiveness to ADH at the receptor and post-receptor level; her elevated plasma ADH represents an appropriate compensatory pituitary response to the resulting polyuria and rising plasma osmolarity, not a primary secretory defect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: lithium-induced nephrogenic DI is managed by minimizing lithium where possible and using amiloride, which blocks lithium entry into principal cells through ENaC; thiazides treat other forms of nephrogenic DI but raise lithium levels, so they are used with caution here.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — lithium causes acquired nephrogenic DI by impairing principal cell responsiveness to ADH","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the pharmacological management of dysnatremias, deconstructing the therapeutic mechanism of ADH antagonists (demeclocycline, vaptans), the physiological paradox of utilizing thiazide diuretics in nephrogenic DI, and the biophysical limits of sodium correction required to prevent Osmotic Demyelination Syndrome (ODS)","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with alcohol use disorder and malnutrition has severe hyponatremia. Which correction target is most appropriate to minimize his risk of osmotic demyelination syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Up to 10 to 12 mEq/L over 24 hours, a ceiling above the standard 8 to 10 mEq/L limit","B":"Up to 15 to 20 mEq/L over 24 hours, appropriate for severe symptomatic hyponatremia cases","C":"Less than 1 to 2 mEq/L over 24 hours, an unnecessarily conservative correction target","D":"Up to 25 mEq/L over the first 24 hours whenever hypertonic saline is the chosen fluid","E":"Less than 6 to 8 mEq/L over 24 hours, given his additional risk factors"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Patients with malnutrition, alcohol use disorder, or advanced liver disease are at higher risk for osmotic demyelination syndrome at any given correction rate, so their target is tightened below the standard 8 to 10 mEq/L per 24 hours to a stricter limit of less than 6 to 8 mEq/L over the same period.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: always ask about alcohol use, malnutrition, and liver disease when setting a correction target, since these patients need a lower ceiling than the general 8-10 mEq/L/24h rule.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 2 — correction limits are tightened further in patients at high risk for ODS","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the pharmacological management of dysnatremias, deconstructing the therapeutic mechanism of ADH antagonists (demeclocycline, vaptans), the physiological paradox of utilizing thiazide diuretics in nephrogenic DI, and the biophysical limits of sodium correction required to prevent Osmotic Demyelination Syndrome (ODS)","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with decompensated heart failure and hyponatremia is started on tolvaptan. Which mechanism accounts for the expected rise in his serum sodium?","choices":{"A":"Tolvaptan blocks V2 receptors, producing pure aquaresis without notable electrolyte loss","B":"Tolvaptan blocks aldosterone receptors, producing combined natriuresis and diuresis","C":"Tolvaptan inhibits the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter, producing a loop-diuretic effect","D":"Tolvaptan stimulates ADH release, paradoxically increasing free water excretion","E":"Tolvaptan blocks V1 receptors, reducing systemic vascular resistance directly"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Tolvaptan's V2 receptor blockade prevents aquaporin-2 insertion in principal cells, producing solute-free water excretion (aquaresis) without a proportional loss of Na+ or K+; this raises serum sodium by removing excess free water, distinguishing it mechanistically from a conventional diuretic that excretes water together with solute.","boardPrep":"Step 1: vaptans are used for euvolemic (SIADH) and hypervolemic (heart failure) hyponatremia because they correct free water excess without worsening volume status the way a standard diuretic might.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 2 — vaptans block the V2 receptor to cause aquaresis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the pharmacological management of dysnatremias, deconstructing the therapeutic mechanism of ADH antagonists (demeclocycline, vaptans), the physiological paradox of utilizing thiazide diuretics in nephrogenic DI, and the biophysical limits of sodium correction required to prevent Osmotic Demyelination Syndrome (ODS)","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman with hereditary nephrogenic diabetes insipidus from a V2 receptor mutation is started on hydrochlorothiazide and her urine output falls substantially. Which classically described mechanism best explains this improvement?","choices":{"A":"Hydrochlorothiazide restores principal cell sensitivity to circulating ADH directly","B":"Mild ECF contraction increases proximal reabsorption, limiting distal delivery","C":"Hydrochlorothiazide raises ADH release enough to overcome the mutant receptor","D":"Hydrochlorothiazide corrects the receptor mutation responsible for her renal ADH resistance","E":"Hydrochlorothiazide suppresses thirst centrally, reducing her total fluid intake"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Thiazide inhibition of the Na+–Cl− cotransporter in the early distal tubule causes sodium loss and mild ECF volume contraction; GFR falls and proximal tubular Na+ and water reabsorption rises, so less fluid reaches the ADH-unresponsive collecting duct and less water is excreted (Costanzo Ch 6 and Ch 9; course thiazide brick, SDL 45 PDF p9, which reports urine volume reduced by up to 50 percent). Guyton Ch 29 (course SDL 13–15/19 supplement) likewise lists a thiazide plus a low-sodium diet for nephrogenic DI. Katzung Ch 15 (course SDL 44–46 supplement, p30) calls this volume mechanism what was \"previously thought\" and shows a different one for lithium-induced disease (higher papillary osmolality, partial aquaporin-2 recovery), but states it is \"not yet clear\" whether that mechanism applies to other forms of nephrogenic DI, so for a hereditary V2-receptor defect the volume-contraction account remains the one the sources give. ADH levels are already high in nephrogenic DI and the receptor cannot respond (Costanzo Ch 6), so raising ADH release would not help; thiazides also do not restore principal-cell ADH sensitivity, correct the mutation, or suppress thirst.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: thiazides (with dietary sodium restriction) treat nephrogenic DI; the classic explanation is volume contraction with increased proximal reabsorption (Costanzo; course thiazide brick), while Katzung shows a papillary-osmolality and aquaporin-2 mechanism only for lithium-induced disease and calls the mechanism in other forms not yet clear. In lithium-induced nephrogenic DI, amiloride can partly reverse the polyuria by blocking Li+ entry into collecting duct cells, and serum Li+ must be monitored if a thiazide is used because diuretics reduce Li+ clearance (Katzung Ch 15 and Ch 29); First Aid lists HCTZ, amiloride, and indomethacin for nephrogenic DI.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 2 — thiazides reduce urine output in nephrogenic DI by causing mild ECF contraction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Deconstruct the biophysics and mathematics of \"the normal saline trap\" in SIADH, explaining why infusing isotonic 0.9% saline into a patient with fixed concentrated urine results in net solute-free water retention and worsening hyponatremia","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with small cell carcinoma of the lung is admitted because of confusion. He appears euvolemic. Blood pressure is 126/78 mm Hg. Serum sodium is 124 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), and serum osmolality is 258 mOsm/kg (reference range: 275–295 mOsm/kg). He is diagnosed with the syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion, and his urine osmolarity is fixed at 600 mOsm/L (reference range: 50–1,200 mOsm/L). He receives 1 liter of 0.9% saline, containing 308 mOsm total solute. Approximately how much solute-free water would be expected to remain in his body after this infusion?","choices":{"A":"About 1.0 liter, since very little of the infused solute would be excreted in the urine","B":"About 0.3 liters, since roughly 0.7 liters of urine would be needed to excrete the infused solute","C":"Zero liters, since isotonic saline does not affect free water balance in SIADH","D":"About 0.5 liters, since roughly 0.5 liters of urine is needed to excrete the infused solute","E":"About 0.7 liters, since roughly 0.3 liters of urine is needed to excrete the infused solute"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Dividing the infused solute load (308 mOsm) by the fixed urine osmolarity (600 mOsm/L) gives a required excretion volume of about 0.5 liters; subtracting that from the 1.0 liter infused leaves approximately 0.5 liters of solute-free water retained in the body, diluting serum sodium further.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this is the exact calculation to reproduce for any saline-trap question — solute load divided by fixed urine osmolarity gives required urine volume, and infused volume minus that gives retained free water.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — the medullary gradient, with high ADH, is what fixes this patient's urine at a high osmolarity","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Deconstruct the biophysics and mathematics of \"the normal saline trap\" in SIADH, explaining why infusing isotonic 0.9% saline into a patient with fixed concentrated urine results in net solute-free water retention and worsening hyponatremia","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with small cell carcinoma of the lung is admitted because of lethargy. He appears euvolemic. Blood pressure is 130/80 mm Hg. Serum sodium is 119 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), and serum osmolality is 248 mOsm/kg (reference range: 275–295 mOsm/kg). He is diagnosed with the syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion, and his urine osmolarity is fixed at 600 mOsm/L (reference range: 50–1,200 mOsm/L). He receives 1 liter of 3% saline, containing roughly 1,000 mOsm total solute. Which outcome would be expected regarding his free water balance?","choices":{"A":"A net free water surplus, since less than a liter of urine is needed to excrete the infused solute","B":"A net free water deficit, since more than a liter of urine is needed to excrete the infused solute","C":"No net change in free water, since hypertonic saline behaves identically to isotonic saline","D":"A net free water surplus, since hypertonic saline directly suppresses ADH secretion","E":"A net free water deficit, driven primarily by centrally stimulated thirst"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Dividing the larger solute load (about 1000 mOsm) by the same fixed urine osmolarity (600 mOsm/L) requires roughly 1.67 liters of urine to excrete it; because this exceeds the 1.0 liter infused, the patient ends up with a net free water deficit, raising serum sodium instead of lowering it.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: at this urine osmolarity, 1 L of isotonic saline (308 mOsm) needs only about 0.5 L of urine for excretion and so leaves free water behind, which is why hypertonic, not isotonic, saline corrects hyponatremia in SIADH.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 3 — hypertonic saline exceeds the fixed urine osmolarity and so produces a free water deficit","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Deconstruct the biophysics and mathematics of \"the normal saline trap\" in SIADH, explaining why infusing isotonic 0.9% saline into a patient with fixed concentrated urine results in net solute-free water retention and worsening hyponatremia","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man is admitted after a fall and is found to have pneumonia. He appears euvolemic. Blood pressure is 134/82 mm Hg. Serum sodium is 126 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), and serum osmolality is 262 mOsm/kg (reference range: 275–295 mOsm/kg). He is diagnosed with the syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion, and his urine osmolarity is fixed at 500 mOsm/L (reference range: 50–1,200 mOsm/L). He is inadvertently given an infusion of 5% dextrose in water (D5W). Which effect on his serum sodium would be expected?","choices":{"A":"A rise toward normal, since D5W provides free water and calories without any true osmotic effect","B":"No meaningful change occurs, since D5W behaves as essentially isotonic once administered intravenously","C":"A further decline, since the retained solute-free water lowers plasma tonicity further","D":"A further decline caused mainly by direct central suppression of endogenous ADH secretion","E":"A rise toward normal, since D5W's effective osmolarity substantially exceeds the fixed urine osmolarity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Once metabolized, D5W behaves as nearly pure free water with an effective osmolarity close to zero, far below the fixed urine osmolarity of 500 mOsm/L; because any fluid whose effective osmolarity is below the fixed urine osmolarity is partly retained as free water, essentially all of it is retained as free water, worsening hyponatremia even more severely than isotonic saline would, since D5W carries no solute at all to be excreted. D5W does not act by suppressing ADH centrally; in SIADH ADH is secreted autonomously, and the further fall comes from retained free water.","boardPrep":"Step 1: because any fluid less concentrated than the fixed urine is partly retained as free water, D5W is a poor fluid choice in SIADH, since it has essentially no solute to help drive its own excretion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 3 — D5W carries no solute, so it is retained as free water in SIADH","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Evaluate the reciprocal autonomic and somatic motor pathways (sympathetic L1-L3, parasympathetic S2-S4, somatic S2-S4) regulating lower urinary tract dynamics, utilizing these pathways to differentiate neurogenic bladder types (atonic/flaccid vs. spastic/hyperreflexic) and the clinical etiologies of urge, stress, and overflow incontinence","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old man sustains a suprasacral spinal cord injury in a motor vehicle accident. Several weeks later, he develops involuntary bladder contractions against a simultaneously contracting external sphincter, with markedly elevated intravesical pressures. Which mechanism best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Loss of the sacral reflex arc itself abolishes detrusor contraction, producing a flaccid, overdistended atonic bladder","B":"Loss of sympathetic innervation directly causes involuntary detrusor contraction to occur spontaneously","C":"Loss of pudendal nerve function eliminates voluntary external urethral sphincter tone","D":"Loss of parasympathetic innervation prevents the detrusor from contracting, so the bladder overfills","E":"Loss of pontine coordination leaves the sacral reflex arc uninhibited, causing detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A suprasacral spinal cord injury interrupts the descending pathways from the pontine micturition center while leaving the sacral reflex arc (S2-S4) intact; the resulting spastic, hyperreflexic detrusor contracts involuntarily but without the normal coordinated relaxation of the external sphincter, producing detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia and dangerously high intravesical pressures.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: contrast this upper-motor-neuron picture with the lower-motor-neuron atonic bladder from a sacral or cauda equina lesion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 4 — a suprasacral lesion leaves the sacral reflex arc intact and causes a spastic bladder","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Evaluate the reciprocal autonomic and somatic motor pathways (sympathetic L1-L3, parasympathetic S2-S4, somatic S2-S4) regulating lower urinary tract dynamics, utilizing these pathways to differentiate neurogenic bladder types (atonic/flaccid vs. spastic/hyperreflexic) and the clinical etiologies of urge, stress, and overflow incontinence","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old man with a cauda equina injury develops continuous urine dribbling with a massively distended, thin-walled bladder and no sensation of fullness. Which mechanism best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Loss of parasympathetic detrusor innervation abolishes micturition, causing passive overdistention","B":"Loss of sympathetic innervation abolishes detrusor relaxation, causing spastic overactivity","C":"Loss of pudendal nerve function chiefly abolishes the voluntary micturition reflex control","D":"Loss of descending pontine inhibition instead produces detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia","E":"Loss of somatic innervation directly triggers involuntary detrusor contraction spontaneously"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A cauda equina lesion damages the sacral nerve roots that carry parasympathetic fibers to the detrusor, abolishing the micturition reflex and interrupting the visceral sensory afferents that normally signal bladder fullness; the bladder then fills passively until pressure exceeds sphincter resistance, producing overflow incontinence with a hypotonic, overdistended bladder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this atonic, lower-motor-neuron bladder is the functional opposite of the spastic, upper-motor-neuron bladder from the suprasacral injury pattern.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 4 — loss of sacral parasympathetic input causes an atonic bladder with overflow","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Evaluate the reciprocal autonomic and somatic motor pathways (sympathetic L1-L3, parasympathetic S2-S4, somatic S2-S4) regulating lower urinary tract dynamics, utilizing these pathways to differentiate neurogenic bladder types (atonic/flaccid vs. spastic/hyperreflexic) and the clinical etiologies of urge, stress, and overflow incontinence","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man with benign prostatic hyperplasia reports frequent dribbling of urine and a sensation of incomplete emptying, with a large post-void residual volume on bladder scan. Which type of incontinence and underlying cause best fit this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Stress incontinence, caused by pelvic floor muscle weakness","B":"Urge incontinence, caused by uninhibited detrusor overactivity","C":"Overflow incontinence, caused by mechanical outlet obstruction","D":"Stress incontinence, caused by detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia","E":"Urge incontinence, caused by mechanical bladder outlet obstruction"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Benign prostatic hyperplasia mechanically obstructs the bladder outlet, preventing complete emptying and leading to an elevated post-void residual volume; as the bladder becomes chronically overdistended, urine dribbles out once intravesical pressure exceeds outlet resistance, the defining pattern of overflow incontinence from a mechanical rather than a neurogenic cause.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: BPH-related overflow incontinence is mechanical, not neurogenic, distinguishing it from the atonic neurogenic bladder that produces the same overflow pattern through nerve injury instead.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 4 — overflow incontinence can arise from mechanical outlet obstruction as well as nerve injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex2t_sdl20_q1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Contrast the pathophysiology, etiology, and clinical presentation of SIADH and Diabetes Insipidus (central and nephrogenic), evaluating the diagnostic use of water deprivation testing and exogenous ADH (dDAVP) administration","batch":3,"stem":"A 46-year-old man treated with lithium for 6 years has polyuria of 5 L daily. After water deprivation, his serum sodium is 150 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L) and urine osmolality is 180 mOsmol/kg H2O (reference range: 50–1200 mOsmol/kg H2O), which changes to 195 mOsmol/kg H2O (reference range: 50–1200 mOsmol/kg H2O) after desmopressin. Where is the defect most likely located?","choices":{"A":"The supraoptic nucleus","B":"The posterior pituitary gland","C":"The thick ascending limb of Henle","D":"The proximal convoluted tubule","E":"The collecting duct principal cell"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Dilute urine after water deprivation with almost no response to desmopressin indicates nephrogenic diabetes insipidus. Lithium interferes with V2 receptor signaling and aquaporin-2 expression in principal cells, so ADH fails to raise collecting duct water permeability. Lesions of the supraoptic nucleus or posterior pituitary cause central diabetes insipidus, in which desmopressin produces a large rise in urine osmolality. The thick ascending limb generates the medullary gradient, but a thick ascending limb defect would leave urine near isosmotic rather than dilute at 180 mOsmol/kg H2O, and lithium does not act there. The proximal tubule reabsorbs fluid isosmotically and does not determine final urine osmolality. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 20, SIADH and diabetes insipidus); Katzung Ch. 15 (lithium-induced nephrogenic diabetes insipidus and amiloride); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 6 (ADH action and nephrogenic diabetes insipidus).","boardPrep":"Step 1: lithium is the most common drug cause of nephrogenic diabetes insipidus; amiloride is useful for lithium-induced nephrogenic diabetes insipidus. COMLEX: thiazides paradoxically reduce urine output in nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, but they raise lithium levels.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 4 — V2 receptor signaling inserts aquaporin-2 into principal cell membranes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex2t_sdl20_q2","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Deconstruct the biophysics and mathematics of \"the normal saline trap\" in SIADH, explaining why infusing isotonic 0.9% saline into a patient with fixed concentrated urine results in net solute-free water retention and worsening hyponatremia","batch":3,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman with small cell lung cancer has a serum sodium of 122 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), serum osmolality of 258 mOsmol/kg H2O (reference range: 275–295 mOsmol/kg H2O), and urine osmolality of 616 mOsmol/kg H2O (reference range: 50–1200 mOsmol/kg H2O). She receives 1 L of isotonic saline, and her serum sodium falls further. Which mechanism explains the fall?","choices":{"A":"Excretion of the infused solute in 2 L of urine","B":"Movement of sodium into cells driven by vasopressin","C":"Retention of about 0.5 L of solute-free water","D":"Reduced glomerular filtration of the infused sodium","E":"Stimulation of thirst with added free water intake"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In SIADH the urine osmolality is fixed by persistent ADH, and volume status is near normal, so infused sodium is excreted. One liter of isotonic saline contains about 308 mOsmol of solute; excreting that solute at a fixed urine osmolality of 616 mOsmol/kg H2O takes only about 0.5 L of urine, so about 0.5 L of the infused water is retained as solute-free water and serum sodium falls (the saline trap). Excreting the same solute in 2 L of urine would require a dilute urine, which high ADH prevents. Vasopressin acts on collecting duct water permeability and does not move sodium into cells. GFR does not fall, and euvolemic patients excrete the sodium load. Thirst is not stimulated by a falling osmolality. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 20, the normal saline trap in SIADH); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 6 (SIADH and free-water balance).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in SIADH, isotonic saline worsens hyponatremia when urine osmolality exceeds the saline osmolality; treat with fluid restriction, hypertonic saline, or vaptans. COMLEX: small cell lung cancer is the classic ectopic ADH source.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 2 — vaptans and hypertonic saline correct hyponatremia when saline fails, with limits on correction rate","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex2t_sdl20_q3","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Evaluate the reciprocal autonomic and somatic motor pathways (sympathetic L1-L3, parasympathetic S2-S4, somatic S2-S4) regulating lower urinary tract dynamics, utilizing these pathways to differentiate neurogenic bladder types (atonic/flaccid vs. spastic/hyperreflexic) and the clinical etiologies of urge, stress, and overflow incontinence","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman has 6 months of sudden, intense urges to void, often leaking urine before she reaches the toilet, and she wakes three times each night to urinate. She has no leakage with coughing or lifting. Neurologic examination is normal, urinalysis is negative, and the postvoid residual volume is 30 mL. Which mechanism best explains her incontinence?","choices":{"A":"Involuntary contraction of an overactive detrusor muscle","B":"Weak urethral support against rising abdominal pressure","C":"Poor detrusor contractility with a distended bladder","D":"Bladder outlet obstruction with incomplete emptying","E":"Detrusor contraction against a contracting external sphincter"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Urge incontinence results from an overactive detrusor muscle, producing a sudden urge to urinate and involuntary urination, with frequent voiding that is often worse at night; it is a clinical diagnosis, is commonly idiopathic, and is treated with pelvic floor exercises and antimuscarinics such as oxybutynin, which block the detrusor M3 receptor. Weak urethral support, from urethral hypermobility or intrinsic sphincter deficiency, causes stress incontinence with leakage on coughing or lifting, which she does not have. Poor detrusor contractility or outlet obstruction causes overflow incontinence with incomplete emptying and a high postvoid residual, but hers is normal. Detrusor contraction against a contracting external sphincter is detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia from a suprasacral cord lesion, excluded by her normal neurologic examination.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Urge = detrusor overactivity (antimuscarinics such as oxybutynin; the beta-3 agonist mirabegron also relaxes the detrusor); stress = urethral hypermobility or sphincter deficiency (leak with cough); overflow = poor detrusor contractility or outlet obstruction (high postvoid residual). COMLEX: The postvoid residual and a cough stress test separate the three types at the bedside.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 30 → Obj 7 — stress incontinence from weak urethral support","isHighYield":true},{"id":"grid_sdl20_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Contrast the pathophysiology, etiology, and clinical presentation of SIADH and Diabetes Insipidus (central and nephrogenic), evaluating the diagnostic use of water deprivation testing and exogenous ADH (dDAVP) administration","batch":4,"stem":"A 66-year-old man is brought to the emergency department by his wife because of 5 days of increasing confusion and headache. He has smoked 1 pack of cigarettes daily for 45 years and has lost 6 kg over the past 3 months. He takes no medications. Blood pressure is 134/82 mm Hg, and pulse is 78/min. Mucous membranes are moist, skin turgor is normal, jugular venous pressure is not elevated, and there is no peripheral edema. A chest x-ray shows a right hilar mass. Laboratory studies show a serum sodium of 121 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), glucose of 92 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL), and TSH of 2.1 µU/mL (reference range: 0.4–4.0 µU/mL); a morning serum cortisol concentration is within the reference range. Which of the following sets of additional laboratory findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["Serum osmolality","Urine osmolality","Urine Na⁺"]},"choices":{"A":["↓","↓","↓"],"B":["↓","↑","↓"],"C":["↓","↑","↑"],"D":["↑","↓","normal"],"E":["↑","↑","↑"]},"correct":"C","explanation":"A heavy smoker with a hilar mass, euvolemic hyponatremia, and normal glucose, thyroid, and adrenal function has ectopic ADH secretion (SIADH), most often from small cell lung carcinoma. The pattern (↓ serum osmolality, ↑ urine osmolality, ↑ urine Na⁺) is correct. Unsuppressed ADH keeps aquaporin-2 in the collecting duct membrane, so water is retained and serum osmolality falls while the urine is inappropriately concentrated (typically above 300 mOsm/kg despite dilute plasma); the mild volume expansion causes pressure natriuresis, so urine Na⁺ is high (above 20–40 mEq/L). The pattern (↓ serum osmolality, ↓ urine osmolality, ↓ urine Na⁺) is appropriate ADH suppression with maximally dilute urine, as in primary polydipsia. The pattern (↓ serum osmolality, ↑ urine osmolality, ↓ urine Na⁺) is hypovolemic hyponatremia from extrarenal losses: low volume stimulates ADH, but the kidney also conserves Na⁺, so urine Na⁺ is below 20 mEq/L; this patient is clinically euvolemic. The pattern (↑ serum osmolality, ↓ urine osmolality, normal urine Na⁺) is diabetes insipidus, in which free-water loss raises serum osmolality when water intake does not keep up, while the urine stays dilute. The pattern (↑ serum osmolality, ↑ urine osmolality, ↑ urine Na⁺) is osmotic diuresis, as from severe hyperglycemia or mannitol: the unreabsorbed solute drags water and Na⁺ into the urine, so urine osmolality and urine Na⁺ are high and serum osmolality rises; the normal glucose here excludes it, and the serum osmolality is low, not high.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SIADH = euvolemic hyponatremia with low serum osmolality, inappropriately concentrated urine, and urine Na⁺ above 20–40 mEq/L; exclude hypothyroidism and adrenal insufficiency first. COMLEX: correct chronic hyponatremia by no more than 8–10 mEq/L in 24 hours to avoid osmotic demyelination syndrome.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 3 — a fixed, concentrated urine is why 0.9% saline can lower the serum sodium further in SIADH","isHighYield":true}],"21":[{"id":"sdl21_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Logarithmic pH Relationship","batch":1,"stem":"The normal arterial [H+] concentration of about 40 nEq/L corresponds to a pH of 7.4. Which statement correctly describes the relationship between pH and [H+]?","choices":{"A":"pH and [H+] are linearly related, so equal pH changes reflect equal changes in [H+]","B":"An increase in pH corresponds directly to a proportional increase in [H+] concentration","C":"The logarithmic relationship applies to intracellular fluid but not to arterial blood","D":"Equal changes in pH do not reflect equal changes in [H+], since the relationship is logarithmic","E":"A pH change from 7.4 to 7.2 reflects a smaller change in [H+] than a pH change from 7.4 to 7.6"},"correct":"D","explanation":"pH is defined as -log10[H+], a logarithmic function of [H+], not a linear one. Because of this, a decrease in pH from 7.4 to 7.2 reflects a larger absolute change in [H+] (an increase of about 23 nEq/L) than the change produced by an equal-magnitude increase in pH from 7.4 to 7.6 (a decrease of only about 15 nEq/L).","boardPrep":"Step 1: memorize that pH 7.4 = [H+] of about 40 nEq/L; this anchor value lets you quickly sanity-check an ABG. COMLEX: the same logarithmic principle explains why a seemingly small pH change on a blood gas can reflect a clinically significant swing in acid load.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 4 — reading pH and [H+] shifts on an arterial blood gas in acid-base disorders"},{"id":"sdl21_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Logarithmic pH Relationship","batch":1,"stem":"The arterial pH range compatible with life is 6.8 to 8.0. Which range of [H+] does this correspond to, given that a pH of 7.4 corresponds to about 40 nEq/L?","choices":{"A":"About 10 to 160 nEq/L","B":"About 25 to 63 nEq/L","C":"About 16 to 100 nEq/L","D":"About 40 to 400 nEq/L","E":"About 1 to 160 nEq/L"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because pH is the negative logarithm of [H+], a pH of 6.8 corresponds to about 160 nEq/L and a pH of 8.0 to about 10 nEq/L. The pH range compatible with life, 6.8 to 8.0, therefore spans roughly a sixteen-fold range of [H+], although the pH scale itself spans only 1.2 units. The other ranges correspond to different pH limits.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pH range compatible with life is 6.8 to 8.0, while the normal arterial range is 7.35 to 7.45; convert with [H+] = 10^-pH, remembering that 7.4 is about 40 nEq/L.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 4 — reading pH and [H+] shifts on an arterial blood gas in acid-base disorders","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl21_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Logarithmic pH Relationship","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following arterial pH values would be classified as alkalemia?","choices":{"A":"7.44","B":"7.38","C":"7.32","D":"7.28","E":"7.48"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The normal arterial pH range is 7.35 to 7.45. A value above 7.45, such as 7.48, is classified as alkalemia. Values of 7.44 and 7.38 fall within the normal range, while 7.32 and 7.28 are below 7.35 and represent acidemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: know the exact normal range (7.35-7.45) and the terms acidemia (<7.35) and alkalemia (>7.45) cold — they are used to define every acid-base disorder question. COMLEX: the range compatible with life is 6.8 to 8.0, a boundary occasionally tested directly.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 4 — reading pH and [H+] shifts on an arterial blood gas in acid-base disorders"},{"id":"sdl21_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Volatile vs. Fixed Acid Kinetics","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following acids is classified as a \"volatile\" acid rather than a \"fixed\" acid?","choices":{"A":"Sulfuric acid formed from sulfur-containing amino acid catabolism, occurring continuously in protein metabolism","B":"CO2 formed from aerobic cellular respiration, occurring continuously throughout the body","C":"Phosphoric acid formed from phospholipid catabolism, occurring continuously in membrane turnover","D":"Lactic acid formed during strenuous exercise or tissue hypoxia, occurring via anaerobic glycolysis","E":"Beta-hydroxybutyric acid formed during untreated diabetes mellitus, occurring via insulin deficiency"},"correct":"B","explanation":"CO2 is the volatile acid: it is produced by aerobic metabolism at 13,000-20,000 mmol/day, combines reversibly with H2O (via carbonic anhydrase) to form H2CO3, and is regenerated and expired by the lungs, making its buffering only a temporary problem for venous blood. Sulfuric acid, phosphoric acid, lactic acid, and ketoacids are all fixed (nonvolatile) acids that must be buffered until the kidneys can excrete them.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"volatile acid\" = CO2, full stop — it is the only acid the lungs can eliminate. COMLEX: this distinction underlies why a primary respiratory problem is corrected by ventilation, while a primary metabolic (fixed-acid) problem requires renal or buffering correction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 2 — fixed acid ultimately excreted via titratable acid and ammonium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Volatile vs. Fixed Acid Kinetics","batch":1,"stem":"Catabolism of which class of molecules generates the approximately 50 mmol/day of fixed acid produced under normal metabolic conditions?","choices":{"A":"Carbohydrates metabolized via aerobic glycolysis in muscle, generating pyruvic acid","B":"Triglycerides metabolized via beta-oxidation in the liver, generating ketoacids","C":"Nucleic acids metabolized via purine catabolism, generating uric acid as waste","D":"Glycogen metabolized via glycogenolysis in the liver, generating glucose-6-phosphate","E":"Proteins containing sulfur amino acids and phospholipids, generating sulfuric and phosphoric acid"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Catabolism of proteins containing sulfur amino acids (methionine, cysteine, cystine) generates sulfuric acid, and catabolism of phospholipids generates phosphoric acid; together these normally produce about 50 mmol/day of fixed acid that must be buffered and then excreted by the kidneys. Ordinary carbohydrate, triglyceride, nucleic acid, and glycogen metabolism are not the source of this baseline fixed-acid load.","boardPrep":"Step 1: know the ~50 mmol/day fixed-acid figure and its protein/phospholipid origin — it is the baseline load the kidneys must handle even without any pathology. COMLEX: a high-protein diet increases this fixed acid load, relevant to CKD acid-base management.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 2 — fixed acid ultimately excreted via titratable acid and ammonium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Volatile vs. Fixed Acid Kinetics","batch":1,"stem":"Which fixed acids are characteristically overproduced in a patient with untreated diabetes mellitus?","choices":{"A":"Lactic acid, from anaerobic muscle metabolism during strenuous exercise","B":"Salicylic acid, from an intentional or accidental aspirin overdose ingestion","C":"Beta-hydroxybutyric acid and acetoacetic acid, from ketogenesis","D":"Formic acid, from accidental or intentional methanol ingestion","E":"Glycolic and oxalic acids, from accidental ethylene glycol ingestion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In untreated diabetes mellitus, fixed acids are overproduced in the form of the ketoacids beta-hydroxybutyric acid and acetoacetic acid. Lactic acid instead accumulates with strenuous exercise or tissue hypoxia, while salicylic, formic, glycolic, and oxalic acids are ingested toxins rather than products of diabetic metabolism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ketoacids (beta-hydroxybutyric and acetoacetic acid) are the classic DKA fixed acids and drive the high anion gap seen on labs. COMLEX: distinguishing ketoacidosis from the toxic-ingestion causes of high anion gap acidosis is a frequently tested differential.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 2 — fixed acid ultimately excreted via titratable acid and ammonium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Henderson-Hasselbalch Equation Applications","batch":1,"stem":"The pK of the HPO4-2/H2PO4- buffer pair is 6.8. Using the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation, what is the approximate ratio of the base form (HPO4-2) to the acid form (H2PO4-) at a blood pH of 7.4?","choices":{"A":"About 4 to 1, HPO4-2 to H2PO4-","B":"About 1 to 1, with equal concentrations of both forms","C":"About 1 to 4, with H2PO4- predominating over HPO4-2","D":"About 2 to 1, HPO4-2 to H2PO4-","E":"About 10 to 1, HPO4-2 to H2PO4-"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Applying pH = pK + log([A-]/[HA]) with pH 7.4 and pK 6.8 gives 0.6 = log(HPO4-2/H2PO4-), so HPO4-2/H2PO4- = 10^0.6 ≈ 3.98, or approximately 4 to 1. This demonstrates that at pH 7.4 the base form of the phosphate buffer predominates over the acid form by roughly fourfold. Equal concentrations occur only when pH equals the pK, and here pH is 0.6 units above it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: be able to run this exact Henderson-Hasselbalch calculation — it is a classic worked \"sample problem\" format. COMLEX: recognize that when pH equals a buffer’s pK, the two forms are present in equal (1:1) concentration.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 3 — urinary phosphate (HPO4-2) as a titratable-acid buffer depends on its pK relative to pH","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Henderson-Hasselbalch Equation Applications","batch":1,"stem":"Using the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation with a pK of 6.1 for the HCO3-/CO2 buffer, a plasma [HCO3-] of 24 mmol/L, and a Pco2 of 40 mm Hg (CO2 solubility 0.03 mmol/L per mm Hg), what is the calculated arterial pH?","choices":{"A":"7.1","B":"6.8","C":"7.4","D":"7.7","E":"6.1"},"correct":"C","explanation":"pH = pK + log([HCO3-]/(0.03 x Pco2)) = 6.1 + log(24/(0.03 x 40)) = 6.1 + log(24/1.2) = 6.1 + log20 = 6.1 + 1.3 = 7.4. This is the standard calculation used to derive the normal arterial pH from normal HCO3- and Pco2 values.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this exact calculation (pK 6.1, HCO3- 24, Pco2 40 -> pH 7.4) is a foundational worked example — know how to substitute values into the equation under time pressure. COMLEX: this equation underlies ABG interpretation on nearly every acid-base vignette.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 3 — the Henderson-Hasselbalch HCO3-/CO2 ratio behind the compensation formulas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Henderson-Hasselbalch Equation Applications","batch":1,"stem":"On the acid-base map, isohydric lines connect combinations of Pco2 and [HCO3-] that yield the same pH. Which combination below would also yield a normal arterial pH of 7.4, despite both values being abnormal?","choices":{"A":"Pco2 of 60 mm Hg with [HCO3-] of 24 mEq/L","B":"Pco2 of 40 mm Hg with [HCO3-] of 36 mEq/L","C":"Pco2 of 20 mm Hg with [HCO3-] of 24 mEq/L","D":"Pco2 of 60 mm Hg with [HCO3-] of 12 mEq/L","E":"Pco2 of 60 mm Hg with [HCO3-] of 36 mEq/L"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Substituting Pco2 60 and HCO3- 36 into the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation gives pH = 6.1 + log(36/(0.03x60)) = 6.1 + log(36/1.8) = 6.1 + log20 = 7.4, matching the normal value despite both variables being elevated. None of the other listed combinations satisfy the equation to yield pH 7.4.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a Pco2 of 60 mm Hg with an HCO3- of 36 mEq/L lies on the pH 7.4 isohydric line, so a normal pH alone does not prove the compensation is appropriate. COMLEX: always check whether the compensation is proportionate, not just whether pH looks normal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 4 — isohydric lines on the acid-base map help diagnose mixed acid-base disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Buffer Titration Curves and pKa Limits","batch":1,"stem":"A buffer’s titration curve is sigmoidal in shape. Within which pH range around its pK is the buffer most effective at resisting a change in pH?","choices":{"A":"Within 0.1 pH unit above or below the pK","B":"Precisely at the pK value, corresponding to a single specific pH","C":"Within 1.0 pH unit above or below the pK","D":"Within 2.0 to 3.0 pH units above or below the pK","E":"Buffering is effective across the whole pH scale if the buffer concentration is high"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The linear portion of a buffer’s sigmoidal titration curve, where small amounts of added or removed H+ produce only small pH changes, extends from about 1.0 pH unit below the pK to 1.0 pH unit above it. Outside this pK ± 1.0 range, the curve steepens and the same amount of H+ produces a much larger pH change. The pK is the midpoint of that linear range, not a single isolated pH at which buffering occurs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pK ± 1.0 rule defines the pH range over which a buffer is most effective, and phosphate (pK 6.8) sits inside it at pH 7.4; bicarbonate (pK 6.1) lies just outside it but is a major buffer because of its high concentration and its open system with CO2. COMLEX: apply the same rule when evaluating any buffer system in a lab or clinical context.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 3 — titratable acid buffering restricted by the pK of urinary buffers"},{"id":"sdl21_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Buffer Titration Curves and pKa Limits","batch":1,"stem":"Although the pK of the HPO4-2/H2PO4- buffer (6.8) is closer to blood pH (7.4) than the pK of the HCO3-/CO2 buffer (6.1), the HCO3-/CO2 system remains the more important extracellular buffer. Which two features explain this?","choices":{"A":"Phosphate has a much lower H+ affinity than bicarbonate does, and CO2 is nonvolatile","B":"HCO3- circulates at a much higher concentration than phosphate, and its CO2 form is volatile","C":"Bicarbonate functions as an intracellular buffer, and phosphate stays outside cells","D":"The phosphate titration curve rises in a straight linear pattern across the full pH range","E":"Bicarbonate’s pK rises substantially with any increase in body temperature"},"correct":"B","explanation":"HCO3- circulates at about 24 mmol/L, far exceeding plasma phosphate’s 1-2 mmol/L, and its acid form (CO2) is volatile and continuously expired by the lungs, making the system an open one that can be regenerated rather than depleted. These two features outweigh phosphate’s theoretically more favorable pK for buffering at pH 7.4. The advantage comes from concentration and volatility, not from any temperature effect on its pK.","boardPrep":"Step 1: remember both reasons — concentration AND volatility — since USMLE distractors often supply only one to make an answer seem incomplete. COMLEX: this open-system property is exactly why bicarbonate buffering is reinforced by respiratory compensation, unlike phosphate buffering.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 3 — titratable acid buffering restricted by the pK of urinary buffers"},{"id":"sdl21_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Buffer Titration Curves and pKa Limits","batch":1,"stem":"The imidazole group of histidine residues on intracellular proteins such as hemoglobin has a pK in which range, making it well suited for physiologic buffering?","choices":{"A":"3.5 to 4.5","B":"5.0 to 5.5","C":"9.0 to 10.0","D":"6.4 to 7.0","E":"1.0 to 2.0"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The imidazole group of histidine has a pK of about 6.4 to 7.0, which lies within 1.0 pH unit of the physiologic pH of 7.4 and therefore falls in the effective buffering range. The alpha-amino groups on proteins have a somewhat higher pK of 7.4 to 7.9, also within the effective range.","boardPrep":"Step 1: histidine’s imidazole pK is why hemoglobin (rich in histidine) is such an effective intracellular buffer — this links directly to the deoxyhemoglobin buffering concept covered in this objective. COMLEX: know that not all amino acid side chains buffer equally well; the pK relative to 7.4 is what matters.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 5 — histidine imidazole groups are why hemoglobin is an effective intracellular buffer"},{"id":"sdl21_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Extracellular, Intracellular, & RBC Hemoglobin Buffering","batch":1,"stem":"Deoxygenated hemoglobin (deoxyhemoglobin) is a more effective intracellular buffer than oxygenated hemoglobin (oxyhemoglobin). Which pK values account for this difference?","choices":{"A":"Oxyhemoglobin has a pK of 7.9, while deoxyhemoglobin has a lower pK of 6.7","B":"Oxyhemoglobin has a pK of 6.7, while deoxyhemoglobin has a higher pK of 7.9","C":"Both forms share an identical pK of 6.7, but deoxyhemoglobin contains more histidine residues","D":"Oxyhemoglobin has a pK of 6.1, matching that of the bicarbonate buffer system","E":"Deoxyhemoglobin’s superior buffering results from a pK of 9.5"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Oxyhemoglobin has a pK of 6.7, which is within the effective physiologic buffering range, but deoxyhemoglobin has a higher pK of 7.9, making it an even more effective buffer at physiologic pH. Both values come from conformational changes in hemoglobin’s histidine residues upon O2 release.","boardPrep":"Step 1: memorize the two pK values (oxyHb 6.7, deoxyHb 7.9) — this pairing is tested directly and also underlies why venous blood buffers its added CO2 so effectively. COMLEX: connect this to the timing of O2 release in the tissues.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 2 — hypercapnia and CO2 loading in respiratory acidosis, where hemoglobin buffering matters","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Extracellular, Intracellular, & RBC Hemoglobin Buffering","batch":1,"stem":"As blood flows through systemic capillaries, which sequence of events allows hemoglobin to buffer the H+ generated from tissue-derived CO2 particularly effectively?","choices":{"A":"Oxyhemoglobin becomes deoxyhemoglobin as CO2 entering the cell generates H+, and deoxyhemoglobin buffers more effectively","B":"Deoxyhemoglobin is converted to oxyhemoglobin as CO2 enters the red blood cell, which coincides with the more effective buffering form","C":"Histidine residues in hemoglobin keep the same pK whether oxygenated or not","D":"CO2 entering the red blood cell converts hemoglobin’s histidine residues to the imidazolium form independent of O2 binding","E":"Oxyhemoglobin buffers H+ more effectively than deoxyhemoglobin, which is why tissue O2 delivery enhances overall buffering"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In systemic capillaries, hemoglobin releases O2 to the tissues and becomes deoxyhemoglobin at the same moment that CO2 diffusing into the red blood cell is converted to H2CO3 and then H+ and HCO3-. This timing is fortunate because deoxyhemoglobin, with its higher pK of 7.9, is the more effective buffering form, so the H+ is buffered just as it is generated.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this coincidence of timing (O2 unloading and CO2 loading happening together) is exactly what keeps venous pH from falling much below arterial pH. COMLEX: this is a classic integrative physiology point linking respiratory gas exchange to acid-base buffering.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 2 — hypercapnia and CO2 loading in respiratory acidosis, where hemoglobin buffering matters","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Extracellular, Intracellular, & RBC Hemoglobin Buffering","batch":1,"stem":"A patient has an excess of a fixed acid, such as hydrochloric acid, with no accompanying organic anion. How does the excess H+ gain access to intracellular buffers?","choices":{"A":"H+ diffuses across the lipid bilayer by itself in its neutral form","B":"H+ enters together with an organic anion such as lactate","C":"H+ is generated inside the cell from CO2 that diffuses across the membrane","D":"H+ exchanges with K+ across the membrane to keep charge balance","E":"H+ is carried into the cell on plasma albumin through endocytosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"H+ must cross the cell membrane to reach intracellular buffers, and there are three routes. CO2 can diffuse in and generate H+ inside the cell, which applies to respiratory disturbances. In lactic acidosis, H+ enters with the lactate anion. When there is an excess of fixed H+ with no accompanying organic anion, H+ exchanges with K+ to preserve electroneutrality. Plasma proteins buffer H+ in the plasma but do not carry it into cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: three routes let H+ reach intracellular buffers: CO2 diffusion (respiratory), H+ with an organic anion such as lactate, and H+-K+ exchange when no organic anion accompanies the fixed H+; the last is why acid-base disturbances shift plasma K+.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 2 — hypercapnia and CO2 loading in respiratory acidosis, where hemoglobin buffering matters","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Logarithmic pH Relationship","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman with severe, prolonged vomiting develops metabolic alkalosis, and her arterial pH rises from 7.4 to 7.6. Which of the following best approximates the resulting change in her plasma [H+] concentration?","choices":{"A":"A decrease of approximately 23 nEq/L, from 40 nEq/L to about 17 nEq/L","B":"A decrease of approximately 20 nEq/L, from 40 nEq/L to about 20 nEq/L","C":"An increase of approximately 15 nEq/L, from 40 nEq/L to about 55 nEq/L","D":"No meaningful change in [H+], since the pH remains within a range compatible with life","E":"A decrease of approximately 15 nEq/L, from 40 nEq/L to about 25 nEq/L"},"correct":"E","explanation":"An increase in pH from 7.4 to 7.6 (0.2 pH units in the alkaline direction) corresponds to a decrease in [H+] of about 15 nEq/L, from the baseline 40 nEq/L down to approximately 25 nEq/L. This is a smaller absolute change than the same-magnitude shift in the acidic direction would produce.","boardPrep":"Step 1: know both anchor values (pH 7.6 -> ~25 nEq/L and pH 7.2 -> ~63 nEq/L) so you can quickly estimate [H+] from either direction of pH change on an ABG.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 1 — the logarithmic pH-[H+] relationship applied to metabolic alkalosis"},{"id":"sdl21_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Logarithmic pH Relationship","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with severe secretory diarrhea develops metabolic acidosis, and his arterial pH falls from 7.4 to 7.2. Which of the following best approximates the resulting change in his plasma [H+] concentration?","choices":{"A":"An increase of approximately 15 nEq/L, from 40 nEq/L to about 55 nEq/L","B":"An increase of approximately 30 nEq/L, from 40 nEq/L to about 70 nEq/L","C":"An increase of approximately 23 nEq/L, from 40 nEq/L to about 63 nEq/L","D":"A decrease of approximately 23 nEq/L, from 40 nEq/L to about 17 nEq/L","E":"No meaningful change in [H+], since a pH of 7.2 remains within the range compatible with life"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A decrease in pH from 7.4 to 7.2 (0.2 pH units in the acidic direction) corresponds to an increase in [H+] of about 23 nEq/L, from the baseline 40 nEq/L up to approximately 63 nEq/L. This is a larger absolute change than the same-magnitude shift in the alkaline direction would produce.","boardPrep":"Step 1: for the same 0.2 pH change, the acidic shift produces the larger absolute change in [H+] (23 versus 15 nEq/L), a frequent testing point in interpreting an ABG pH.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 1 — the logarithmic pH-[H+] relationship applied to metabolic acidosis"},{"id":"sdl21_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Logarithmic pH Relationship","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man is admitted to the intensive care unit with septic shock from a perforated colon. Blood pressure is 78/42 mm Hg despite fluids, pulse is 124/min, and respirations are 30/min. His extremities are cool and mottled. Arterial blood gas analysis shows a pH of 7.10 (reference range: 7.35–7.45), Pco2 of 30 mm Hg (reference range: 35–45 mm Hg), and bicarbonate of 9 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Given that a [H+] of about 40 nEq/L corresponds to a normal arterial pH of 7.40 (reference range: 7.35–7.45), which value is closest to his [H+]?","choices":{"A":"About 63 nEq/L","B":"About 100 nEq/L","C":"About 80 nEq/L","D":"About 126 nEq/L","E":"About 159 nEq/L"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Because [H+] = 10^-pH, a pH of 7.1 corresponds to about 80 nEq/L, roughly double the normal 40 nEq/L, even though the pH changed by only 0.3 units. The other values correspond to pH 7.2 (63), 7.0 (100), 6.9 (126), and 6.8 (159).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pH is the negative log of [H+], so a 0.3-unit fall in pH roughly doubles [H+] (7.4 is about 40 nEq/L; 7.1 is about 80 nEq/L).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 4 — reading pH and [H+] shifts on an arterial blood gas","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl21_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Volatile vs. Fixed Acid Kinetics","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes presents with polyuria, Kussmaul breathing, and a fruity breath odor after several days of missed insulin doses. Her serum bicarbonate is markedly low. Which category of acid is primarily responsible for her acidosis?","choices":{"A":"Volatile acid, from CO2 retention secondary to hypoventilation","B":"Fixed acid, from excess beta-hydroxybutyric and acetoacetic acid production","C":"Fixed acid, from excess catabolism of dietary sulfur amino acids","D":"Volatile acid, from decreased pulmonary CO2 excretion during her illness","E":"Fixed acid, from ingestion of salicylic acid"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In diabetic ketoacidosis, insulin deficiency drives excessive production of the ketoacids beta-hydroxybutyric acid and acetoacetic acid, both fixed (nonvolatile) acids that consume extracellular bicarbonate during buffering and must ultimately be excreted by the kidneys. Kussmaul breathing reflects hyperventilation (a respiratory compensation for metabolic acidosis), not a primary volatile-acid problem, and her presentation is unrelated to protein catabolism or salicylate ingestion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DKA is the prototypical fixed-acid, high anion gap metabolic acidosis — do not confuse the compensatory Kussmaul hyperventilation with a primary respiratory acid-base disorder.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 2 — fixed ketoacid production in diabetic ketoacidosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Volatile vs. Fixed Acid Kinetics","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man is brought to the emergency department several hours after intentionally ingesting antifreeze. He is found to have a severe high anion gap metabolic acidosis, an elevated osmolar gap, and calcium oxalate crystals on urine microscopy. Which fixed acid or acids are most likely responsible for his acidosis?","choices":{"A":"Formic acid, generated directly from methanol metabolism inside his liver cells","B":"Beta-hydroxybutyric acid, generated from diabetic ketoacidosis","C":"Lactic acid, generated from severe tissue hypoxia during his illness","D":"Glycolic acid and oxalic acid, generated from ethylene glycol metabolism","E":"Sulfuric acid, generated from sulfur-amino-acid catabolism"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Antifreeze ingestion is a classic source of ethylene glycol, which is metabolized to the fixed acids glycolic acid and oxalic acid. Formic acid instead comes from methanol ingestion, and the remaining choices are unrelated to this specific poisoning.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pair the toxin with its fixed acid and clinical clue — ethylene glycol -> glycolic and oxalic acid; methanol -> formic acid. COMLEX: both are classic high anion gap, high osmolar gap toxic ingestions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 2 — ingested fixed acids from ethylene glycol metabolism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Volatile vs. Fixed Acid Kinetics","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with severe COPD and chronic CO2 retention has a compensated respiratory acidosis with an elevated Pco2 and a compensatorily elevated [HCO3-]. Which statement correctly characterizes the acid responsible for his primary acid-base disturbance?","choices":{"A":"It is a volatile acid that is normally eliminated by the lungs","B":"It is a fixed acid derived from sulfur-containing amino acid catabolism in his liver","C":"It is a fixed acid that is excreted as titratable acid or ammonium by his kidneys","D":"It is produced at a fixed rate of about 50 mmol per day under normal conditions","E":"It is buffered by extracellular bicarbonate and is not eliminated through the lungs"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The acid underlying his primary disturbance is CO2, the volatile acid, which is normally regenerated from H2CO3 and expired by the lungs rather than requiring renal buffering and excretion; his chronic CO2 retention is a failure of this pulmonary elimination pathway. Fixed acids like sulfuric acid, and the ~50 mmol/day baseline fixed-acid load, are unrelated to his primary respiratory problem.","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic CO2 retention in COPD is a volatile-acid (respiratory) problem, compensated by renal HCO3- retention — do not mistake the compensatory renal response for the primary disturbance.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 2 — chronic volatile-acid retention in compensated respiratory acidosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Henderson-Hasselbalch Equation Applications","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man is intubated, sedated, and paralyzed in the intensive care unit, with his ventilator settings fixed. Before the event described, his serum bicarbonate was 24 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L) and his arterial Pco2 was 40 mm Hg (reference range: 35–45 mm Hg), corresponding to a dissolved CO2 concentration of 1.2 mmol/L (reference range: 1.05–1.35 mmol/L). He then acquires an acute fixed-acid load equivalent to adding 12 mmol of HCl to each liter of his extracellular fluid. Assuming the additional CO2 generated by this buffering reaction cannot be expired by his lungs, what is the resulting pH (pK for HCO3-/CO2 = 6.1)?","choices":{"A":"About 7.32","B":"About 6.7","C":"About 5.5","D":"About 6.06","E":"About 7.1"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Adding 12 mmol/L of H+ converts 12 mmol/L of the original 24 mmol/L HCO3- into 12 mmol/L of new CO2, leaving [HCO3-] at 12 mmol/L and raising the CO2 concentration to 1.2 + 12 = 13.2 mmol/L. Substituting into the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation gives pH = 6.1 + log(12/13.2) = 6.1 - 0.04 = 6.06, a value that would be fatal without further compensation. About 7.1 is what results if the lungs could expire the new CO2, keeping it at 1.2 mmol/L, while about 7.32, 6.7 and 5.5 do not follow from 12 mmol/L of bicarbonate over 13.2 mmol/L of CO2.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this worked example demonstrates why buffering alone is insufficient without respiratory compensation — hyperventilation that lowers Pco2 to about 24 mm Hg restores the pH to about 7.32.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 3 — Henderson-Hasselbalch calculation of pH before respiratory compensation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Henderson-Hasselbalch Equation Applications","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old man with type 1 diabetes mellitus is brought to the emergency department because of vomiting and abdominal pain after missing several insulin doses. He is breathing deeply and rapidly at 32/min. Blood pressure is 102/64 mm Hg. Serum glucose is 480 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL). After buffering of the acute acid load, his serum bicarbonate is 12 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). His hyperventilation has driven his arterial Pco2 down to 24 mm Hg (reference range: 35–45 mm Hg), corresponding to a dissolved CO2 concentration of 0.72 mmol/L (reference range: 1.05–1.35 mmol/L). What is his calculated pH (pK 6.1)?","choices":{"A":"About 6.06","B":"About 7.4","C":"About 6.8","D":"About 7.6","E":"About 7.32"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Substituting the compensated values into the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation gives pH = 6.1 + log(12/0.72) = 6.1 + log(16.7) = 6.1 + 1.22 = 7.32. This is nearly normal despite both [HCO3-] and Pco2 being far below their normal values, illustrating how hyperventilation (respiratory compensation) protects pH after an acute fixed-acid load. About 6.06 is the uncompensated value with the new CO2 retained, 7.4 would need a bicarbonate-to-CO2 ratio of 20 rather than about 16.7, and 6.8 and 7.6 do not follow from these values.","boardPrep":"Step 1: if the CO2 generated by buffering could not be exhaled, pH would fall to about 6.06, which is fatal, but hyperventilation restores a survivable pH of 7.32 even though full correction still requires the kidneys to regenerate the consumed HCO3-.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 3 — Henderson-Hasselbalch calculation of pH after respiratory compensation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Henderson-Hasselbalch Equation Applications","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man with severe, long-standing COPD has a chronic Pco2 of 60 mm Hg and a compensatorily elevated [HCO3-] of 30 mEq/L. His arterial pH is about 7.32. Which principle best explains why his pH is only mildly decreased despite both values being far from their normal set points?","choices":{"A":"Renal HCO3- retention restores the HCO3-/CO2 ratio partway, limiting the pH fall","B":"His kidneys have reabsorbed filtered bicarbonate at a rate independent of his elevated Pco2","C":"The pK of the HCO3-/CO2 buffer decreases as Pco2 rises, falling to a value near 5.5","D":"His hemoglobin has converted to the oxyhemoglobin form, maximizing its buffering capacity","E":"The additional CO2 is converted to lactic acid within his red blood cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because pH depends on the ratio of [HCO3-] to Pco2 rather than on their absolute values, chronic renal HCO3- retention raises [HCO3-] by 0.3 mEq/L for each 1 mm Hg rise in Pco2, so [HCO3-] climbs from 24 to about 30 mEq/L as Pco2 rises from 40 to 60 mm Hg; then pH = 6.1 + log(30/(0.03 x 60)) = 6.1 + log(16.7) = 7.32, only mildly acidic. This chronic renal compensation is distinct from the buffer’s pK, hemoglobin oxygenation state, or lactic acid production, none of which are the mechanism here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in chronic respiratory acidosis, HCO3- rises about 0.3 mEq/L per 1 mm Hg rise in Pco2 (Costanzo; BRS and the course notes give 0.35 to 0.4), so Pco2 60 predicts HCO3- near 30 to 32 and a mildly acidic pH; a bicarbonate well above the predicted value suggests a coexisting metabolic alkalosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 3 — Henderson-Hasselbalch ratio applied to chronic compensated respiratory acidosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Buffer Titration Curves and pKa Limits","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man enrolls as a healthy volunteer in a phase 1 trial of an investigational intravenous buffering agent intended for metabolic acidosis. Blood pressure is 122/78 mm Hg. Arterial pH is 7.40 (reference range: 7.35–7.45), and serum bicarbonate is 24 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). The agent is a weak acid buffer with a pK of 5.5. Based on the principle that effective physiologic buffering occurs within 1.0 pH unit of a buffer’s pK, would this agent contribute meaningfully to buffering his blood at his current arterial pH?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because pH 7.4 lies within its effective buffering range of 4.5 to 6.5","B":"No, because pH 7.4 lies outside its effective buffering range of 4.5 to 6.5","C":"Yes, because weak acid buffers are generally effective across the physiologic pH range","D":"No, because its effective range is 6.5 to 8.5, and 7.4 falls just outside it","E":"Yes, because a pK of 5.5 is closer to 7.4 than the pK of the bicarbonate buffer"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The effective buffering range of a buffer with pK 5.5 extends from 4.5 to 6.5 (pK ± 1.0). Since 7.4 lies well outside this range, this buffer would contribute little to buffering blood at physiologic pH unless it were present at a very high concentration.","boardPrep":"Step 1: apply the pK ± 1.0 rule mechanically to any given pK value — this is a common way boards test the concept with an unfamiliar or hypothetical buffer rather than bicarbonate or phosphate.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 4 — a buffer works best within 1.0 pH unit of its pK"},{"id":"sdl21_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Buffer Titration Curves and pKa Limits","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is admitted because of increasing dyspnea and somnolence. Respirations are 10/min and shallow. Arterial blood gas analysis shows a pH of 7.25 (reference range: 7.35–7.45), Pco2 of 75 mm Hg (reference range: 35–45 mm Hg), and HCO3- of 32 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Much of the H+ generated from his retained CO2 is buffered inside cells, in part by organic phosphates such as ATP, ADP, and 2,3-diphosphoglycerate. Which of the following best explains why these organic phosphates serve as effective intracellular buffers?","choices":{"A":"Their pKs range from about 6.0 to 7.5, which lies close to the physiologic pH range","B":"Their pKs range from 9.0 to 10.0, providing buffering at markedly alkaline pH","C":"They exist in an ionized, non-buffering form at physiologic pH","D":"They function as buffers after first converting to inorganic phosphate","E":"Their intracellular concentration is too low to contribute to buffering"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Organic phosphates such as ATP, ADP, AMP, glucose-1-phosphate, and 2,3-diphosphoglycerate have pKs ranging from about 6.0 to 7.5, placing them well within the pK ± 1.0 range needed for effective buffering at physiologic intracellular pH.","boardPrep":"Step 1: organic phosphates are a less commonly emphasized but real intracellular buffer class — know they buffer via the same pK-proximity principle as bicarbonate and phosphate, just intracellularly.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 4 — organic phosphates buffer intracellularly because their pKs sit near physiologic pH"},{"id":"sdl21_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Buffer Titration Curves and pKa Limits","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man is scheduled for coronary artery bypass surgery. Blood pressure is 136/84 mm Hg, and his serum creatinine is 1.0 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). The perfusion team is preparing a solution for his cardiopulmonary bypass circuit that will be held at a pH of 7.40 (reference range for arterial blood: 7.35–7.45). They can add one of two buffers at similar concentrations: one with a pK of 7.0 and another with a pK of 8.6. Which of the following best describes which buffer would be more effective at this pH?","choices":{"A":"The pK 8.6 buffer, because a higher pK gives greater buffering capacity at any solution pH","B":"Both buffers are equally effective, since effectiveness does not depend on the relationship between pH and pK","C":"The buffer with pK 7.0, because it lies within 1.0 pH unit of 7.4, while the pK 8.6 buffer does not","D":"The pK 8.6 buffer, because its titration curve is linear across the entire pH scale","E":"Neither buffer is effective, since neither pK exactly equals 7.4"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The buffer with pK 7.0 is only 0.4 pH units from 7.4, placing it well within the pK ± 1.0 effective buffering range, while the pK 8.6 buffer is 1.2 pH units away, placing it just outside that range. The pK 7.0 buffer would therefore be the more effective one at pH 7.4.","boardPrep":"Step 1: distance from the solution pH to the pK, not the absolute value of the pK, determines buffering effectiveness — a common trap is assuming a \"higher\" or \"lower\" pK is inherently better or worse.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 4 — distance from pH to pK, not the pK value itself, sets buffering effectiveness"},{"id":"sdl21_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Extracellular, Intracellular, & RBC Hemoglobin Buffering","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old woman having an acute panic attack hyperventilates and develops perioral tingling and carpopedal spasm. Arterial blood gas shows a pH of 7.55. Which mechanism best explains her neuromuscular symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Increased free H+ displaces Ca2+ from plasma proteins, raising ionized Ca2+ and excitability","B":"Decreased free H+ lets more Ca2+ bind plasma proteins, lowering ionized Ca2+ and raising excitability","C":"Hyperventilation directly increases her renal Ca2+ excretion, producing hypocalcemia within minutes","D":"Respiratory alkalosis rapidly decreases bone calcium release independent of parathyroid hormone","E":"The low Pco2 directly binds free Ca2+ in her plasma, removing it from the ionized pool"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Her acute hyperventilation produces respiratory alkalosis (pH 7.55); the resulting deficit of free H+ allows more Ca2+ to bind to plasma proteins such as albumin, lowering free ionized Ca2+ and making the threshold potential of nerve and muscle more negative, which increases excitability and produces perioral tingling and carpopedal spasm. This is a functional, protein-binding-related hypocalcemia rather than a true loss of total body calcium through the kidneys or bone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this is the classic mechanism behind acute hyperventilation-induced tetany — a rapid, reversible drop in ionized (not total) calcium from H+/Ca2+ competition for albumin binding sites.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 5 — H+/Ca2+ plasma protein binding competition in acute respiratory alkalosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Extracellular, Intracellular, & RBC Hemoglobin Buffering","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after a prolonged generalized seizure. His lactic acid level is markedly elevated. Which mechanism allows the H+ generated by this fixed acid to enter cells for intracellular buffering?","choices":{"A":"H+ freely diffuses into the cell unaccompanied, with K+ subsequently exiting to preserve electroneutrality","B":"Lactate is first converted to CO2, which then crosses the cell membrane independent of H+","C":"H+ enters the cell together with the lactate anion, preserving electroneutrality","D":"H+ exchanges directly for intracellular Na+ across the plasma membrane","E":"Lactic acid stays outside cells and is buffered by extracellular bicarbonate"},"correct":"C","explanation":"When excess fixed acid is accompanied by an organic anion such as lactate, H+ and the anion enter the cell together, preserving electroneutrality without requiring an exchange for another intracellular cation. This differs from the H+/K+ exchange mechanism used when fixed acid accumulates without an accompanying organic anion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: contrast this organic-anion cotransport mechanism with the H+/K+ exchange mechanism used for acids like HCl — the presence of an organic anion (lactate, ketoacids) is why organic acidoses cause less hyperkalemia than inorganic mineral acidoses.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 5 — intracellular buffering of fixed acid accompanied by an organic anion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl21_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Extracellular, Intracellular, & RBC Hemoglobin Buffering","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department after taking a large number of her oxycodone tablets. She is difficult to arouse, and her pupils are pinpoint. Respirations are 6/min and shallow. Arterial blood gas analysis shows a pH of 7.22 (reference range: 7.35–7.45), Pco2 of 70 mm Hg (reference range: 35–45 mm Hg), and bicarbonate of 27 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Which statement correctly describes where the excess H+ from her retained CO2 is taken up?","choices":{"A":"Extracellular HCO3- takes up the H+ because CO2 stays outside cells","B":"Plasma proteins bind the H+ in the plasma and intracellular pH stays fixed","C":"Renal H+ excretion removes the excess within minutes of the CO2 rise","D":"Hemoglobin releases bound H+ into the plasma and lowers the CO2 load","E":"CO2 enters cells and its H+ binds proteins and organic phosphates"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In respiratory acidosis, buffering of the excess CO2 occurs in the ICF, especially in red blood cells. CO2 is lipid soluble and diffuses across cell membranes, where it is converted to H+ and HCO3-, and the H+ is buffered by intracellular proteins such as hemoglobin and by organic phosphates. Renal compensation is a slower process, and HCO3- does not buffer the H+ made from CO2.","boardPrep":"Step 1: respiratory acid-base disturbances are buffered in the ICF, because CO2 crosses cell membranes and HCO3- cannot buffer its own acid; renal compensation follows more slowly.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 2 — buffering and renal compensation in primary respiratory disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex2t_sdl21_q1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Henderson-Hasselbalch Equation Applications","batch":3,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with a COPD exacerbation has an arterial pH of 7.33 (reference range: 7.35–7.45), PaCO2 of 60 mm Hg (reference range: 33–45 mm Hg), and bicarbonate of 31 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Using the Henderson-Hasselbalch relationship, the ratio of bicarbonate to dissolved carbon dioxide is closest to which value?","choices":{"A":"About 20 to 1","B":"About 17 to 1","C":"About 24 to 1","D":"About 12 to 1","E":"About 31 to 1"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Dissolved carbon dioxide equals 0.03 times the PaCO2, so 0.03 x 60 = 1.8 mmol/L. The ratio of bicarbonate to dissolved carbon dioxide is 31 divided by 1.8, about 17 to 1. With a pK of 6.1, pH = 6.1 + log 17, which is about 6.1 + 1.23 = 7.33, matching the measured value. A ratio of 20 to 1 corresponds to a pH of 7.40, the normal value, which this patient does not have. A ratio of 24 to 1 would give an alkalemic pH. A ratio of 12 to 1 would give a pH near 7.18. A ratio of 31 to 1 simply restates the bicarbonate value and ignores the dissolved carbon dioxide denominator. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 21, Henderson-Hasselbalch applications); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 7 (Henderson-Hasselbalch equation for the bicarbonate buffer).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pH depends on the ratio of bicarbonate to 0.03 times PaCO2, and a 20 to 1 ratio gives pH 7.40. COMLEX: chronic respiratory acidosis with renal compensation narrows the fall in pH by raising bicarbonate.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 4 — the same equation lets you check whether ABG values are internally consistent","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex2t_sdl21_q2","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Volatile vs. Fixed Acid Kinetics","batch":3,"stem":"A healthy 28-year-old man eats a diet rich in methionine and cysteine. His daily net acid excretion rises, although his arterial PaCO2 of 40 mm Hg (reference range: 33–45 mm Hg) is unchanged. Which acid accounts for the rise in renal acid excretion?","choices":{"A":"Carbonic acid from oxidative metabolism","B":"Lactic acid from anaerobic glycolysis","C":"Acetoacetic acid from fatty acid oxidation","D":"Formic acid from ingested methanol","E":"Sulfuric acid from protein catabolism"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Catabolism of sulfur-containing amino acids such as methionine and cysteine produces sulfuric acid, a fixed (nonvolatile) acid that the lungs do not remove. Its H+ is first buffered by extracellular bicarbonate, and the kidney then excretes the H+ as titratable acid and ammonium, generating new bicarbonate. Carbonic acid from oxidative metabolism is volatile and is eliminated by the lungs as carbon dioxide, consistent with the unchanged PaCO2. Lactic acid and acetoacetic acid are fixed acids, but a healthy person eating this diet does not generate them in meaningful amounts. Formic acid comes from methanol poisoning, which is not part of this diet. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 21, volatile vs. fixed acids); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 7 (fixed acid production from protein catabolism).","boardPrep":"Step 1: volatile acid (carbon dioxide) is excreted by the lungs, whereas fixed acids such as sulfuric and phosphoric acid require renal excretion. COMLEX: a high-protein diet raises daily fixed acid load and net acid excretion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 2 — fixed acid is ultimately excreted as titratable acid and ammonium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex2t_sdl21_q3","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Extracellular, Intracellular, & RBC Hemoglobin Buffering","batch":3,"stem":"A 24-year-old man is studied during moderate exercise. Simultaneous samples show an arterial pH of 7.40 (reference range: 7.35–7.45) and a mixed venous pH of 7.36 (reference range: 7.31–7.41) despite a large venous carbon dioxide load. Which property of deoxyhemoglobin limits the fall in venous pH?","choices":{"A":"A lower oxygen affinity that releases protons","B":"A greater chloride affinity that traps bicarbonate","C":"A larger number of histidine residues than oxyhemoglobin","D":"A higher pK of its histidine imidazole groups","E":"A faster carbonic anhydrase turnover in the red cell"},"correct":"D","explanation":"As hemoglobin releases oxygen in the tissues, its imidazole groups shift from a pK of about 6.7 in oxyhemoglobin to about 7.9 in deoxyhemoglobin. The higher pK makes deoxyhemoglobin a better proton acceptor at physiologic pH, so it binds the H+ formed from carbon dioxide inside red cells and the venous pH falls only slightly. Deoxyhemoglobin does have lower oxygen affinity, but it binds rather than releases protons (the Haldane effect). Chloride shift moves chloride into red cells in exchange for bicarbonate, but it does not trap bicarbonate through a change in hemoglobin affinity. Oxyhemoglobin and deoxyhemoglobin have the same number of histidine residues; only their pK differs. Carbonic anhydrase speeds carbon dioxide hydration and would create more H+, not buffer it. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 21, red cell hemoglobin buffering); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 7 (pK of oxyhemoglobin and deoxyhemoglobin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: deoxyhemoglobin is the better buffer, which is the Haldane effect: unloading oxygen helps the blood carry carbon dioxide and H+. COMLEX: this coupling keeps venous pH close to arterial pH during exercise.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 2 — hemoglobin buffering matters most when carbon dioxide accumulates in respiratory acidosis","isHighYield":true}],"22":[{"id":"sdl22_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Proximal HCO3- Reclamation Cellular Mechanics","batch":1,"stem":"Reabsorption of filtered HCO3- in the early proximal tubule begins with H+ secretion into the tubular lumen by which transporter?","choices":{"A":"The H+-ATPase on the luminal membrane","B":"The Na+-HCO3- cotransporter on the basolateral membrane","C":"The Cl--HCO3- exchanger on the basolateral membrane","D":"The H+-K+-ATPase on the luminal membrane","E":"The Na+-H+ exchanger NHE3 on the luminal membrane"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The luminal membrane of the early proximal tubule contains the Na+-H+ exchanger (NHE3), a secondary active transporter that moves Na+ into the cell down its electrochemical gradient while secreting H+ into the lumen against its gradient, and NHE3 accounts for most proximal H+ secretion. The H+-ATPase and H+-K+-ATPase are instead the main H+ secretors of collecting duct alpha-intercalated cells, and the basolateral transporters listed are responsible for HCO3- exit from the cell, not H+ entry into the lumen.","boardPrep":"Step 1: NHE3 is the workhorse of proximal HCO3- reclamation, and impaired proximal HCO3- reabsorption underlies proximal (Type 2) RTA. COMLEX: this transporter is distinct from the aldosterone-stimulated H+-ATPase of the collecting duct.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 1 — proximal tubule glutamine metabolism also generates new HCO3-","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Proximal HCO3- Reclamation Cellular Mechanics","batch":1,"stem":"With a GFR of 180 L/day and a plasma [HCO3-] of 24 mEq/L, the filtered load of HCO3- is 4320 mEq/day. If urinary HCO3- excretion is only 2 mEq/day, approximately what percentage of the filtered HCO3- is reabsorbed?","choices":{"A":"Approximately 95%","B":"Approximately 90%","C":"Approximately 80%","D":"More than 99.9%","E":"Approximately 50%"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Reabsorption is calculated as filtered load minus excretion: 4320 - 2 = 4318 mEq/day reabsorbed, which is 4318/4320 ≈ 99.95%, or more than 99.9% of the filtered load. This near-complete reabsorption ensures that the major extracellular buffer is conserved rather than lost in the urine. Reabsorbing only about 95%, 90%, 80% or 50% would mean excreting roughly 216, 432, 864 or 2160 mEq/day, far more than the 2 mEq/day measured.","boardPrep":"Step 1: know this exact worked calculation cold — it is the standard way boards illustrate that HCO3- reabsorption, unlike many other filtered solutes, is essentially complete under normal conditions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 1 — a lowered renal threshold for HCO3- reabsorption underlies Type 2 RTA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Proximal HCO3- Reclamation Cellular Mechanics","batch":1,"stem":"According to Costanzo, above approximately what plasma [HCO3-] concentration does the filtered load of HCO3- exceed the proximal tubule’s reabsorptive capacity, causing HCO3- to appear in the urine?","choices":{"A":"24 mEq/L","B":"30 mEq/L","C":"40 mEq/L","D":"55 mEq/L","E":"15 mEq/L"},"correct":"C","explanation":"When plasma [HCO3-] rises above about 40 mEq/L, the filtered load becomes high enough to saturate the proximal reabsorption mechanism, and any additional filtered HCO3- is excreted in the urine (the Exam 3 course notes instead describe the normal renal threshold as about 24 mEq/L). This is the mechanism by which the kidney can correct metabolic alkalosis: as plasma HCO3- rises, filtered load eventually exceeds reabsorptive capacity and the excess HCO3- is excreted.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this saturation threshold explains why severe metabolic alkalosis is partially self-limiting once plasma HCO3- climbs high enough. COMLEX: relevant to understanding why correcting the underlying cause (e.g., volume contraction) is still required for full resolution.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 2 — renal maintenance of metabolic alkalosis despite an elevated plasma HCO3-","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Collecting Duct Alpha-Intercalated Cell Mechanics","batch":1,"stem":"Excretion of H+ as titratable acid occurs primarily in which cell type, and which hormone stimulates the responsible luminal transporter?","choices":{"A":"Alpha-intercalated cells of the distal tubule and collecting duct, stimulated by aldosterone","B":"Principal cells of the collecting duct, stimulated directly by aldosterone","C":"Alpha-intercalated cells of the proximal tubule, stimulated by angiotensin II","D":"Beta-intercalated cells of the collecting duct, stimulated similarly by aldosterone","E":"Alpha-intercalated cells of the late distal tubule, stimulated by ADH"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Titratable acid is excreted primarily by alpha-intercalated cells of the late distal tubule and collecting ducts, where aldosterone stimulates the luminal H+-ATPase (in addition to its actions on principal cells). Principal cells handle Na+ reabsorption and K+ secretion rather than H+ secretion, and alpha-intercalated cells are not present in the proximal tubule.","boardPrep":"Step 1: aldosterone has a dual renal action — stimulating principal cell Na+/K+ handling AND alpha-intercalated cell H+ secretion — a frequently tested integrative point.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 2 — collecting duct handling of both titratable acid and ammonium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Collecting Duct Alpha-Intercalated Cell Mechanics","batch":1,"stem":"The H+ secreted into the collecting duct lumen by the H+-ATPase is generated inside the alpha-intercalated cell from which reaction?","choices":{"A":"Glutamine metabolism by mitochondrial glutaminase","B":"CO2 and H2O forming H2CO3 that dissociates into H+ and HCO3-","C":"Ammonia diffusing from the interstitium into the cell","D":"Direct transport of H+ from the peritubular capillary blood","E":"Dissociation of filtered titratable acid within the lumen"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Inside the alpha-intercalated cell, CO2 and H2O combine (via intracellular carbonic anhydrase) to form H2CO3, which dissociates into H+ (secreted into the lumen by the H+-ATPase) and HCO3- (reabsorbed into the blood via basolateral Cl--HCO3- exchange). Glutamine metabolism and ammonia handling instead underlie the separate NH4+ excretion pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this is the same CO2/H2O -> H2CO3 -> H+ + HCO3- reaction used in the proximal tubule, just paired with different luminal (H+-ATPase vs. NHE3) and basolateral (Cl--HCO3- exchange vs. Na+-HCO3- cotransport) transporters.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 2 — collecting duct handling of both titratable acid and ammonium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Collecting Duct Alpha-Intercalated Cell Mechanics","batch":1,"stem":"For each H+ excreted as titratable acid in the collecting duct, what happens to systemic HCO3- stores?","choices":{"A":"One HCO3- is filtered and excreted alongside the titratable acid","B":"HCO3- stores decrease further, since titratable acid excretion consumes additional HCO3-","C":"HCO3- stores remain unchanged, since titratable acid excretion does not involve HCO3-","D":"One new HCO3- is synthesized within the cell and reabsorbed into the blood","E":"Two new HCO3- molecules are synthesized per titratable acid molecule excreted"},"correct":"D","explanation":"For each H+ excreted as titratable acid, one new HCO3- is synthesized within the alpha-intercalated cell and reabsorbed into the blood, replenishing the HCO3- stores that were previously depleted while buffering fixed acid. This 1:1 relationship, not 2:1 or 0:1, is what allows ongoing titratable acid and NH4+ excretion to fully restore ECF HCO3- over time.","boardPrep":"Step 1: memorize the 1:1 ratio of titratable acid H+ excreted to new HCO3- generated — this is what makes the kidney capable of fully replacing bicarbonate consumed by fixed-acid buffering.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 2 — collecting duct handling of both titratable acid and ammonium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — The 4.4 Minimum Urine pH Barrier & Buffer Kinetics","batch":1,"stem":"The minimum achievable urine pH is 4.4. Approximately how many times higher is the [H+] concentration in this maximally acidified urine compared with blood at pH 7.4?","choices":{"A":"1000-fold higher","B":"10-fold higher","C":"100-fold higher","D":"10,000-fold higher","E":"3-fold higher"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A urine pH of 4.4 versus a blood pH of 7.4 represents a difference of 3.0 pH units, and because pH is a negative log10 scale, each whole pH unit corresponds to a tenfold change in [H+]; 3 pH units therefore corresponds to a 10^3, or 1000-fold, higher [H+] concentration in the urine. This is the largest concentration gradient against which the H+-ATPase can secrete H+.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the 1000-fold figure is a direct application of the logarithmic pH scale — a good check that you understand both the acid-base foundations objective and this urine pH barrier concept together.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 1 — the logarithmic pH scale and why equal pH changes are not linear shifts in [H+]"},{"id":"sdl22_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — The 4.4 Minimum Urine pH Barrier & Buffer Kinetics","batch":1,"stem":"Two patients both have a urine pH of 4.4, but patient A has abundant urinary phosphate while patient B has very little. Which statement correctly compares their total H+ excretion as titratable acid?","choices":{"A":"Both patients excrete an identical amount of H+ as titratable acid, since their urine pH is the same","B":"Patient B excretes more H+ as titratable acid, since less buffer allows the lower pH to be reached faster","C":"Patient A excretes much more H+ as titratable acid, since he has more buffer available","D":"Neither patient excretes any H+ as titratable acid once urine pH reaches 4.4","E":"Patient A excretes less H+ as titratable acid, since more buffer slows the rate of H+ secretion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The amount of H+ excreted as titratable acid depends on the amount of urinary buffer available, not on the final urine pH value itself. Patient A, with abundant phosphate buffer, can have large quantities of H+ secreted and buffered before urine pH falls to 4.4, while patient B, with little buffer, reaches the same pH after excreting far less total H+.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this distinction between the AMOUNT of H+ excreted and the urine pH VALUE is a classic conceptual trap — urine pH alone never tells you total acid excretion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 4 — titratable acid excretion limited by the pK of urinary buffers"},{"id":"sdl22_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — The 4.4 Minimum Urine pH Barrier & Buffer Kinetics","batch":1,"stem":"The phosphate buffer (pK 6.8) has a titration curve with a linear portion extending from about pH 5.8 to 7.8. Within this linear range, what happens to tubular fluid pH as H+ is progressively secreted into it?","choices":{"A":"The pH decreases precipitously with even small amounts of secreted H+","B":"The pH falls gradually as the added H+ is buffered","C":"The pH stays constant however much H+ is secreted","D":"The pH increases, since H+ secretion converts H2PO4- back to HPO4-2","E":"The pH immediately falls to 4.4 as soon as H+ secretion begins"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Within the linear portion of the phosphate titration curve (pH 5.8 to 7.8), added H+ converts HPO4-2 to H2PO4- while producing only a modest decrease in pH; only after most of the HPO4-2 has been converted does further H+ secretion cause the pH to fall precipitously toward the 4.4 minimum.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this is the direct clinical application of the general titration-curve/pK ± 1.0 rule to phosphate specifically inside the nephron.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 4 — titratable acid excretion limited by the pK of urinary buffers"},{"id":"sdl22_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Proximal HCO3- Reclamation Cellular Mechanics","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with heart failure is treated with a loop diuretic and develops mild metabolic alkalosis. Which mechanism, triggered by her ECF volume contraction, directly increases proximal HCO3- reabsorption and contributes to this contraction alkalosis?","choices":{"A":"Aldosterone stimulation of H+-ATPase in alpha-intercalated cells","B":"Increased filtered load of HCO3- from a rising plasma concentration","C":"Decreased Pco2 reducing the intracellular CO2 supply for H+ generation","D":"Angiotensin II stimulation of the luminal Na+-H+ exchanger","E":"Direct inhibition of carbonic anhydrase by the loop diuretic"},"correct":"D","explanation":"ECF volume contraction activates the renin-angiotensin II-aldosterone system, and angiotensin II directly stimulates the proximal tubule Na+-H+ exchanger, thereby increasing HCO3- reabsorption and raising blood HCO3- concentration — the mechanism underlying contraction alkalosis from loop or thiazide diuretics or vomiting. The alpha-intercalated cell H+-ATPase and Pco2-driven CO2 supply are separate mechanisms not primarily responsible for this diuretic-triggered effect, and loop diuretics do not act by inhibiting carbonic anhydrase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"contraction alkalosis\" is a named, testable entity — know both mechanisms (Starling-force-driven proximal reabsorption AND angiotensin II-driven NHE3 stimulation) and that it is corrected with isotonic NaCl.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 4 — contraction alkalosis from volume depletion activating the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Proximal HCO3- Reclamation Cellular Mechanics","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with severe COPD has chronic CO2 retention and a compensatorily elevated plasma [HCO3-]. Which mechanism directly links his elevated Pco2 to increased renal HCO3- reabsorption?","choices":{"A":"Increased Pco2 raises the CO2 supply for H+ generation by the Na+-H+ exchanger","B":"Increased Pco2 directly increases the number of Na+-HCO3- cotransporters on the basolateral membrane","C":"Increased Pco2 stimulates aldosterone release to increase proximal H+-ATPase activity","D":"Increased Pco2 raises the filtered load of HCO3- and mechanically increases its reabsorption","E":"Increased Pco2 inhibits angiotensin II release and reduces proximal Na+ reabsorption"},"correct":"A","explanation":"One proposed mechanism is that a chronically elevated Pco2 increases the supply of CO2 available to proximal tubule cells, providing more substrate to generate the H+ secreted by the Na+-H+ exchanger, which in turn increases HCO3- reabsorption and raises plasma [HCO3-] as a compensatory response. This is distinct from any direct change in transporter number, aldosterone-mediated signaling, filtered load, or angiotensin II suppression.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this CO2-substrate mechanism is the physiologic basis for chronic renal compensation of respiratory acidosis, which takes days (unlike the immediate respiratory compensation for metabolic disorders).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 3 — the chronic respiratory acidosis delta rule predicting the compensatory rise in HCO3-","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Proximal HCO3- Reclamation Cellular Mechanics","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old man is planning a trek to 4,500 m (14,800 ft) and asks for prophylaxis against altitude sickness. He is healthy, and his blood pressure is 118/76 mm Hg. Serum bicarbonate is 25 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L), and creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). He is prescribed a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor. Which direct effect on proximal tubule HCO3- handling would be expected?","choices":{"A":"Increased HCO3- reabsorption, since carbonic anhydrase inhibition preserves intracellular HCO3-","B":"Decreased HCO3- reabsorption, since carbonic anhydrase transports HCO3- across the basolateral membrane","C":"No change in HCO3- reabsorption, since carbonic anhydrase is limited to red blood cells","D":"Decreased HCO3- reabsorption, since brush border carbonic anhydrase converts luminal H2CO3 to CO2 and H2O","E":"Increased HCO3- reabsorption, since carbonic anhydrase inhibition upregulates the Na+-H+ exchanger"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Acetazolamide inhibits carbonic anhydrase, including the brush border enzyme needed to convert luminal H2CO3 into CO2 and H2O so that they can diffuse into the proximal tubule cell; blocking this step interferes with HCO3- reabsorption and produces a mild diuresis with bicarbonaturia. Carbonic anhydrase does not transport HCO3- directly and is not confined to red blood cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: acetazolamide’s mechanism (brush border carbonic anhydrase inhibition -> decreased proximal HCO3- reabsorption) is a classic pharmacology-physiology integration question.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 3 — carbonic anhydrase inhibitors causing bicarbonaturia by blocking proximal HCO3- reclamation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Collecting Duct Alpha-Intercalated Cell Mechanics","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with primary hyperaldosteronism (Conn syndrome) has excess aldosterone secretion. Which acid-base disturbance would be expected from its effect on alpha-intercalated cells?","choices":{"A":"Metabolic acidosis, from aldosterone-induced suppression of the H+-ATPase","B":"Metabolic alkalosis, from aldosterone-induced inhibition of proximal tubule HCO3- reabsorption","C":"Metabolic alkalosis, from aldosterone-stimulated overactivity of the luminal H+-ATPase","D":"Respiratory alkalosis, from aldosterone-stimulated hyperventilation","E":"No acid-base disturbance, since aldosterone action is restricted to principal cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Excess aldosterone in Conn syndrome overstimulates the H+-ATPase in alpha-intercalated cells, increasing H+ secretion (and new HCO3- generation) beyond what is needed, producing metabolic alkalosis. Aldosterone does not suppress the H+-ATPase or inhibit proximal HCO3- reabsorption, has no direct respiratory effect, and acts on alpha-intercalated cells in addition to principal cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: primary hyperaldosteronism classically presents with hypertension, hypokalemia, AND metabolic alkalosis — this alpha-intercalated cell mechanism explains the alkalosis component.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 2 — mineralocorticoid excess as a saline-unresponsive cause of metabolic alkalosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Collecting Duct Alpha-Intercalated Cell Mechanics","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with severe COPD has chronic CO2 retention. Which change in alpha-intercalated cell function contributes to his renal compensation?","choices":{"A":"Decreased Pco2 reduces intracellular CO2 supply, decreasing H+ secretion and new HCO3- synthesis","B":"Increased Pco2 directly stimulates aldosterone release, independent of CO2 supply","C":"Increased Pco2 inhibits H+-ATPase activity, substantially reducing titratable acid excretion","D":"Increased Pco2 raises intracellular CO2 supply, increasing H+ secretion and new HCO3- synthesis","E":"Increased Pco2 has essentially no effect on alpha-intercalated cell H+ secretion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"One proposed mechanism is that in respiratory acidosis the elevated Pco2 increases the CO2 supply available within alpha-intercalated cells, increasing H+ secretion and the accompanying synthesis and reabsorption of new HCO3-, which contributes to the compensatory rise in plasma [HCO3-]. This mirrors the same CO2-supply mechanism operating in proximal tubule cells for the same disorder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this parallels the proximal tubule Pco2 mechanism — both nephron segments increase HCO3- generation in response to chronic hypercapnia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 3 — delayed renal tubular compensation during chronic respiratory acidosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Collecting Duct Alpha-Intercalated Cell Mechanics","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison disease) has aldosterone deficiency. Which effect on his alpha-intercalated cell H+ secretion and acid-base status would be expected?","choices":{"A":"Increased H+-ATPase activity, contributing to metabolic alkalosis","B":"Decreased H+-ATPase activity, contributing to metabolic acidosis","C":"No change in H+-ATPase activity, since Pco2 is its main regulator","D":"Decreased H+-K+-ATPase activity, contributing to metabolic alkalosis","E":"Increased Cl--HCO3- exchange, contributing to metabolic acidosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Aldosterone deficiency in Addison disease removes a key stimulus for the alpha-intercalated cell H+-ATPase, decreasing H+ secretion and new HCO3- synthesis and contributing to a hyperkalemic metabolic acidosis (alongside the loss of aldosterone’s effect on principal cell K+ secretion). H+-ATPase activity is not regulated solely by Pco2, and the direction of the acid-base change is acidosis, not alkalosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Addison disease produces hyperkalemia AND metabolic acidosis together — the mirror image of Conn syndrome’s hypokalemia and alkalosis, both explained by the same alpha-intercalated (and principal) cell aldosterone actions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 3 — hypoaldosteronism and hyperkalemia in Type 4 RTA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — The 4.4 Minimum Urine pH Barrier & Buffer Kinetics","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with severe protein-calorie malnutrition has markedly reduced dietary phosphate intake and a correspondingly low urinary phosphate concentration. Although his alpha-intercalated cell H+-ATPase function is normal, his capacity to excrete H+ as titratable acid is reduced. Which principle best explains this?","choices":{"A":"H+-ATPase activity is directly proportional to dietary phosphate intake","B":"Malnutrition directly impairs the Na+-H+ exchanger in the proximal tubule","C":"Titratable acid excretion is capped by urinary buffer supply despite intact transporters","D":"Reduced phosphate intake lowers the minimum achievable urine pH below 4.4","E":"Titratable acid excretion depends mainly on plasma aldosterone concentration, not on urinary buffer"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Even with fully functional H+-ATPase, titratable acid excretion is limited by the amount of urinary buffer (chiefly phosphate) available to accept secreted H+; with markedly reduced urinary phosphate, urine pH falls to the 4.4 floor after far less total H+ has been buffered and excreted, reducing overall titratable acid excretion despite normal transporter function. Proximal Na+-H+ exchange mainly reclaims filtered HCO3-, while titratable acid is excreted mainly by the alpha-intercalated cells, and nothing in the stem points to a proximal transport defect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this is a clinically relevant application of the buffer-amount principle — malnourished patients have reduced titratable acid capacity with little urinary buffer available.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 3 — titratable acid excretion depends on the filtered buffer supply, unlike ammonium excretion"},{"id":"sdl22_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — The 4.4 Minimum Urine pH Barrier & Buffer Kinetics","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old man comes to the clinic because of 2 weeks of profuse watery diarrhea. Blood pressure is 108/68 mm Hg. Serum bicarbonate is 15 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L), and arterial pH is 7.30 (reference range: 7.35–7.45). His kidneys are increasing titratable acid excretion, using phosphate (pK 6.8) and creatinine (pK 5.0) as urinary buffers. For equal amounts of each buffer, which statement correctly compares the H+ they take up as his tubular fluid is acidified from his arterial pH to a urine pH of 5.5?","choices":{"A":"Creatinine takes up more H+ by urine pH 5.5, since its pK is closer to the final urine pH","B":"Both buffers take up equal H+ by urine pH 5.5, since total capacity does not depend on pK","C":"Creatinine takes up no H+ until the tubular fluid pH falls below its pK of 5.0","D":"Phosphate stops taking up H+ once the tubular fluid pH falls below its pK of 6.8","E":"Phosphate takes up more H+ by pH 5.5, since its pK lies between the starting and final fluid pH"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Over a fixed pH interval, the H+ a buffer takes up equals the change in its protonated fraction, 1/(1 + 10^(pH − pK)). From his plasma pH of 7.30 to a urine pH of 5.5, phosphate (pK 6.8) goes from about 24% to 95% protonated and takes up about 0.71 H+ per mole, whereas creatinine (pK 5.0) goes from under 1% to about 24% protonated and takes up only about 0.24 H+ per mole. Phosphate wins because its pK lies inside the pH range the tubular fluid actually passes through. Creatinine does take up some H+ above its pK, phosphate keeps buffering below 6.8, and capacity over a given interval clearly depends on pK, so the other choices are false. Over the full 7.4-to-4.4 window used in Costanzo Fig. 7.7, equal amounts of the two buffers would take up the same H+ (about 0.80 per mole each), which is why this comparison ends at a typical acid urine pH.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the H+ a urinary buffer carries as titratable acid equals the change in its protonated fraction over the pH range the tubular fluid passes through (about 7.4 down to 5.5), so phosphate, with its pK of 6.8 inside that range, carries most titratable acid. COMLEX: creatinine (pK 5.0) adds meaningfully only when urine is very acidic.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 4 — how a buffer pKa relative to the pH sets how well it buffers"},{"id":"sdl22_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — The 4.4 Minimum Urine pH Barrier & Buffer Kinetics","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old woman with stage 4 chronic kidney disease has a chronic metabolic acidosis. Blood pressure is 146/88 mm Hg. Serum bicarbonate is 17 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L), and creatinine is 3.6 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). Her nephrologist explains why urinary buffers limit how much acid she can excrete, using a hypothetical case. If her tubular fluid contained no urinary buffers whatsoever, what would happen as her alpha-intercalated cells began to secrete H+ into the tubular fluid?","choices":{"A":"Urine pH would rapidly fall to 4.4 after just a few H+ ions, halting further net secretion","B":"H+ secretion would continue indefinitely, since the H+-ATPase does not require any buffer to function","C":"Urine pH would stay at 7.4, since H+ secretion requires a buffer to be present","D":"The kidney would compensate by secreting NH4+ instead, restoring normal titratable acid excretion","E":"Urine pH would gradually decline over several hours before reaching a stable value near 6.0"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Without any urinary buffer to accept secreted H+, the first few H+ ions secreted would remain free in solution and rapidly drive the pH down to the 4.4 floor, at which point the H+-ATPase can no longer secrete against the resulting 1000-fold gradient and net H+ secretion ceases. This illustrates why urinary buffers, not transporter capacity alone, determine how much total H+ can be excreted.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this thought experiment is exactly how Costanzo’s text itself illustrates the buffer-dependence principle — useful for building intuition before applying it to real clinical scenarios like malnutrition or CKD.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 4 — without a buffer, added H+ changes pH steeply"},{"id":"ex2t_sdl22_q1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Proximal HCO3- Reclamation Cellular Mechanics","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with 4 days of vomiting has orthostatic hypotension, an arterial pH of 7.50 (reference range: 7.35–7.45), bicarbonate of 34 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L), and chloride of 86 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L). Which factor best explains why her kidneys continue to reabsorb the elevated filtered bicarbonate?","choices":{"A":"Atrial natriuretic peptide inhibition of distal sodium uptake","B":"A low arterial carbon dioxide tension in tubular cells","C":"Angiotensin II stimulation of proximal Na+-H+ exchange","D":"Saturation of the proximal bicarbonate transport maximum","E":"Blockade of alpha-intercalated cell proton pumps"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Vomiting generates the alkalosis, and volume and chloride depletion maintain it. Low effective arterial volume raises angiotensin II, which stimulates the apical Na+-H+ exchanger (NHE3) and basolateral Na+-HCO3- cotransport in the proximal tubule, so more filtered bicarbonate is reclaimed (contraction alkalosis); aldosterone also stimulates distal H+ secretion. Atrial natriuretic peptide is suppressed by volume depletion and does not drive bicarbonate reabsorption. Hypoventilation in metabolic alkalosis raises, rather than lowers, PaCO2. Saturating the transport maximum would cause bicarbonaturia, the opposite of what is maintaining her alkalosis. Proton pump blockade would lower bicarbonate generation. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 22, proximal bicarbonate reclamation); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 7 (angiotensin II and contraction alkalosis after vomiting).","boardPrep":"Step 1: saline-responsive metabolic alkalosis has a low urine chloride and corrects with isotonic NaCl. COMLEX: correcting the volume deficit removes the angiotensin II and aldosterone drive that maintains the alkalosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 2 — chloride depletion and low volume maintain metabolic alkalosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex2t_sdl22_q2","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Collecting Duct Alpha-Intercalated Cell Mechanics","batch":3,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman with Sjögren syndrome has an arterial pH of 7.31 (reference range: 7.35–7.45), sodium of 139 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), bicarbonate of 16 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L), chloride of 112 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L), and potassium of 3.1 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Her urine pH remains 6.4 during the systemic acidosis. Failure of which apical transporter best explains these findings?","choices":{"A":"The Na+-H+ exchanger of the proximal tubule","B":"The Na+-Cl- cotransporter of the distal tubule","C":"The Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter of the thick limb","D":"The H+-ATPase of alpha-intercalated cells","E":"The epithelial sodium channel of principal cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Alpha-intercalated cells secrete H+ through the apical H+-ATPase (and H+-K+-ATPase) and return the new bicarbonate to blood through the basolateral Cl--HCO3- exchanger. Autoimmune injury in Sjögren syndrome can disable this apical proton pump, so urine pH stays above 5.5 despite acidemia, producing a hypokalemic normal-anion-gap (distal) acidosis. Proximal Na+-H+ exchange failure causes proximal RTA, in which urine pH can fall below 5.5 once serum bicarbonate drops below the lowered threshold. Loss of the distal Na+-Cl- cotransporter or of NKCC2 causes a hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis, not an acidosis, and ENaC loss causes a hyperkalemic rather than hypokalemic acidosis. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 22, alpha-intercalated cell mechanics); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 7 (alpha-intercalated cell H+-ATPase and new bicarbonate); Guyton and Hall Ch. 31 (renal tubular acidosis); Robbins Ch. 6 (renal tubular acidosis in Sjögren syndrome).","boardPrep":"Step 1: distal RTA has urine pH above 5.5, hypokalemia, and calcium phosphate stones; Sjögren syndrome and amphotericin B are classic causes. COMLEX: proximal RTA can acidify urine once bicarbonate falls below the reduced threshold.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 2 — collecting duct H+ secretion also traps ammonia as ammonium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex2t_sdl22_q3","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — The 4.4 Minimum Urine pH Barrier & Buffer Kinetics","batch":3,"stem":"A 26-year-old man with diabetic ketoacidosis excretes a large acid load, and his urine pH is 4.6. His collecting duct proton pumps are working against a steep gradient. Which factor allows his net acid excretion to continue at this urine pH?","choices":{"A":"Urinary buffers that bind secreted protons","B":"A steeper proton gradient across the apical membrane","C":"Greater filtration of bicarbonate into the tubule","D":"Higher luminal flow that dilutes secreted protons","E":"A lower pK of the phosphate buffer pair"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The collecting duct H+-ATPase can lower urine pH to about 4.4, a gradient of about 1000-fold relative to plasma; at that limit pumping stops. Free H+ therefore accounts for very little acid excretion. Secreted H+ is instead bound by urinary buffers, phosphate as titratable acid and ammonia trapped as ammonium, so luminal free H+ stays low and the pumps keep working. A steeper gradient is what halts the pump. Filtered bicarbonate is nearly fully reclaimed upstream and its excretion would represent a loss of base. Luminal flow does not dilute H+ enough to substitute for buffering. The phosphate pK is a fixed chemical constant of about 6.8 that the kidney does not change; at a urine pH of 4.6 nearly all phosphate is already in the H2PO4- form, so continued acid excretion depends mainly on ammonia trapped as ammonium, with ketoacid anions adding buffer in DKA. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 22, the 4.4 minimum urine pH barrier); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 7 (minimum urine pH of 4.4 and the need for urinary buffers).","boardPrep":"Step 1: minimum urine pH is about 4.4; net acid excretion depends on titratable acid and ammonium, not free H+. COMLEX: in chronic acidosis, ammonium production rises and provides most of the adaptive increase.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 3 — ammonium, not titratable acid, expands to meet a sustained acid load","isHighYield":false}],"23":[{"id":"sdl23_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Proximal Tubule Glutamine Metabolism & Potassium Regulation","batch":1,"stem":"Which enzyme metabolizes glutamine to glutamate and NH4+ in proximal tubule cells, initiating renal ammonium excretion?","choices":{"A":"Glutamate dehydrogenase, a mitochondrial enzyme in proximal tubule cells","B":"Carbonic anhydrase, a brush-border enzyme in proximal tubule cells","C":"Glutamine synthetase, a cytosolic enzyme found in hepatocytes","D":"Glutaminase, a mitochondrial enzyme in proximal tubule cells","E":"Alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, a mitochondrial enzyme in proximal tubule cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The mitochondrial enzyme glutaminase metabolizes glutamine to glutamate and NH4+ in proximal tubule cells; the glutamate is then further metabolized to alpha-ketoglutarate and ultimately to CO2, H2O, and new HCO3-. Glutamate dehydrogenase and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase act downstream of glutaminase rather than initiating the pathway, carbonic anhydrase handles CO2/H2CO3 interconversion rather than glutamine metabolism, and glutamine synthetase runs the reverse hepatic reaction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: glutaminase induction drives the adaptive rise in ammonium excretion seen in chronic acidosis. COMLEX: know this enzyme by name; its activity is enhanced by acidosis and hypokalemia and suppressed by hyperkalemia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 3 — hyperkalemia-induced glutaminase inhibition also underlies Type 4 RTA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Proximal Tubule Glutamine Metabolism & Potassium Regulation","batch":1,"stem":"In proximal tubule cells, what happens to the NH4+ generated by glutamine metabolism before it appears in the tubular lumen?","choices":{"A":"It equilibrates with NH3 and H+, and lipid-soluble NH3 diffuses into the lumen while H+ is secreted separately","B":"It is transported intact across the luminal membrane by a dedicated NH4+-specific channel protein","C":"It equilibrates with NH3 and H+, and water-soluble H+ diffuses into the lumen while NH3 is secreted separately","D":"It is reabsorbed directly across the basolateral membrane before ever reaching the tubular lumen","E":"It combines with secreted HCO3- to form ammonium bicarbonate, which is excreted as one unit"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Intracellular NH4+ is in equilibrium with NH3 and H+; the lipid-soluble NH3 diffuses down its concentration gradient into the lumen, while H+ is secreted separately by the Na+-H+ exchanger (NHE3). Once both are in the lumen, they recombine into NH4+. NH4+ itself does not cross membranes via a dedicated channel, is not reabsorbed basolaterally at this step, and does not form an \"ammonium bicarbonate\" complex.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this NH3/H+ splitting-and-recombining pattern in the proximal tubule is the same diffusion-trapping logic reused later in the collecting duct — recognizing the parallel saves time on paired-concept questions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 3 — hyperkalemia-induced glutaminase inhibition also underlies Type 4 RTA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Proximal Tubule Glutamine Metabolism & Potassium Regulation","batch":1,"stem":"For each NH4+ molecule generated from glutamine metabolism and ultimately excreted, how much new HCO3- is reabsorbed into the blood?","choices":{"A":"Two new HCO3- molecules, since each NH4+ ion carries two units of alkali","B":"One new HCO3-, matched to the NH4+ that appears in the urine","C":"Zero new HCO3-, since NH4+ excretion does not regenerate bicarbonate stores","D":"Half a new HCO3- molecule, since part of the glutamine carbon skeleton is lost as CO2","E":"Three new HCO3- molecules, produced by carbonic anhydrase acting on filtered CO2"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Each glutamine molecule metabolized by glutaminase yields two NH4+ and, via downstream metabolism of the carbon skeleton to CO2 and H2O, two new HCO3- that are reabsorbed across the basolateral membrane via Na+-HCO3- cotransport, so each NH4+ excreted is matched by one new HCO3-. This 1:1 ratio per NH4+ mirrors the titratable acid mechanism and helps replenish HCO3- stores depleted by fixed-acid buffering; it is not a 2:1, 0:1, or fractional relationship per NH4+, and it originates from glutamine metabolism rather than filtered CO2.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pair this 1:1 NH4+-to-new-HCO3- ratio with the 1:1 titratable-acid-to-new-HCO3- ratio from SDL 22 — both mechanisms replenish bicarbonate at the same fixed rate per H+ handled.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 3 — hyperkalemia-induced glutaminase inhibition also underlies Type 4 RTA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Segmental Ammonium Transport & Collecting Duct Diffusion Trapping","batch":1,"stem":"By what mechanism is a portion of the secreted NH4+ reabsorbed in the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle?","choices":{"A":"NH4+ is actively pumped by a basolateral NH4+-ATPase","B":"NH4+ diffuses passively through paracellular tight junctions","C":"NH4+ substitutes for K+ on the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter NKCC2","D":"NH4+ substitutes for Na+ on the apical Na+-H+ exchanger NHE3","E":"NH4+ is cotransported with Cl- on an NH4+-Cl- symporter"},"correct":"C","explanation":"NH4+ delivered to the thick ascending limb is reabsorbed by substituting for K+ on the luminal Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter (NKCC2), the same transporter that drives countercurrent multiplication for NaCl. There is no dedicated NH4+-ATPase or NH4+-Cl- symporter here, paracellular diffusion is not the mechanism described for this segment, and NHE3 mediates H+ secretion in the proximal tubule rather than NH4+ reabsorption in the thick ascending limb.","boardPrep":"Step 1: remember that NH4+ substitutes for K+ on NKCC2, the same transporter that moves NaCl in the thick ascending limb.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 2 — NKCC2-mediated Na+ reabsorption in the thick ascending limb","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Segmental Ammonium Transport & Collecting Duct Diffusion Trapping","batch":1,"stem":"What is the physiological consequence of NH4+ reabsorption by the thick ascending limb?","choices":{"A":"NH4+ is immediately excreted unchanged into the urine before reaching the collecting duct","B":"NH4+ is converted directly into urea within hepatocytes for systemic excretion","C":"NH4+ diffuses back into the systemic circulation without ever entering the interstitium","D":"NH4+ is degraded by a dedicated thick ascending limb glutaminase into free ammonia gas","E":"NH4+ concentrates within the medullary interstitium via countercurrent multiplication"},"correct":"E","explanation":"By substituting for K+ on NKCC2, NH4+ is reabsorbed alongside Na+ and Cl- and participates in countercurrent multiplication, becoming concentrated in the medullary interstitial fluid, where it equilibrates with NH3. This concentrated interstitial reservoir is what later supplies NH3 for diffusion trapping in the collecting duct; it is not excreted at this point, not converted to urea here, does not simply diffuse back into the circulation, and is not degraded by a thick-ascending-limb glutaminase reaction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this medullary NH3/NH4+ reservoir is analogous to the medullary NaCl gradient built by countercurrent multiplication; medullary urea additionally depends on ADH-driven urea recycling.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 1 — countercurrent multiplication and the medullary osmotic gradient","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Segmental Ammonium Transport & Collecting Duct Diffusion Trapping","batch":1,"stem":"In the collecting duct, why does only the NH3 form of the NH3/NH4+ buffer pair diffuse from the medullary interstitium into the tubular lumen?","choices":{"A":"NH3 is lipid soluble and can cross cell membranes","B":"NH3 is transported by a dedicated carrier protein that excludes NH4+","C":"NH4+ is too large in molecular size to cross the same channels as NH3","D":"NH4+ is repelled electrically by the negative charge of the tubular lumen","E":"NH3 is actively secreted by a dedicated ATP-dependent transporter in the luminal membrane"},"correct":"A","explanation":"NH3 is lipid soluble and diffuses freely across cell membranes, while its charged conjugate NH4+ is water soluble and cannot cross the collecting duct cell membranes; this is why only NH3 moves from the medullary interstitium into the lumen, where it recombines with secreted H+ to re-form NH4+, which is then trapped and excreted. No dedicated carrier protein, size-exclusion effect, electrical repulsion, or active ATP-dependent transporter is required to explain this movement — it is passive diffusion governed by lipid solubility.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"diffusion trapping\" is the exact term Costanzo uses for this NH3-diffuses/NH4+-is-trapped pattern — expect it tested by name.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 2 — distal H+-ATPase failure also disrupts urinary acidification","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clinical Excretion Adaptations: Titratable Acid vs. Ammonium","batch":1,"stem":"In a healthy person eating a typical high-protein diet, approximately how is the roughly 50 mEq/day of fixed H+ produced normally excreted?","choices":{"A":"About 35 mEq/day as titratable acid and 15 mEq/day as NH4+","B":"About 20 mEq/day as titratable acid and 30 mEq/day as NH4+","C":"About 45 mEq/day as titratable acid and 5 mEq/day as NH4+","D":"About 12 mEq/day as titratable acid and 38 mEq/day as NH4+","E":"About 28 mEq/day as titratable acid and 22 mEq/day as NH4+"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Of the roughly 50 mEq/day of fixed H+ produced from protein and phospholipid catabolism, about 40% (20 mEq/day) is normally excreted as titratable acid and about 60% (30 mEq/day) as NH4+, so that all of the daily fixed acid load is eliminated and the HCO3- consumed buffering it is fully replaced. The other proportions listed do not match these normal reference values.","boardPrep":"Step 1: memorize the 20/30 split (40%/60%) for normal fixed acid excretion — it is the baseline every acidosis vignette compares against.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 3 — blunted NH3 synthesis and NH4+ excretion, the defect behind Type 4 RTA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clinical Excretion Adaptations: Titratable Acid vs. Ammonium","batch":1,"stem":"In diabetic ketoacidosis, fixed H+ production can rise roughly tenfold. Which renal adaptation allows the kidneys to excrete this additional acid load?","choices":{"A":"An adaptive fall in NH3 synthesis, offset by a matching rise in titratable acid excretion","B":"A fixed rate of NH4+ excretion that does not rise with the increased acid load","C":"A compensatory rise in filtered phosphate load produced directly by the liver","D":"An adaptive rise in NH3 synthesis, plus extra buffering from filtered ketoacid salts","E":"A shift of most fixed acid excretion away from NH4+ and toward respiratory CO2 elimination"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In diabetic ketoacidosis, acidosis induces glutaminase and drives an adaptive rise in NH3 synthesis, increasing NH4+ excretion, while filtered ketoacid salts (beta-hydroxybutyrate and acetoacetate) serve as additional urinary buffers that raise titratable acid excretion as well. NH3 synthesis rises rather than falls, NH4+ excretion is not fixed, the liver does not produce filtered phosphate, and acid excretion is not shifted toward respiratory CO2 elimination, which is a separate compensatory system entirely.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ketoacid-salts-as-urinary-buffers detail explains why DKA patients can still mount a titratable acid response — a classic integrative point.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 3 — blunted NH3 synthesis and NH4+ excretion, the defect behind Type 4 RTA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clinical Excretion Adaptations: Titratable Acid vs. Ammonium","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with advanced chronic kidney disease produces the usual 50 mEq/day of fixed H+ but excretes only about 15 mEq/day total (10 mEq/day as titratable acid, 5 mEq/day as NH4+). What best explains this reduced excretion?","choices":{"A":"Progressive nephron loss impairs NH3 synthesis while titratable acid excretion stays at its normal level","B":"Progressive nephron loss impairs phosphate filtration while NH3 synthesis stays intact","C":"Nephron loss reduces both filtered phosphate buffering and NH3 synthesis for ammonium excretion","D":"Increased plasma aldosterone directly suppresses both excretion pathways to an equal degree","E":"Increased dietary phosphate intake overwhelms the kidneys’ titratable acid buffering capacity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In advanced chronic kidney disease, progressive nephron loss reduces the glomerular filtration and hence the filtered phosphate available for titratable acid buffering, and impairs NH3 synthesis in the remaining diseased nephrons, so both excretion pathways fall together; the resulting mismatch between the 50 mEq/day produced and only 15 mEq/day excreted is the source of the chronic metabolic acidosis of CKD. Neither pathway remains fully intact in isolation, aldosterone is not the mechanism here, and dietary phosphate intake is not implicated.","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic kidney disease acidosis is a failure of excretion, not overproduction — contrast with DKA, where acid production itself is what rises.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 3 — blunted NH3 synthesis and NH4+ excretion, the defect behind Type 4 RTA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Proximal Tubule Glutamine Metabolism & Potassium Regulation","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and stage 3 chronic kidney disease is found to have a plasma [K+] of 6.2 mEq/L and a mild non-anion-gap metabolic acidosis. Which mechanism best explains how his hyperkalemia contributes to impaired renal acid excretion?","choices":{"A":"K+ enters proximal tubule cells in exchange for H+, lowering intracellular pH and stimulating glutaminase","B":"K+ directly binds and inactivates the basolateral Na+-HCO3- cotransporter","C":"K+ competitively inhibits NH4+ binding to the Na+-H+ exchanger in the proximal tubule","D":"K+ enters proximal tubule cells in exchange for H+, raising intracellular pH and inhibiting glutaminase","E":"K+ stimulates aldosterone release, which paradoxically suppresses collecting duct H+-ATPase activity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In hyperkalemia, K+ enters renal cells in exchange for H+ leaving; the resulting rise in intracellular and intramitochondrial pH inhibits glutaminase, reducing NH3 synthesis and NH4+ excretion, which is the mechanism underlying Type 4 renal tubular acidosis. Intracellular pH rises rather than falls, and K+ does not directly inactivate the Na+-HCO3- cotransporter, compete for the Na+-H+ exchanger, or act through an aldosterone-mediated suppression of H+-ATPase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperkalemia-induced Type 4 RTA is a top-tested cause of non-anion-gap metabolic acidosis, classically seen in diabetic nephropathy with hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 3 — hyperkalemia-induced glutaminase inhibition causing Type 4 RTA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Proximal Tubule Glutamine Metabolism & Potassium Regulation","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with bulimia nervosa and chronic vomiting has a plasma [K+] of 2.8 mEq/L. Which effect on her renal ammoniagenesis would be expected from this potassium disturbance?","choices":{"A":"Increased NH3 synthesis, since intracellular H+ entry lowers intramitochondrial pH and stimulates glutaminase","B":"Decreased NH3 synthesis, since intracellular H+ entry directly raises intramitochondrial pH and inhibits glutaminase","C":"Increased NH3 synthesis, mediated through direct hypokalemia-induced upregulation of NKCC2","D":"No change in NH3 synthesis, since glutaminase activity is insensitive to intracellular pH shifts","E":"Decreased NH3 synthesis, mediated through direct hypokalemia-induced downregulation of NKCC2"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In hypokalemia, K+ leaves renal cells while H+ enters; the resulting fall in intracellular and intramitochondrial pH stimulates glutaminase, increasing NH3 synthesis and NH4+ excretion. Intracellular H+ entry lowers, rather than raises, intramitochondrial pH, glutaminase activity is directly sensitive to this intracellular pH shift, and the mechanism is a pH effect on glutaminase rather than an NKCC2-mediated effect.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: hypokalemia from chronic vomiting or diuretic use classically produces a metabolic alkalosis, yet this same hypokalemia independently boosts ammoniagenesis — a nuance worth distinguishing from the acid-base disturbance itself.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 3 — hyperkalemia-induced glutaminase inhibition causing Type 4 RTA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Proximal Tubule Glutamine Metabolism & Potassium Regulation","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with short bowel syndrome after extensive small-bowel resection has chronic diarrhea and a chronic metabolic acidosis. Blood pressure is 112/70 mm Hg. Serum bicarbonate is 16 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L), arterial pH is 7.31 (reference range: 7.35–7.45), and creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). Through adaptive glutaminase induction, his NH4+ excretion has risen from a baseline of 30 mEq/day to 150 mEq/day. Approximately how much additional new HCO3- would be expected to enter his blood as a result?","choices":{"A":"About 60 mEq/day, since half of the increased NH4+ excretion generates new HCO3-","B":"About 240 mEq/day, since two new HCO3- molecules are generated per NH4+ excreted","C":"About 120 mEq/day, matching the increase in NH4+ excretion on a 1:1 basis","D":"Essentially zero, since NH4+ excretion depletes existing HCO3- stores","E":"About 120 mEq/day, but limited to the initial 24 hours of adaptation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Because each NH4+ excreted is generated alongside one new HCO3- reabsorbed into the blood, an increase in NH4+ excretion from 30 to 150 mEq/day (an increase of 120 mEq/day) is matched by an additional 120 mEq/day of new HCO3- entering the blood. The ratio is 1:1, not 1:2 or 2:1, NH4+ excretion generates rather than depletes HCO3-, and this relationship holds for as long as the adaptive glutaminase induction persists, not only for the first 24 hours.","boardPrep":"Step 1: quantitative 1:1-ratio problems like this are a favorite way to test whether the ammonium-HCO3- coupling is truly understood rather than just memorized as a phrase.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 2 — each H+ excreted as titratable acid generates one new HCO3- for the blood","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Segmental Ammonium Transport & Collecting Duct Diffusion Trapping","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman comes to the emergency department because of 5 days of severe watery diarrhea after returning from travel abroad. Blood pressure is 100/62 mm Hg, and pulse is 104/min. Her mucous membranes are dry. Serum sodium is 136 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), potassium is 3.2 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), chloride is 114 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L), and bicarbonate is 14 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L), giving a normal anion gap. Urinalysis shows a urine pH of 5.0 (reference range: 4.5–8.0). Which effect would this acidic urine have on her ammonium excretion via diffusion trapping?","choices":{"A":"It would decrease NH4+ excretion, since low luminal NH3 concentration flattens the gradient favoring NH3 diffusion into the lumen","B":"It would have no measurable effect on NH4+ excretion, since diffusion trapping is independent of luminal urine pH","C":"It would increase NH4+ excretion, since low luminal NH3 concentration steepens the gradient favoring NH3 diffusion into the lumen","D":"It would increase NH4+ excretion, but through direct stimulation of glutaminase within the collecting duct cells","E":"It would decrease NH4+ excretion, since acidic urine directly inhibits collecting duct H+-ATPase activity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A low tubular fluid pH converts more of the luminal NH3/NH4+ pool into NH4+, lowering the free luminal NH3 concentration; this steepens the concentration gradient favoring diffusion of NH3 from the medullary interstitium into the lumen, increasing NH4+ formation and excretion. The gradient is steepened, not flattened, diffusion trapping is directly dependent on luminal pH rather than independent of it, glutaminase is a proximal tubule enzyme rather than a collecting duct effect, and a urine pH of 5.0 is well above the 4.4 floor, so H+ secretion continues.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this is why acidic urine and NH4+ excretion rise together in states like diarrhea — low urine pH is not just a marker of acidosis, it is part of the mechanism that boosts ammonium elimination.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 2 — distal H+-ATPase failure disrupting the diffusion-trapping gradient for NH4+","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Segmental Ammonium Transport & Collecting Duct Diffusion Trapping","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman is evaluated for hypertension that persists despite three medications. Blood pressure is 168/100 mm Hg. Serum potassium is 2.9 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), and bicarbonate is 31 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Plasma aldosterone concentration is elevated with suppressed renin activity, and CT scan shows a 1.5-cm left adrenal adenoma. Her collecting duct H+-ATPase activity is increased. How would this be expected to affect her ammonium excretion via diffusion trapping, independent of any change in NH3 synthesis?","choices":{"A":"Increased H+ secretion would leave local NH4+ formation unchanged, since NH3 diffusion is the true rate-limiting step","B":"Increased H+ secretion would have no effect on NH4+ formation, since NH3 diffusion is governed primarily by renal blood flow","C":"Increased H+ secretion would directly inhibit NH3 diffusion from the medullary interstitium","D":"Increased H+ secretion would form more NH4+ locally, retaining more of it within the tubular fluid","E":"Increased H+ secretion would convert trapped NH4+ back into diffusible NH3"},"correct":"D","explanation":"More luminal H+ combines with more diffused NH3 to form more NH4+, which remains trapped in the tubular fluid because it cannot diffuse back across the lipid membrane; increased H+-ATPase activity therefore increases local NH4+ formation and trapping. H+ secretion does not decrease NH4+ formation, is not unrelated to ammonium handling since it directly supplies the H+ half of the diffusion-trapping reaction, does not inhibit NH3 diffusion, and does not convert NH4+ back into NH3, which would require the opposite (alkaline) shift.","boardPrep":"Step 1: diffusion trapping links titratable acid physiology and ammonium physiology through the same H+-ATPase — a single transporter effect can be tested from either angle.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 2 — distal H+-ATPase failure disrupting the diffusion-trapping gradient for NH4+","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Segmental Ammonium Transport & Collecting Duct Diffusion Trapping","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man volunteers for a renal metabolic study. He is healthy, with a blood pressure of 120/76 mm Hg, a serum bicarbonate of 25 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L), and a serum creatinine of 0.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). He receives an infusion of isotope-labeled glutamine, and investigators follow the labeled ammonium made by his proximal tubule cells down the nephron. A portion of the label appears in his urine directly. Which path describes how the remainder is ultimately excreted?","choices":{"A":"Reabsorbed by the proximal tubule, deposited in the cortical interstitium, then secreted into the distal tubule","B":"Reabsorbed by the collecting duct, deposited in the medullary interstitium, then secreted into the thick ascending limb","C":"Reabsorbed by the thin descending limb, deposited in the medullary interstitium, then secreted into the collecting duct","D":"Reabsorbed by the thick ascending limb, deposited in the medullary interstitium, then returned to the proximal tubule","E":"Reabsorbed by the thick ascending limb, deposited in the medullary interstitium, then secreted into the collecting duct"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Part of the NH4+ secreted into the proximal tubule lumen is excreted directly; the remainder takes a circuitous route. It is reabsorbed by the thick ascending limb, where NH4+ substitutes for K+ on the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter, is concentrated in the medullary interstitial fluid by countercurrent multiplication, and then enters the collecting duct as lipid-soluble NH3, which combines with secreted H+ to form trapped NH4+. The other paths reverse the order of segments or place the steps in segments that the source does not assign to them.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ammonium handling is sequential: proximal secretion, thick ascending limb reabsorption on NKCC2 in place of K+, medullary accumulation, then collecting duct diffusion trapping.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — NH4+ is concentrated in the medullary interstitium by countercurrent multiplication, much like NaCl","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clinical Excretion Adaptations: Titratable Acid vs. Ammonium","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman has a chronic metabolic acidosis from persistent diarrhea due to a carcinoid tumor. Blood pressure is 110/70 mm Hg. Serum bicarbonate is 15 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L), arterial pH is 7.30 (reference range: 7.35–7.45), and creatinine is 0.8 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). Over the following week, her urinary ammonium excretion rises several-fold above baseline, whereas her titratable acid excretion increases much less. What best explains this difference in adaptive capacity?","choices":{"A":"Titratable acid excretion can be upregulated enzymatically, while NH3 synthesis is capped by fixed phosphate availability","B":"Both pathways are capped by fixed buffer availability, so neither can meaningfully increase during acidosis","C":"Titratable acid excretion is capped by fixed filtered phosphate, while NH3 synthesis is enzymatically upregulatable","D":"Titratable acid excretion is capped by aldosterone secretion, while NH3 synthesis is capped by ADH secretion","E":"Both pathways can be upregulated enzymatically to an equal, unlimited degree throughout chronic acidosis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Titratable acid excretion is fundamentally limited by the fixed amount of filtered phosphate (and, in states like diabetic ketoacidosis, filtered ketoacid salts) available to serve as urinary buffer, whereas NH4+ excretion depends on glutaminase-driven NH3 synthesis, an enzymatic pathway that can be upregulated several-fold during chronic acidosis. The roles are not reversed, titratable acid excretion is not capped by aldosterone nor NH3 synthesis by ADH, and titratable acid excretion clearly does not scale enzymatically to the same degree as NH4+ excretion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"fixed buffer amount vs. inducible enzyme\" is the single sentence that explains almost every titratable-acid-versus-ammonium comparison question on this topic.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 3 — titratable acid is capped by the urinary buffer supply and the minimum urine pH","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clinical Excretion Adaptations: Titratable Acid vs. Ammonium","batch":2,"stem":"Two patients are admitted to the hospital with an identical fixed-acid load. A 22-year-old woman has diabetic ketoacidosis with normal underlying renal function; her serum creatinine before admission was 0.8 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). A 70-year-old man has advanced chronic kidney disease, with a serum creatinine of 5.8 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). Both patients have a serum bicarbonate of 14 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Which best describes their expected ammonium excretion responses?","choices":{"A":"The chronic kidney disease patient mounts a large adaptive rise in NH4+ excretion, while the diabetic ketoacidosis patient cannot mount a comparable rise","B":"The diabetic ketoacidosis patient mounts a large rise in NH4+ excretion, while the chronic kidney disease patient cannot mount a comparable rise","C":"Both patients mount an equally large adaptive rise in NH4+ excretion, since their acid load is identical in magnitude and duration","D":"Neither patient can increase NH4+ excretion, since this acid load exceeds the kidneys’ maximum capacity in both clinical cases","E":"Both patients fail to raise NH4+ excretion, each for a distinct underlying physiologic mechanism that blocks the response"},"correct":"B","explanation":"With intact nephron mass, the diabetic ketoacidosis patient can induce glutaminase and mount a large adaptive rise in NH4+ excretion to help handle the increased acid load; the chronic kidney disease patient, with a markedly reduced number of functioning nephrons, cannot mount a comparable response, since NH4+ excretion stays at only about 5 mEq/day. The roles are not reversed, the two patients do not respond identically despite an identical acid load, the acid load does not exceed renal capacity when nephron mass is intact, and only the chronic kidney disease patient has a mechanism that blocks the response.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this side-by-side comparison is a common exam device for testing whether nephron mass, not just acid production, determines the adaptive ammonium response.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 3 — impaired NH3 synthesis blunting NH4+ excretion, as in Type 4 RTA","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clinical Excretion Adaptations: Titratable Acid vs. Ammonium","batch":2,"stem":"A 20-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes mellitus is admitted because of vomiting, abdominal pain, and rapid breathing after an insulin pump failure. Blood pressure is 98/60 mm Hg, and respirations are 30/min. Serum glucose is 560 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL), bicarbonate is 8 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L), and the anion gap is 28 mEq/L (reference range: 8–16 mEq/L). Serum ketones are strongly positive. Over the next day, her titratable acid excretion rises above the normal baseline of 20 mEq/day. What best accounts for this additional titratable acid excretion?","choices":{"A":"Filtered ketoacid salts such as beta-hydroxybutyrate and acetoacetate act as additional urinary buffers","B":"Increased glomerular filtration rate delivers more phosphate to the urine for buffering","C":"Increased aldosterone secretion directly raises phosphate reabsorption and urinary concentration","D":"Increased NH3 synthesis chemically converts into additional titratable acid in the lumen","E":"Increased H+-ATPase activity, without any change in buffer, accounts for the extra excretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In diabetic ketoacidosis, overproduced ketoacids such as beta-hydroxybutyric acid and acetoacetic acid are filtered as their salts and act as additional urinary buffers analogous to phosphate, raising the total amount of H+ excreted as titratable acid. Glomerular filtration rate and aldosterone are not implicated in this increase, NH3 does not chemically convert into titratable acid, and increased H+-ATPase activity alone cannot generate titratable acid without additional buffer being present to accept the secreted H+.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ketoacid-salts-as-buffer detail is exactly why DKA patients can excrete more titratable acid than their phosphate load alone would predict — a frequently tested integrative point.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 1 — the ketoacid load of DKA is a high anion gap metabolic acidosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex2t_sdl23_q1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Proximal Tubule Glutamine Metabolism & Potassium Regulation","batch":3,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with diabetic nephropathy has a serum potassium of 5.9 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), chloride of 112 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L), bicarbonate of 18 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L), sodium of 138 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), and creatinine of 1.3 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). His urine pH is 5.1 with low urinary ammonium. Which mechanism best explains the low ammonium excretion?","choices":{"A":"Failure of apical proton pumps in intercalated cells","B":"Loss of nephron mass with reduced glutamine delivery","C":"Trapping of ammonia inside the medullary interstitium","D":"Intracellular alkalosis that slows proximal glutaminase","E":"Increased proximal reabsorption of bicarbonate"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hyperkalemia shifts K+ into proximal tubule cells in exchange for H+, creating intracellular alkalosis that inhibits mitochondrial glutaminase and ammoniagenesis. This is the mechanism of type 4 (hyperkalemic) RTA from hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism in diabetic nephropathy, and the normal-anion-gap acidosis with a urine pH that can still fall to 5.1 shows that H+ secretion, although reduced by low aldosterone, can still acidify the urine; the limiting factor is the NH3 supply. Complete proton pump failure, as in distal RTA, would keep urine pH above 5.5. Mild loss of nephron mass, with a creatinine of only 1.3 mg/dL, does not explain the selective drop in ammonium with hyperkalemia. Medullary trapping of ammonia normally increases urinary ammonium rather than lowering it. Increased proximal bicarbonate reabsorption would raise, not lower, serum bicarbonate. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 23, glutamine metabolism and potassium regulation); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 7 (hyperkalemia inhibits ammonia synthesis through intracellular alkalosis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: type 4 RTA = hyperkalemia, normal-anion-gap acidosis, and urine pH below 5.5 from low aldosterone or aldosterone resistance. COMLEX: correcting hyperkalemia restores ammoniagenesis and improves the acidosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 -> Obj 3 — transcellular H+-K+ exchange links potassium shifts to intracellular pH","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex2t_sdl23_q2","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Segmental Ammonium Transport & Collecting Duct Diffusion Trapping","batch":3,"stem":"A 22-year-old woman with 4 days of watery diarrhea has a bicarbonate of 15 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L) and chloride of 115 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L). Her urine pH is 5.0 with a high urinary ammonium concentration. Which property keeps ammonium in her collecting duct lumen?","choices":{"A":"Low membrane permeability of the charged form","B":"Active pumping of ammonia back into the cell","C":"Substitution of ammonium for potassium on ROMK","D":"Absence of carbonic anhydrase from the lumen","E":"Generation of ammonia within collecting duct cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Uncharged NH3 is lipid soluble and diffuses from the medullary interstitium into the collecting duct lumen, where secreted H+ converts it to NH4+. Because the charged NH4+ crosses membranes poorly, it is trapped in the acidic lumen and excreted (diffusion trapping), carrying H+ out and leaving new bicarbonate in blood. Ammonia is not actively pumped back into cells. Ammonium substitutes for K+ on NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb, not on ROMK in the collecting duct. Absence of luminal carbonic anhydrase does not determine membrane permeability. Ammonia is produced from glutamine in the proximal tubule rather than in collecting duct cells. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 23, segmental ammonium transport and diffusion trapping); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 7 (lipid-soluble NH3 and trapping of NH4+ in acidic urine).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diffusion trapping requires an acidic collecting duct lumen, so distal RTA reduces ammonium excretion. COMLEX: in diarrhea, high urinary ammonium with a urine pH below 5.5 separates it from distal RTA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 1 — diarrhea causes a normal-anion-gap metabolic acidosis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex2t_sdl23_q3","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Clinical Excretion Adaptations: Titratable Acid vs. Ammonium","batch":3,"stem":"A 19-year-old man has had ketoacidosis for 3 days, and his serum bicarbonate is 10 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). His net acid excretion has risen several-fold. Which component accounts for most of the increase in his net acid excretion?","choices":{"A":"Phosphate-buffered titratable acid","B":"Free hydrogen ions in the urine","C":"Bicarbonate reclaimed proximally","D":"Chloride excreted with sodium","E":"Ammonium synthesized from glutamine"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sustained acidosis induces proximal glutaminase, so ammonium production from glutamine rises severalfold over days and accounts for most of the adaptive increase in net acid excretion; each ammonium excreted adds a new bicarbonate to blood. Titratable acid rises several-fold in ketoacidosis, mainly from ketoacid anion buffers, but far less than ammonium, which rises from about 30 to 400–500 mEq/day. Free H+ is negligible because urine pH does not fall below about 4.4. Reclaiming filtered bicarbonate prevents base loss but does not add net acid excretion. Chloride excretion with sodium is not a form of acid excretion. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 23, titratable acid vs. ammonium adaptations); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 7 (adaptive increase in NH4+ excretion in chronic acidosis); Guyton and Hall Ch. 31 (ammonium as the main adaptive buffer).","boardPrep":"Step 1: net acid excretion = titratable acid + ammonium - bicarbonate; ammonium is the adjustable component. COMLEX: chronic kidney disease limits ammonium production and causes acidosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 3 — urinary buffers let H+ secretion continue near the minimum urine pH","isHighYield":true}],"24":[{"id":"sdl24_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — High-Anion-Gap vs. Normal-Anion-Gap Metabolic Acidosis","batch":1,"stem":"A patient has plasma Na+ 140 mEq/L, HCO3- 12 mEq/L, and Cl- 100 mEq/L. What is her plasma anion gap, and is it elevated above the normal range of 8-14 mEq/L?","choices":{"A":"12 mEq/L, essentially unchanged from her baseline","B":"16 mEq/L, marginally higher than her baseline","C":"28 mEq/L, a distinctly high calculated result","D":"40 mEq/L, a strikingly high calculated result","E":"8 mEq/L, near the lower boundary typically seen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Plasma anion gap = [Na+] - ([HCO3-] + [Cl-]) = 140 - (12 + 100) = 28 mEq/L, which exceeds the normal range of 8-14 mEq/L (Costanzo; First Aid 2025 and the course notes give 8-12 mEq/L) and indicates accumulation of unmeasured anions. None of the other listed values result from correctly applying this formula to the given electrolytes. 12 mEq/L is simply the bicarbonate value, 16 mEq/L subtracts bicarbonate twice, and 8 mEq/L sits at the low end of normal, which this result clearly exceeds.","boardPrep":"Step 1: memorize the anion gap formula cold — it is calculated identically on nearly every acid-base vignette regardless of which disorder is ultimately diagnosed.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 4 — differentiating RTA subtypes builds directly on this normal-anion-gap framework","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — High-Anion-Gap vs. Normal-Anion-Gap Metabolic Acidosis","batch":1,"stem":"The plasma anion gap reflects which underlying physiological principle?","choices":{"A":"Osmotic equilibrium between plasma and interstitial fluid","B":"Renal autoregulation of glomerular filtration rate","C":"Colloid osmotic pressure exerted by plasma proteins","D":"Acid-base buffering capacity of the bicarbonate system","E":"Electroneutrality of body fluid compartments"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Plasma must remain electroneutral, so when the measured cation (Na+) exceeds the sum of the measured anions (HCO3- and Cl-), the difference reflects unmeasured anions such as plasma proteins, phosphate, citrate, and sulfate. The anion gap is not a measure of osmotic equilibrium, glomerular filtration rate, colloid osmotic pressure, or direct H+ titration.","boardPrep":"Step 1: understanding electroneutrality as the basis for the anion gap explains why an elevated gap means \"something is there that we did not measure,\" not \"something is wrong with sodium.\"","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 4 — differentiating RTA subtypes builds directly on this normal-anion-gap framework","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — High-Anion-Gap vs. Normal-Anion-Gap Metabolic Acidosis","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following is a classic cause of metabolic acidosis with an increased anion gap?","choices":{"A":"Diarrhea, from direct gastrointestinal loss of bicarbonate-rich intestinal fluid","B":"Type 2 renal tubular acidosis, from failure to reabsorb filtered HCO3-","C":"Infusion of large volumes of isotonic saline, from dilution of plasma HCO3-","D":"Diabetic ketoacidosis, from accumulation of beta-hydroxybutyric and acetoacetic acids","E":"Type 4 renal tubular acidosis, from hypoaldosteronism with hyperkalemia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Diabetic ketoacidosis accumulates the unmeasured organic anions beta-hydroxybutyrate and acetoacetate, producing a high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis, alongside lactic acidosis, salicylate poisoning, and methanol/ethylene glycol poisoning. Diarrhea and Types 2 and 4 RTA instead cause normal-anion-gap (hyperchloremic) acidosis, and isotonic saline infusion is not a classic cause of increased-anion-gap acidosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the MUDPILES mnemonic groups the high-anion-gap causes; RTAs and diarrhea belong on the opposite (normal-anion-gap) list.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 4 — differentiating RTA subtypes builds directly on this normal-anion-gap framework","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Metabolic Alkalosis Maintenance & Respiratory Acid-Base Etiologies","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with metabolic alkalosis from prolonged vomiting has persistent extracellular fluid volume contraction. Which combination of mechanisms best explains why her elevated HCO3- is not simply excreted in the urine?","choices":{"A":"Angiotensin II stimulating proximal Na+-H+ exchange and aldosterone stimulating distal H+ secretion","B":"Beta-intercalated cells secreting new HCO3- into the lumen, overriding any proximal reabsorption change","C":"Angiotensin II stimulating proximal Na+-H+ exchange and aldosterone stimulating distal HCO3- secretion","D":"A rise in glomerular filtration rate that delivers an overall smaller filtered HCO3- load to the tubule","E":"A drop in renin release that substantially blunts angiotensin II- and aldosterone-mediated H+ secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Volume contraction activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, so angiotensin II stimulates proximal Na+-H+ exchange and aldosterone stimulates alpha-intercalated cell H+ secretion; together with the Starling-force rise in proximal reabsorption, these prevent the excess HCO3- from being excreted, maintaining the alkalosis (contraction alkalosis). Decreased renin release, increased GFR, and distal HCO3- secretion all move in the wrong direction to explain this maintenance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: angiotensin II plus aldosterone, along with Starling-force proximal reabsorption, is the textbook mechanism of \"contraction alkalosis\" and explains why saline, not diuretics, corrects it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 1 — angiotensin II and ECF volume status modifying proximal HCO3- reclamation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Metabolic Alkalosis Maintenance & Respiratory Acid-Base Etiologies","batch":1,"stem":"Which mechanism contributes to the hypokalemia commonly seen in vomiting-induced metabolic alkalosis?","choices":{"A":"Volume contraction-induced secondary hypoaldosteronism substantially decreases principal cell K+ secretion","B":"Volume contraction-induced secondary hyperaldosteronism increases principal cell K+ secretion","C":"Direct loss of K+ in vomitus accounts for the majority of the observed hypokalemia","D":"Metabolic alkalosis shifts K+ out of cells in direct exchange for H+ entering the cells","E":"Increased glomerular filtration of K+ directly increases its urinary excretion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Volume contraction from vomiting activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, and the resulting secondary hyperaldosteronism increases principal cell K+ secretion, which is the most important contributor to hypokalemia; direct gastric K+ loss and the alkalosis-induced cellular H+/K+ shift (H+ leaves cells, K+ enters, lowering plasma K+) contribute more modestly. Hypoaldosteronism moves in the wrong direction, cellular shifts move K+ into rather than out of cells in alkalosis, and glomerular filtration of K+ is not the operative mechanism.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: rank the three contributors to vomiting-induced hypokalemia by importance — secondary hyperaldosteronism first, direct GI loss and cellular shifts second — this ranking is a favorite way to test genuine understanding versus rote memorization.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 2 — aldosterone-stimulated H+ secretion in collecting duct alpha-intercalated cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Metabolic Alkalosis Maintenance & Respiratory Acid-Base Etiologies","batch":1,"stem":"Which cause of metabolic alkalosis is classified as saline-unresponsive, meaning it is not corrected simply by giving intravenous saline?","choices":{"A":"Vomiting, since gastric H+ loss produces a classic volume-contraction alkalosis","B":"Loop diuretic use, since diuretic-induced natriuresis produces a volume-contraction alkalosis","C":"Thiazide diuretic use, since diuretic-induced natriuresis similarly produces a volume-contraction alkalosis","D":"Primary hyperaldosteronism, since it is not caused by extracellular volume contraction","E":"Nasogastric suction, since it removes gastric H+ much like vomiting does"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Primary hyperaldosteronism causes metabolic alkalosis independent of extracellular volume status (volume is often expanded, not contracted), so giving saline does not correct it; this is termed saline-unresponsive alkalosis, along with Cushing syndrome and excessive licorice intake. Vomiting, loop and thiazide diuretic use, and nasogastric suction are all saline-responsive causes driven by volume contraction, which saline directly corrects.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"saline-responsive versus saline-unresponsive\" is a favorite classification question — volume-contraction causes respond to saline, mineralocorticoid-excess causes do not.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 2 — aldosterone excess overdriving alpha-intercalated cell H+ secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Rules, Winters' Formula, & the Delta-Delta Ratio","batch":1,"stem":"A patient has a plasma HCO3- concentration of 14 mEq/L, which is 10 mEq/L below the normal value of 24 mEq/L. Using the renal rule of a 1.2 mm Hg decrease in Pco2 per 1 mEq/L decrease in HCO3-, what Pco2 would indicate simple metabolic acidosis?","choices":{"A":"About 22 mm Hg","B":"About 34 mm Hg","C":"About 40 mm Hg","D":"About 16 mm Hg","E":"About 28 mm Hg"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The predicted decrease in Pco2 is 10 mEq/L x 1.2 mm Hg/mEq/L = 12 mm Hg, so the predicted Pco2 is 40 - 12 = 28 mm Hg. A measured Pco2 close to this value indicates simple metabolic acidosis with appropriate respiratory compensation; the other values do not match this calculation. 40 mm Hg would mean no respiratory compensation and 34 mm Hg too little, while 22 and 16 mm Hg are lower than the rule predicts and would suggest an added respiratory alkalosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: practice this exact calculation until it is automatic — every metabolic acidosis vignette on boards expects you to check the predicted Pco2 before concluding \"simple\" versus \"mixed\" disorder.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 2 — the same delta rules are applied specifically to primary respiratory disturbances","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Rules, Winters' Formula, & the Delta-Delta Ratio","batch":1,"stem":"A patient has a plasma HCO3- concentration of 37 mEq/L, which is 13 mEq/L above the normal value of 24 mEq/L. Using the renal rule of a 0.7 mm Hg increase in Pco2 per 1 mEq/L increase in HCO3-, what Pco2 would indicate simple metabolic alkalosis?","choices":{"A":"About 40 mm Hg","B":"About 49 mm Hg","C":"About 53 mm Hg","D":"About 45 mm Hg","E":"About 58 mm Hg"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The predicted increase in Pco2 is 13 mEq/L x 0.7 mm Hg/mEq/L = 9.1 mm Hg, so the predicted Pco2 is 40 + 9.1 = 49.1 mm Hg, rounding to about 49 mm Hg. A measured Pco2 close to this value indicates simple metabolic alkalosis with appropriate respiratory compensation; the other values do not match this calculation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Costanzo’s worked example uses 0.7 mm Hg per mEq/L for metabolic alkalosis (Table 7.3 lists 0.6), which is smaller than the metabolic acidosis rule of 1.2 mm Hg per mEq/L — mixing these up is a common test-taking error.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 2 — the same delta rules are applied specifically to primary respiratory disturbances","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Rules, Winters' Formula, & the Delta-Delta Ratio","batch":1,"stem":"A patient has a Pco2 of 70 mm Hg, which is 30 mm Hg above the normal value of 40 mm Hg, and an HCO3- of 33 mEq/L, which is 9 mEq/L above normal. Applying the acute rule (0.1 mEq/L HCO3- increase per 1 mm Hg Pco2 increase) and the chronic rule (0.3 mEq/L per 1 mm Hg), which best characterizes this patient?","choices":{"A":"Closer to the chronic respiratory acidosis prediction of a 9 mEq/L increase in HCO3-","B":"Closer to the acute respiratory acidosis prediction of a 3 mEq/L increase in HCO3-","C":"Closer to the chronic respiratory acidosis prediction of a 21 mEq/L increase in HCO3-","D":"Closer to the acute respiratory acidosis prediction of a 30 mEq/L increase in HCO3-","E":"Consistent with neither the acute nor the chronic prediction for respiratory acidosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The acute rule predicts a 30 mm Hg x 0.1 = 3 mEq/L increase in HCO3-, while the chronic rule predicts a 30 mm Hg x 0.3 = 9 mEq/L increase; the patient’s actual 9 mEq/L increase matches the chronic prediction closely (Costanzo uses 0.3; BRS and the course notes give up to 0.4, or about 12 mEq/L, which still points to the chronic pattern), indicating renal compensation has had time to occur. The other values do not correctly apply either rule to this Pco2 change.","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute respiratory acidosis relies only on buffering (small HCO3- rise); chronic respiratory acidosis adds renal compensation over days (larger HCO3- rise) — this contrast is a recurring board theme.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 2 — the same delta rules are applied specifically to primary respiratory disturbances","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Mixed Acid-Base Disturbances & Arterial Blood Gas Synthesis","batch":1,"stem":"What defines a 'mixed' acid-base disorder, as opposed to a 'simple' one?","choices":{"A":"Exactly one primary disturbance is present, with an exaggerated compensatory response","B":"More than one primary acid-base disturbance is present and acting simultaneously","C":"A single disturbance affects both HCO3- and Pco2 through the same underlying mechanism","D":"A disturbance resolves spontaneously, with no compensatory response occurring","E":"A disturbance in which the pH stays within the normal range despite abnormal HCO3- and Pco2"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A mixed acid-base disorder is defined by the presence of more than one primary disturbance occurring simultaneously, such as a metabolic acidosis coexisting with a respiratory alkalosis; a simple disorder has only one primary disturbance, with a single compensatory response. An exaggerated compensation, a single mechanism affecting both variables, spontaneous resolution, and a normal pH despite abnormal values are not what defines the mixed-versus-simple distinction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"mixed\" always means multiple PRIMARY disturbances, not an unusually large or small compensatory response to a single disturbance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 1 — the acid-base map formalizes this simple-versus-mixed distinction visually","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Mixed Acid-Base Disturbances & Arterial Blood Gas Synthesis","batch":1,"stem":"How is the acid-base map used to distinguish a simple from a mixed acid-base disorder?","choices":{"A":"Values falling inside one disorder’s shaded zone indicate that more than one disturbance is definitely present","B":"The acid-base map is used primarily to calculate the plasma anion gap value","C":"Inside a zone points to a lone disturbance; between zones points to more than one","D":"The acid-base map replaces the need for any arterial blood gas measurement at the bedside","E":"The acid-base map applies mainly to metabolic disorders, largely excluding respiratory ones"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Each simple acid-base disorder occupies a defined range of pH, Pco2, and HCO3- values on the acid-base map; a patient’s values falling within one shaded area are consistent with only that disorder being present, while values falling outside any shaded area (e.g., between two areas) indicate a mixed disorder. The map does not calculate the anion gap, does not replace arterial blood gas measurement (it is built from those same values), and applies to both metabolic and respiratory disorders.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the acid-base map is the visual complement to the renal rules calculation — both answer the same question, \"is this simple or mixed?\"","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 1 — the acid-base map formalizes this simple-versus-mixed distinction visually","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Mixed Acid-Base Disturbances & Arterial Blood Gas Synthesis","batch":1,"stem":"What does the term 'isohydric line' refer to on the acid-base map?","choices":{"A":"A line representing the border between compensated and uncompensated states","B":"A line representing a constant anion gap across HCO3- and Pco2 values","C":"A line representing a constant Pco2 across HCO3- concentrations","D":"A line representing a constant HCO3- concentration across Pco2 values","E":"A line representing a constant ratio of HCO3- to dissolved CO2 concentration"},"correct":"E","explanation":"An isohydric line connects all combinations of HCO3- and dissolved CO2 that yield the same ratio, and therefore, by the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation, the same pH; this is why the acid-base map can display pH indirectly through HCO3- and Pco2 axes. It does not represent a constant anion gap, a constant Pco2 alone, a constant HCO3- alone, or a compensation boundary.","boardPrep":"Step 1: recognizing isohydric lines lets you read pH directly off the acid-base map without recalculating Henderson-Hasselbalch for every point.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 1 — the acid-base map formalizes this simple-versus-mixed distinction visually","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — High-Anion-Gap vs. Normal-Anion-Gap Metabolic Acidosis","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes presents with nausea, vomiting, and Kussmaul respirations. Labs: Na+ 132 mEq/L, Cl- 94 mEq/L, HCO3- 5 mEq/L. What is her plasma anion gap, and what acid-base category does her metabolic acidosis fall into?","choices":{"A":"33 mEq/L, consistent with normal-anion-gap metabolic acidosis","B":"33 mEq/L, consistent with high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis","C":"23 mEq/L, consistent with high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis","D":"13 mEq/L, consistent with normal-anion-gap metabolic acidosis","E":"43 mEq/L, consistent with high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Her anion gap is 132 - (94 + 5) = 33 mEq/L, well above the normal range of 8-14 mEq/L (Costanzo; First Aid 2025 gives 8-12 mEq/L), so this is high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis, consistent with diabetic ketoacidosis and accumulation of beta-hydroxybutyrate and acetoacetate as unmeasured anions. The other numeric values do not correctly apply the anion gap formula to her given electrolytes, and 33 mEq/L cannot be classified as normal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this exact DKA electrolyte panel (Na+ 132, Cl- 94, HCO3- 5) reproduces a classic teaching case — memorize the pattern of severe acidosis, dehydration, and a markedly elevated anion gap.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 4 — the RTA diagnostic algorithm covers the normal anion gap contrast to this high anion gap DKA case","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — High-Anion-Gap vs. Normal-Anion-Gap Metabolic Acidosis","batch":2,"stem":"Two patients are evaluated in the clinic for a normal-anion-gap metabolic acidosis. A 36-year-old woman has had 2 weeks of watery diarrhea. A 29-year-old woman with Sjögren syndrome has suspected distal (type 1) renal tubular acidosis. Each has a serum bicarbonate of 15 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L), a sodium of 138 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), a chloride of 114 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L), and a potassium of 3.1 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Which additional finding would best distinguish the two at the bedside?","choices":{"A":"A urine pH that fluctuates unpredictably between acidic and alkaline values suggests the renal tubular acidosis diagnosis","B":"An elevated plasma anion gap suggests the renal tubular acidosis diagnosis","C":"A urine pH persistently above 5.5 despite systemic acidemia suggests the renal tubular acidosis diagnosis","D":"A normal serum potassium concentration reliably distinguishes the renal tubular acidosis diagnosis","E":"Hyperchloremia present in one patient but not the other distinguishes the two conditions"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In distal (type 1) renal tubular acidosis, alpha-intercalated cell H+-ATPase failure prevents urine pH from falling below about 5.5 even during systemic acidemia, whereas a patient with diarrhea and intact renal function usually acidifies urine below 5.5. Both conditions produce a normal anion gap and hyperchloremia, and serum potassium is not a reliable distinguishing feature since type 1 RTA, like diarrhea, commonly causes hypokalemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"urine pH will not fall below 5.5 despite systemic acidemia\" is the single most quoted distinguishing fact for distal RTA on board exams.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 4 — differentiating RTA subtypes builds on this normal-anion-gap framework","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — High-Anion-Gap vs. Normal-Anion-Gap Metabolic Acidosis","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman with long-standing diabetic nephropathy and advanced chronic kidney disease comes for a routine visit. Blood pressure is 148/86 mm Hg. Serum creatinine is 4.6 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL), potassium is 5.1 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), and bicarbonate is 17 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). She has developed a metabolic acidosis. Which statement about her anion gap status is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Her anion gap may be elevated, normal, or mixed, depending on retained organic anions versus chloride retention","B":"Her anion gap is necessarily elevated, since chronic kidney disease is a classic high-anion-gap cause","C":"Her anion gap is normal, since chronic kidney disease causes a hyperchloremic acidosis","D":"Her anion gap is unreliable in chronic kidney disease, since retained creatinine distorts chloride assays","E":"Her anion gap will be elevated early in disease and become normal after dialysis begins"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Chronic kidney disease can present with either an increased anion gap (from retained unmeasured organic anions as filtration falls) or a normal anion gap (from impaired NH4+ excretion causing hyperchloremic acidosis), and many patients show a mixed picture; the anion gap is not necessarily fixed to one category. Creatinine does not interfere with the anion gap calculation, and gap status is not reliably tied to dialysis initiation in the way described.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: chronic kidney disease is a useful reminder that a single etiology can produce either anion-gap pattern — do not assume every listed \"cause\" maps to only one category.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 3 — nephron loss cuts both titratable acid and NH4+ excretion in chronic kidney disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Metabolic Alkalosis Maintenance & Respiratory Acid-Base Etiologies","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old man with a pyloric ulcer and several days of vomiting has metabolic alkalosis with orthostatic hypotension. His vomiting has now stopped, but his metabolic alkalosis persists. What is the most effective treatment to correct his acid-base disturbance?","choices":{"A":"Intravenous isotonic saline given specifically to directly neutralize the excess plasma HCO3-","B":"Acetazolamide to inhibit proximal tubule HCO3- reabsorption without restoring volume","C":"Intravenous isotonic saline to restore volume and permit renal HCO3- excretion","D":"Intravenous potassium chloride given without any accompanying volume repletion","E":"Oral ammonium chloride to directly titrate the excess plasma HCO3-"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Because his alkalosis is being maintained by extracellular volume contraction rather than ongoing gastric H+ loss, restoring volume with isotonic saline removes the stimulus for increased proximal and distal HCO3- reabsorption, allowing the kidneys to finally excrete the excess HCO3- and correct the alkalosis. Saline does not chemically neutralize HCO3-, acetazolamide is not the standard approach and does not address the underlying volume deficit, potassium alone does not correct the volume contraction driving the maintenance, and ammonium chloride is not standard first-line treatment here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"saline-responsive\" alkalosis literally means giving saline is the treatment — remember this pairs directly with the volume-contraction maintenance mechanism.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 1 — ECF volume status and the proximal HCO3- reclamation rate","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Metabolic Alkalosis Maintenance & Respiratory Acid-Base Etiologies","batch":2,"stem":"In a research study, a healthy 25-year-old man with normal blood pressure and no volume depletion ingests a large dose of sodium bicarbonate. His arterial HCO3- rises to 32 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L) and his pH to 7.49 (reference range: 7.35–7.45), but both return to normal within a day as his urine becomes alkaline. Besides excretion of filtered HCO3- in excess of reabsorptive capacity, which collecting duct process helps correct his alkalosis?","choices":{"A":"HCO3- secretion by beta-intercalated cells into the urine","B":"H+ secretion by alpha-intercalated cells as titratable acid","C":"Angiotensin II-stimulated Na+-H+ exchange in the collecting duct","D":"Aldosterone-driven H+-ATPase activity in intercalated cells","E":"NH4+ trapping driven by alpha-intercalated cell H+ secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"When blood HCO3- is elevated, beta-intercalated cells of the collecting ducts secrete HCO3- into the urine, reversing the membrane arrangement of the Cl--HCO3- exchanger and H+-ATPase so that HCO3- enters the lumen and H+ is returned to the blood; together with excretion of filtered HCO3- beyond the reabsorptive capacity, this corrects the alkalosis when nothing maintains it. H+ secretion as titratable acid and NH4+ by alpha-intercalated cells generates new HCO3-, which would worsen rather than correct alkalosis. Angiotensin II stimulates Na+-H+ exchange in the proximal tubule, not the collecting duct, and with aldosterone-driven H+ secretion it is a maintenance factor of volume contraction, which this man does not have.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Metabolic alkalosis has a generating cause (loss of H+, gain of HCO3-, volume contraction) and, when it persists, a maintaining one (volume contraction: angiotensin II on proximal Na+-H+ exchange, aldosterone on distal H+ secretion); without maintenance the kidney excretes the excess HCO3-. COMLEX: An alkalosis that persists implies a maintenance factor, most often volume contraction, which isotonic saline corrects.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 2 — alpha-intercalated cells normally secrete H+ and add new HCO3- to the blood","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Metabolic Alkalosis Maintenance & Respiratory Acid-Base Etiologies","batch":2,"stem":"A 23-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after being found unresponsive with a syringe beside him. He responds only to painful stimuli, and his pupils are pinpoint. Respirations are 5/min and shallow, blood pressure is 104/66 mm Hg, and oxygen saturation is 82% on room air. Serum glucose is 92 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL), and lactate is 1.4 mmol/L (reference range: 0.5–2.2 mmol/L). Which acid-base and blood gas pattern would be expected as the direct consequence of his acute opioid overdose?","choices":{"A":"Respiratory alkalosis, from CO2 loss due to inhibition of the medullary respiratory center","B":"Respiratory acidosis, from CO2 retention due to peripheral chemoreceptor overstimulation","C":"Respiratory alkalosis, from CO2 loss due to peripheral chemoreceptor overstimulation","D":"Metabolic acidosis, from opioid-induced lactic acid accumulation","E":"Respiratory acidosis, from CO2 retention due to inhibition of the medullary respiratory center"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Opioids directly inhibit the medullary respiratory center, causing hypoventilation, CO2 retention, and respiratory acidosis (decreased pH, increased Pco2). Hypoventilation causes CO2 retention rather than loss, the mechanism is central inhibition rather than peripheral chemoreceptor overstimulation, and this scenario is a primary respiratory disturbance rather than a metabolic one from lactic acid accumulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: opioids, barbiturates, and anesthetics are the classic causes of respiratory acidosis from central respiratory center inhibition — a frequently tested pharmacology-physiology link.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 3 — acute versus chronic respiratory acidosis and the delayed renal response","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Rules, Winters' Formula, & the Delta-Delta Ratio","batch":2,"stem":"A 21-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes mellitus is brought to the emergency department because of vomiting and abdominal pain. She is breathing deeply and rapidly. Blood pressure is 100/62 mm Hg. Serum glucose is 520 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL), and plasma HCO3- concentration is 10 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Using Winters' formula (predicted Pco2 = 1.5 x [HCO3-] + 8, plus or minus 2), what is the predicted Pco2 range for appropriate respiratory compensation in this patient?","choices":{"A":"About 21 to 25 mm Hg","B":"About 16 to 20 mm Hg","C":"About 26 to 30 mm Hg","D":"About 33 to 37 mm Hg","E":"About 8 to 12 mm Hg"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Winters' formula gives predicted Pco2 = (1.5 x 10) + 8 = 23 mm Hg, plus or minus 2, for a range of about 21 to 25 mm Hg; a measured Pco2 within this range indicates appropriate respiratory compensation for a simple metabolic acidosis. The other ranges do not result from correctly applying this formula to an HCO3- of 10 mEq/L.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Winters' formula and the renal-rule slope (1.2 mm Hg per mEq/L) are two commonly taught approximations for the same compensation — either gets you to a similar predicted Pco2 on boards.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 2 — analogous renal compensation rules predict HCO3- shifts in primary respiratory disturbances","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Rules, Winters' Formula, & the Delta-Delta Ratio","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with alcohol use disorder is admitted because of 3 days of persistent vomiting and poor oral intake. Blood pressure is 102/64 mm Hg, and pulse is 112/min. Serum sodium is 140 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), chloride is 92 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L), and plasma HCO3- is 16 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L), giving an anion gap of 32 mEq/L (reference range: 8–16 mEq/L). Serum ketones are positive. Compared with normal values, his plasma HCO3- has fallen by about 8 mEq per liter, while his anion gap has risen by about 20 mEq per liter. What does this delta-delta analysis suggest?","choices":{"A":"A second disorder is present that is independently decreasing HCO3-, such as a normal-anion-gap metabolic acidosis","B":"A second disorder is present that is independently increasing HCO3-, such as a metabolic alkalosis","C":"No second disorder is present, since the decrease in HCO3- exactly mirrors the increase in anion gap","D":"A second disorder is present affecting the respiratory compensation, not the metabolic component","E":"The primary disorder should be reclassified as a normal-anion-gap metabolic acidosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because the HCO3- decreased by less (8 mEq/L) than the anion gap increased (20 mEq/L), an additional process is raising HCO3- beyond what the high-anion-gap acidosis alone would produce, such as a coexisting metabolic alkalosis. A fall in HCO3- roughly equal to the rise in anion gap would indicate no second disorder, and a fall in HCO3- greater than the rise in anion gap would instead suggest a coexisting normal-anion-gap metabolic acidosis lowering HCO3- further; the respiratory compensation and the underlying anion-gap classification are not what this comparison addresses.","boardPrep":"Step 1: delta-delta ratio = ΔAG/ΔHCO3- (ΔAG = AG − 12; ΔHCO3- = 24 − HCO3-). A ratio above 2 means HCO3- fell much less than the gap rose, pointing to a coexisting metabolic alkalosis; a ratio below 1 points to an added normal-anion-gap acidosis; ratios from 1 to 2 fit an uncomplicated high-anion-gap acidosis. COMLEX: vomiting before ketoacidosis is the classic setup for the alkalosis pattern.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — the same stepwise approach separates simple from mixed disorders on the acid-base map","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Rules, Winters' Formula, & the Delta-Delta Ratio","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with long-standing, stable COPD has a Pco2 of 78 mm Hg, predicting a chronic HCO3- increase of about 11 mEq/L, but his actual HCO3- is only 26 mEq/L, an increase of just 2 mEq/L from normal. What does this discrepancy suggest?","choices":{"A":"A concurrent metabolic alkalosis is present, since the HCO3- rise is much less than predicted for chronic compensation","B":"A concurrent metabolic acidosis is present, since the HCO3- rise is much greater than predicted for chronic compensation","C":"Simple chronic respiratory acidosis is present, since some discrepancy from the predicted value is expected normally","D":"A concurrent metabolic acidosis is present, since the HCO3- rise is much less than predicted for chronic compensation","E":"A concurrent respiratory alkalosis is present, since the HCO3- rise is much less than predicted for chronic compensation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The actual HCO3- rise (2 mEq/L) is far below the value predicted for chronic renal compensation (about 11 mEq/L), meaning an additional process is independently lowering HCO3-, most consistent with a concurrent metabolic acidosis such as lactic acidosis from poor tissue oxygenation. A concurrent metabolic alkalosis would raise, not lower, the observed HCO3-; the discrepancy is far too large to be normal variation around simple chronic respiratory acidosis; and a concurrent respiratory alkalosis would not directly explain an HCO3- lower than predicted for renal compensation of this respiratory acidosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a smaller-than-predicted HCO3- rise in chronic respiratory acidosis should always prompt a search for hidden metabolic acidosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 2 — the same delta rules are applied specifically to primary respiratory disturbances","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Mixed Acid-Base Disturbances & Arterial Blood Gas Synthesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department 6 hours after intentionally ingesting a large number of aspirin tablets. She reports ringing in her ears and nausea. Respirations are 30/min and deep, pulse is 112/min, and temperature is 38.1°C (100.6°F). Serum salicylate concentration is 68 mg/dL (reference range: undetectable). Which combination of primary acid-base disturbances would be expected in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Primary respiratory acidosis plus primary high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis, a double acid-base disorder","B":"Primary respiratory alkalosis plus primary normal-anion-gap metabolic acidosis, a double acid-base disorder","C":"Just primary respiratory alkalosis, since salicylate's direct effect is limited to the respiratory center","D":"Just primary high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis, since the respiratory center is not directly affected","E":"Primary respiratory alkalosis plus primary high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis, a double acid-base disorder"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Salicylate directly stimulates the medullary respiratory center, producing a primary respiratory alkalosis, while uncoupling of oxidative phosphorylation, with accumulating lactic acid, ketoacids and salicylate itself, independently produces a primary high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis; together these constitute a double acid-base disorder rather than a single one. The disturbance is respiratory alkalosis rather than acidosis, the accompanying metabolic disorder has an increased rather than normal anion gap, and salicylate affects both the respiratory center and the metabolic (anion gap) component simultaneously rather than only one.","boardPrep":"Step 1: salicylate overdose is the single most commonly tested example of a double acid-base disorder — respiratory alkalosis plus high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis, with the respiratory alkalosis appearing first and the metabolic acidosis added as poisoning progresses.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — the acid-base map formalizes this simple-versus-mixed distinction visually","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Mixed Acid-Base Disturbances & Arterial Blood Gas Synthesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old man has an in-hospital cardiac arrest. During prolonged resuscitation, ventilation through a poorly fitting mask is inadequate, and he receives several ampules of sodium bicarbonate. After return of spontaneous circulation, his serum lactate is 11 mmol/L (reference range: 0.5–2.2 mmol/L). Arterial blood gas analysis shows a pH of 7.18 (reference range: 7.35–7.45), Pco2 of 50 mm Hg (reference range: 35–45 mm Hg), and HCO3- of 18 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L); serum sodium is 142 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L) and chloride is 96 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L). How many primary acid-base disturbances does this represent?","choices":{"A":"One, since each of these processes ultimately reflects the same final common pathway of illness","B":"Two, since the metabolic alkalosis and lactic acidosis cancel each other into one net metabolic state","C":"Two, since respiratory acidosis and lactic acidosis are considered functionally the same disturbance","D":"Three, since lactic acidosis, respiratory acidosis, and metabolic alkalosis each independently alter it","E":"Four, since lactic acidosis, respiratory acidosis, metabolic alkalosis, and hypoxia are each separate"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Lactic acidosis (a metabolic acidosis from tissue hypoxia), respiratory acidosis (from inadequate ventilation), and iatrogenic metabolic alkalosis (from bicarbonate administration) each independently alter blood HCO3- or Pco2 through separate mechanisms, together constituting a triple acid-base disorder. These are not a single shared pathway, the alkalosis does not simply cancel the acidosis (they can coexist as distinct primary disturbances), respiratory and lactic acidosis are mechanistically distinct (Pco2-driven versus HCO3--driven), and tissue hypoxia itself is the trigger for the lactic acidosis rather than a separate primary acid-base disturbance.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: cardiorespiratory arrest with bicarbonate resuscitation is a classic setup for a triple acid-base disorder — count each independent process rather than assuming they cancel out.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — the acid-base map formalizes this simple-versus-mixed distinction visually","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Mixed Acid-Base Disturbances & Arterial Blood Gas Synthesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with long-standing COPD has had several days of vomiting. Arterial blood gas shows pH 7.45, Pco2 60 mm Hg, and HCO3- 40 mEq/L. Using the chronic respiratory acidosis rule of 0.3 mEq/L HCO3- per mm Hg Pco2, which interpretation best explains this profile?","choices":{"A":"A simple chronic respiratory acidosis with the expected renal HCO3- retention","B":"Chronic respiratory acidosis with a superimposed primary metabolic alkalosis","C":"Chronic respiratory acidosis with a superimposed lactic acidosis","D":"Chronic respiratory alkalosis with a superimposed primary metabolic acidosis","E":"Chronic respiratory acidosis with a superimposed primary respiratory alkalosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The chronic respiratory acidosis rule predicts HCO3- = 24 + 0.3 x (60 - 40) = 30 mEq/L (Costanzo; with the 0.4 used by BRS and the course notes, about 32 mEq/L). The measured HCO3- of 40 mEq/L is well above that, so a second process is adding bicarbonate: a primary metabolic alkalosis from vomiting. The near-normal pH reflects the two opposing disturbances together (6.1 + log 40/1.8 = 7.45). A simple chronic respiratory acidosis would stop near 30, a metabolic acidosis would push HCO3- below the predicted value, and two respiratory disorders cannot coexist.","boardPrep":"Step 1: compare the measured HCO3- with the value the compensation rule predicts; a value above the prediction means an added metabolic alkalosis, and a value below it means an added metabolic acidosis. COMLEX: a near-normal pH with grossly abnormal Pco2 and HCO3- should prompt a search for a mixed disorder.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — the acid-base map separates simple from mixed disorders by where the values fall relative to the compensation bands","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex2t_sdl24_q1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — High-Anion-Gap vs. Normal-Anion-Gap Metabolic Acidosis","batch":3,"stem":"A 29-year-old man has had 5 days of watery diarrhea. His serum Na+ is 138 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), Cl- is 115 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L), HCO3- is 14 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L), arterial pH is 7.29 (reference range: 7.35–7.45), and PaCO2 is 30 mm Hg (reference range: 33–45 mm Hg); his urine pH is 5.0. Which process best explains his acid-base disorder?","choices":{"A":"Accumulation of unmeasured organic anions in plasma","B":"Bicarbonate loss from the gut with chloride retention","C":"Failure of distal proton secretion by the collecting duct","D":"Bicarbonate wasting from a proximal tubular defect","E":"Retention of carbon dioxide from alveolar hypoventilation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The anion gap is 138 - (115 + 14) = 9 mEq/L, so this is a normal-anion-gap (hyperchloremic) metabolic acidosis. Diarrheal fluid is rich in bicarbonate, and the kidney retains chloride to preserve electroneutrality, so the fall in bicarbonate is matched by a rise in chloride. Accumulation of organic anions would raise the anion gap. Distal RTA is unlikely because the urine pH of 5.0 shows intact distal acidification. Proximal RTA is a renal cause with no link to diarrhea. Carbon dioxide retention would cause respiratory acidosis with a high bicarbonate. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 24, high-anion-gap vs. normal-anion-gap metabolic acidosis); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 7 (anion gap and hyperchloremic acidosis from diarrhea).","boardPrep":"Step 1: normal-anion-gap acidosis causes include diarrhea, RTA, acetazolamide, and saline infusion. COMLEX: a urine pH below 5.5 with high urinary ammonium favors diarrhea over distal RTA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 2 — high urinary ammonium in diarrhea reflects intact diffusion trapping","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex2t_sdl24_q2","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Renal Rules, Winters' Formula, & the Delta-Delta Ratio","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with septic shock has an arterial pH of 7.17 (reference range: 7.35–7.45), bicarbonate of 12 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L), and PaCO2 of 34 mm Hg (reference range: 33–45 mm Hg). Her sodium is 140 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L) and chloride is 100 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L). Which interpretation best fits her blood gas?","choices":{"A":"A concurrent respiratory alkalosis","B":"A concurrent metabolic alkalosis","C":"A concurrent normal gap acidosis","D":"A concurrent respiratory acidosis","E":"An adequate respiratory compensation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The anion gap is 140 - (100 + 12) = 28 mEq/L, a high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis such as lactic acidosis. Winters formula predicts PaCO2 = 1.5 x 12 + 8 = 26 ± 2 mm Hg. Her measured PaCO2 of 34 mm Hg is higher than expected, so ventilation is inadequate and a respiratory acidosis coexists. A respiratory alkalosis would push PaCO2 below 24 mm Hg. The delta ratio is (28 - 12)/(24 - 12), about 1.3, which falls in the range of a pure high-anion-gap acidosis, so neither a metabolic alkalosis (ratio above 2) nor a normal-gap acidosis (ratio below 1) is suggested. Compensation is not adequate because PaCO2 exceeds the predicted range. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 24, renal rules, Winters formula, and the delta-delta ratio); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 7 (respiratory compensation for metabolic acidosis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Winters formula, PaCO2 = 1.5 x bicarbonate + 8 ± 2, identifies a second respiratory disorder in metabolic acidosis. COMLEX: a \"normal\" PaCO2 in severe metabolic acidosis signals impending respiratory failure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 3 — Henderson-Hasselbalch links the inadequate PaCO2 fall to the low pH","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex2t_sdl24_q3","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Mixed Acid-Base Disturbances & Arterial Blood Gas Synthesis","batch":3,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman is admitted after an aspirin overdose preceded by 2 days of vomiting. Arterial pH is 7.54 (reference range: 7.35–7.45), PaCO2 is 22 mm Hg (reference range: 33–45 mm Hg), bicarbonate is 18 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L), sodium is 140 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), and chloride is 92 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L). Which interpretation does the delta ratio support?","choices":{"A":"A hidden metabolic alkalosis","B":"A hidden normal gap acidosis","C":"A hidden respiratory acidosis","D":"A pure high anion gap acidosis","E":"A hidden lactic acidosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The anion gap is 140 - (92 + 18) = 30 mEq/L, a rise of about 18 above a normal of 12, while bicarbonate has fallen by only 6 (from 24 to 18). The delta ratio of 18/6 = 3.0 is above 2 (and stays above 2, at about 2.3, even if a normal gap of 16 is used) and means bicarbonate is higher than the gap acidosis alone would leave it, so the ratio supports a hidden metabolic alkalosis from vomiting coexisting with the gap acidosis. The low PaCO2 reflects the primary respiratory alkalosis of salicylate stimulation of the respiratory center, which is read from the gas rather than the delta ratio. A normal-gap acidosis would give a ratio below 1. Respiratory acidosis is excluded by the low PaCO2. A pure high-anion-gap acidosis is the interpretation for a ratio between 1 and 2, not one above 2. A lactic acidosis would add to the gap and is part of the high-gap process rather than a hidden disorder. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 24, mixed acid-base disturbances and ABG synthesis); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 7 (anion gap and mixed disorders).","boardPrep":"Step 1: salicylate toxicity classically causes respiratory alkalosis plus high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis; vomiting adds a metabolic alkalosis. COMLEX: treat salicylate toxicity with bicarbonate to alkalinize the urine while monitoring arterial pH, and consider dialysis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 2 — volume and chloride depletion from vomiting help maintain the hidden alkalosis","isHighYield":true}]};
